Fig. \ref{vs P_FA} presents a comparative analysis of tracking steps for different beamforming strategies under varying CFAR constraints, denoted by values of $P_\mathrm{FA}$. The four subfigures correspond to $P_\mathrm{FA}=10^{-1},10^{-2},10^{-3},$ and $10^{-4}$, illustrating the performance of online DRL, offline DRL, DT-DRL with CQL, and DT-DRL without CQL. It can be observed that as $P_\mathrm{FA}$ decreases, tracking steps tend to increase across all methods, indicating a direct impact of CFAR constraints on beamforming performance and a trade-off between detection reliability and tracking efficiency. Stricter false alarm control reduces detection sensitivity, necessitating more steps for beam alignment. Furthermore, DT-DRL with CQL maintains relatively low tracking steps compared to DT-DRL without CQL, confirming that incorporating CQL enhances the efficiency of DT-assisted DRL models. Offline DRL requires more tracking steps than DT-DRL with CQL, underscoring the benefits of the DT module. Finally, DT-DRL with CQL proves to be the most effective approach under all CFAR constraints, reinforcing the advantages of our proposed method for adaptive beamforming.
