\section{Related Work}

Two important early studies from an acoustic perspective examined how sustain pedal affects sound. The first one \cite{lehtonen_analysis_2007} found that pressing the pedal increases decay times in mid-range frequencies and alters the tone through sympathetic resonance. The further study \cite{lehtonen_analysis_2009} investigated part-pedaling and identified three phases: initial free vibration, damper-string interaction, and final free vibration. This provides scientific evidence that varying the pedal depth allows for gradual and continuous control over the sound rather than a simple on/off function.

% \hanwen{remove some low-level details}
In MIR-related field, Liang et al. implemented an optical sensor \cite{liang_piano_2017} and explored the relationship between pedaling techniques and physical gestures. Their subsequent work includes classifying isolated notes with different sustain pedal timings \cite{liang_detection_2017}, classifying short excerpts from a few recordings with four levels of pedal depth \cite{liang_measurement_2018}, and detecting legato-pedal onsets by measuring sympathetic resonance \cite{liang_piano_2018}. These studies have shown potential but have not yet fully solved the pedal depth estimation problem in real-world scenarios.
% Liang et al. \cite{liang_piano_2017} explored the relationship between pedaling techniques and physical gestures using a near-field optical reflectance sensor. Their subsequent studies \cite{liang_detection_2017, liang_measurement_2018} further investigated this relationship. In \cite{liang_detection_2017}, they used decision trees and SVM to classify audio of isolated notes with different pedal timings (normal, anticipatory, and legato), while \cite{liang_measurement_2018} applied HMM and SVM to classify four levels of sustain pedal depth in a small Chopin recording dataset. Expanding on this work, they later introduced a method for identifying legato-pedal onsets by measuring sympathetic resonance via unstruck string energy in short excerpts from a few selected recordings \cite{liang_piano_2018}. Even on this small dataset, the model's performance varied significantly across composers, suggesting the influence of musical style and articulation on sustain pedal usage.
