\paragraph{Budget management} 
% \label{sssec:budget}




% Verifying every design at every scale is computationally prohibitive. Inspired by scaling laws \cite{kaplan2020scaling,tay2022scaling} – which suggest performance correlates across scales – and practical LM development workflows \cite{hu2024minicpm}, we implement a \textbf{Ladder of Scales} strategy (Fig. \ref{fig:los}). We define a total verification budget $B_m = \{\beta_0, ..., \beta_{N_S}\}$ across scales $i=0...N_S$ (e.g., 14M to 350M parameters). This budget is structured pyramidally, allocating significantly more trials $\beta_i$ to smaller scales: $\beta_{i+1} \approx sr_i \cdot \beta_i$, where $sr_i < 1$ is the target selection ratio between scales.
% To ensure fairness and prevent early depletion of higher-scale budgets, these budgets are released gradually over time. A dynamic allocatable budget $B_a = \{\alpha_0, ..., \alpha_{N_S}\}$ tracks currently available verification slots. $B_a$ is initialized with a small budget at the lowest scale ($\alpha_0=1, \alpha_{i>0}=0$). Budgets are replenished at the lowest scale as they are used, and higher-scale budgets $\alpha_{i+1}$ are released automatically whenever the number of used lower-scale budgets $\beta_i$ exceeds the target ratio $1/sr_i$. The experimenter in a verifier node selects a design (via §4.3.2) and attempts to verify it at the lowest scale $i$ for which it hasn't been verified and for which budget $\alpha_i > 0$ is available. If no match exists, it re-selects a compatible design. This strategy prioritizes cheaper, lower-scale verification while progressively promoting promising candidates to more expensive, higher scales.



Verifying every design at every scale is prohibitively expensive. Inspired by scaling laws \citep{kaplan2020scaling,tay2022scaling} – which suggest that performance correlates across scales – and the methodology commonly employed for small LMs \citep{hu2024minicpm}, we implement the \textbf{Ladder of Scales} strategy shown in Fig. \ref{fig:los} \update{where several experiments or trials are performed at small parameter/token sizes, then scaled with decreasingly fewer trials.} Formally, a total verification budget $B_m = \{\beta_0, ..., \beta_{N_S}\}$ across $N_S+1$ scales (e.g., 14M-350M parameters) is structured pyramidally: more trials $\beta_i$ at smaller scales, with $\beta_{i+1} \approx sr_i \cdot \beta_i$ ($sr_i < 1$ is the target inter-scale selection ratio).  Higher-scale budgets are released gradually to ensure fairness and to prevent early depletion.
A dynamic \textit{allocatable budget} $B_a = \{\alpha_0, ..., \alpha_{N_S}\}$ is initialized with $\alpha_0=1$ and $\alpha_{i>0}=0$.
Budgets are replenished at the lowest scale upon use, and higher-scale budgets $\alpha_{i+1}$ are released when used lower-scale budgets $\beta_i$ exceed $1/sr_i$.
The verifier node selects designs per the above strategy, verifying at the lowest unverified scale $i$ with an available budget $\alpha_i > 0$ \update{(see Alg.~\ref{alg:ladder_of_scale})}. 

