RK2 demonstrates stability only for very small step sizes, specifically for values of $h \leq 0.001$. However, for $h \geq 0.05$, the relative error grows uncontrollably. This behavior confirms a strict stability limit around $h = 0.001$, beyond which the method fails to provide reliable results. On the other hand, ExpRK2 exhibits excellent stability across all tested step sizes. Even for larger ones, the method maintains a reasonably small relative error. Rb2 also demonstrates stable behavior, albeit within a more restricted range. For step sizes up to $0.01$, the method produces accurate results. However, for $h = 0.05$, the relative error starts increasing significantly, and for $h = 0.1$ it becomes quite large ($1.53 \times 10^{-1}$). The superior accuracy makes ExpRK2 a strong candidate for solving stiff problems. 

