\section{Implications of Constrained Architectural Choices in Restricting Message Content}

\subsection{Input and Message Inference with KL Penalties}
\label{app:kl_sparsity}

KL penalties with standard Gaussian priors in variational autoencoders are known to reduce latent space dimensionality by pruning unnecessary dimensions \cite{dai2018connections, miller2024cognitive}. Consequently, with sufficiently high KL penalty coefficients $(\klweight{u}$, $\klweight{m})$, \mrlfads is incentivized to use only the communication channels essential for data reconstruction. This effect is evident when comparing the most active channel (i.e., the one with the highest input or message norm across trials and time) to the least active ones (\Cref{fig:kl_sparsity}a, b). Examining input and message norms across all channels further confirms that some channels are effectively silenced (\Cref{fig:kl_sparsity}c).

