\section{Introduction.} 
\label{sec:intro}

Let $G$ be a connected, simply-connected, simple (linear) algebraic group over $\BC$, 
with $T$ a maximal torus, $B$ a Borel subgroup containing $T$. 
We take an arbitrary parabolic subgroup $P$ of $G$ containing $B$, 
and let $Y := G/P$ be the corresponding (partial) flag manifold. 
Given an effective degree $d \in H_2(Y; \mathbb{Z})$, let $\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y, d)$ denote the Kontsevich moduli space parametrizing all $m$-point, genus $0$, 
  degree $d$ stable maps to $Y$. Given subvarieties $\Omega_1, \dots, \Omega_m \subset Y$ in general position, the cohomological Gromov-Witten invariant $\angles{[\Omega_1], \dots, [\Omega_m]}_d^Y$ counts the number of parametrized curves $\mathbb{P}^1 \to Y$ of degree $d$ with $m$ marked points in the domain (up to projective transformation) such that the $k$-th marked point is sent into $\Omega_k$ for $1 \leq k \leq m$, assuming that only finitely many such ones exist. By transversality, one can show that if $[\Omega_m] = D$ is a divisor class on $Y$, then 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gwdiv}
\angles{[\Omega_1],\dots, [\Omega_{m-1}],D}_d^Y = \left(\int_d D\right) \cdot \angles{[\Omega_1],\dots, [\Omega_{m-1}]}_d^Y 
\end{equation} 
(see, for instance, \cite[Section 7]{FP}). This useful identity is called the divisor axiom\footnote{Equation \eqref{eq:gwdiv} holds for more general target spaces, but defining Gromov-Witten invariants in general requires the use of virtual fundamental classes, which we do not wish to introduce in this paper. }. 
More generally, the $K$-theoretic 
Gromov-Witten invariant $\angles{[\cO_{\Omega_1}], \dots, [\cO_{\Omega_m}]}^{Y}_{d}$
is defined as the sheaf Euler characteristic $\chi_{\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y, d)}([\cO_{\GW_d}])$ of the
Gromov-Witten subvariety $\GW_d \subset \ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y,d)$ of stable maps that
send the $k$-th marked point into $\Omega_k$ for $1 \leq k \leq m$.
 However, no analog of the divisor axiom \eqref{eq:gwdiv} is known in the general setting of $K$-theory. 

Let $X := G/B$ denote the (full) flag manifold. 
For each element $w$ of the Weyl group $W$ of $G$, 
let $X_{w} := \ol{BwB/B} \subset X$ be the corresponding Schubert variety, 
and $X^{w} := \ol{B^{-} wB/B} \subset X$ the opposite Schubert variety, 
where $B^{-}$ is the Borel subgroup containing $T$ that is opposite to $B$. 

We identify the 2nd homology group $H_{2}(X; \BZ)$ of $X$ 
with the coroot lattice $Q^{\vee} = \sum_{j \in I} \BZ \alpha_j^{\vee}$ of $G$, 
where $\alpha_j^{\vee}$, $j \in I$, are the simple coroots, 
with $\alpha_j^{\vee}$ identified with the homology class $[X_{s_j}] \in H_2(X; \BZ)$ 
associated to the simple reflection $s_j \in W$; 
we set $Q^{\vee,+} := \sum_{j \in I} \BZ_{\geq 0} \alpha_j^{\vee}$, 
the set of effective degrees. 
Let $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$ and $m \geq 1$. 
Let $\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(X, d)$ denote 
the Kontsevich moduli space parametrizing all $m$-point, genus $0$, 
degree $d$ stable maps to $X$ (see \cite{FP}, \cite{Tho}), 
which is equipped with ($T$-equivariant) evaluation maps 
$\ev_k : \ol{\CM}_{0,m}(X,d) \to X$ for $1 \leq k \leq m$, 
where $\ev_k$ sends a stable map to its image of the $k$-th marked point in its domain. 

Let $K_{T}(X)$ denote the $T$-equivariant $K$-theory ring of the flag manifold $X$, 
equipped with the product $\cdot$ coming from the tensor product; 
it has the (opposite) Schubert classes $\CO^{w} := [\CO_{X^w}]$, $w \in W$, 
as a basis over the representation ring $R(T)$ of $T$, 
which is identified with the group algebra $\BZ[\Lambda]$ of 
the weight lattice $\Lambda = \sum_{j \in I} \BZ \vpi_{j}$ of $G$, 
where $\vpi_{j}$, $j \in I$, are the fundamental weights; 
$K_T(X)$ also has an $R(T)$-basis consisting of 
the Schubert classes $\CO_w := [\CO_{X_w}]$, $w \in W$. 
For $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$, $m \geq 1$, and classes $\gamma_k \in K_{T}(X)$, $1 \leq k \leq m$, 
the $m$-point ($T$-equivariant) $K$-theoretic Gromov-Witten (KGW) invariant is 
defined to be
%
\begin{equation*}
\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \ldots , \gamma_m \rangle_d := 
\chi^{T}_{\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(X,d)}(\ev_1^*(\gamma_1) \cdot \cdots \cdot \ev_m^*(\gamma_m)) 
\in K_{T}( \mathrm{pt} ) = R(T),
\end{equation*}
%
where $\chi^{T}_{\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(X,d)}$ is 
the ($T$-equivariant) pushforward along 
the structure morphism $\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(X,d) \rightarrow \{ \mathrm{pt} \}$; 
the KGW invariant $\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \ldots , \gamma_m \rangle_d$ is 
$R(T)$-linear in each argument $\gamma_j$. 
Note that if we take $\gamma_3 = 1 \in K_T(X)$, with $m = 3$, 
then, by the (simplest case of the) string equation, 
the 2-point KGW invariant $\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_d$ is 
identical to the 3-point KGW invariant $\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, 1 \rangle_d$ 
for $\gamma_1, \gamma_2 \in K_T(X)$.
Also, for $u, v \in W$ and $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$, 
the 2-point KGW invariant $\twp{u}{v}_d$ is 
identical to the ($T$-equivariant) Euler characteristic of 
the structure sheaf of the Gromov-Witten variety 
$\ev_1^{-1}(X^u) \cap \ev_2^{-1}(X_v) \subset \ol{\CM}_{0,3}(X,d)$ of 
stable maps with one marked point sent into $X^u$ and another into $X_v$. 

The purpose of this paper is to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} below 
(which is the combination of Theorem~\ref{thm:qk1} and Corollary~\ref{cor:qk2}), 
conjectured by Buch-Mihalcea (see \cite[Section~5.1.2, Conjecture~1]{Mi}); 
note that this theorem can be thought of as a replacement 
for the ``divisor axiom'' in the ($T$-equivariant) quantum $K$-theory of 
$X = G/B$, and hence we call it the quantum $K$-divisor axiom. 
For an effective degree $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$ and $j \in I$, 
we set $d_j := \pair{\vpi_j}{d} \in \BZ_{\geq 0}$, 
where $\pair{\cdot\,}{\cdot}$ is the canonical pairing 
between $\Ft$ and its dual $\Ft^*$, 
with $\Ft := \Lie(T)$; 
note that $d = \sum_{j \in I} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee}$. 
Also, let $\theta \in \Delta^+$ be the highest root of 
the root system $\Delta \subset \Ft^*$ of $G$, 
with $\Delta^+$ the set of positive roots; 
we denote by $\theta^{\vee} \in \Ft$ the coroot of $\theta$. 
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%% thm:qkdivisor %%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
\begin{ithm}\label{thm:qkdivisor} 
Let $i \in I$. 
Then, for an effective degree $d = \sum_{j \in I} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}$, the following holds: 
%
\begin{equation}
\thp{s_i}{u}{v}_{d} =
%
\begin{cases}
\twp{u}{v}_{d} & \text{\rm if $d_i > 0$ and $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$}, \\  
\langle \CO^{s_i} \cdot \CO^{u}, \CO_{v} \rangle_{d} & \text{\rm if $d_i = 0$},
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation}
%
where $\CO^{s_i} \cdot \CO^u$ denotes the ordinary product in $K_T(X)$. 
\end{ithm}

\begin{irem}
The condition $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$ holds 
for all minuscule or cominuscule fundamental weights $\vpi_i$. 
In addition to these, in type $C$, 
we have $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$ for all $i \in I$; 
in type $F_4$, we have $\pair{\vpi_4}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$, 
where $\alpha_4$ is a short simple root; in type $G_2$, 
we have $\pair{\vpi_1}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$, 
where $\alpha_1$ is the short simple root; see Appendix~\ref{sec:QLS=LS}. 
\end{irem}

\begin{irem}
In the case that $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} \not= 1$, 
for $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$ such that $d_i > 0$, 
the equality $\thp{s_i}{u}{v}_{d} = \twp{u}{v}_{d}$ 
does not necessarily hold (see \cite[Section 4]{LM} for details). 
Indeed, assume that $G$ is of type $G_2$, and 
let $u = s_2 s_1 s_2 s_1 s_2$, $v = s_1$, and 
$d = \alpha_1^{\vee} + 2 \alpha_2^{\vee}$, 
where $\alpha_1$ is the short simple root. 
Then, we have $\thp{s_2}{u}{v}_{d} = 1 + \be^{-\vpi_{2}}$, 
while $\twp{u}{v}_{d} = 1$; see Example~\ref{ex:G2-2}. 
However, for an arbitrary $i \in I$, we can explicitly describe 
the ``correction terms'' which are needed for the equality 
(see Theorem~\ref{thm:correction} below). 
\end{irem} % hoge

Since the 2-point and 3-point KGW invariants are $R(T)$-linear in each argument, 
we immediately obtain the following from Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor}:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:kgwdiv}
\langle \CO^{s_i}, \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d} =
%
\begin{cases}
\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d} 
 & \text{\rm if $d_i > 0$ and $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$},  \\  
\langle \CO^{s_i} \cdot \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d} 
 & \text{\rm if $d_i = 0$}
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation}
%
for all classes $\gamma_1, \gamma_2 \in K_T(X)$ and an effective degree $d = \sum_{j \in I} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}$. 

The (small) $T$-equivariant quantum $K$-theory ring $QK_T(X)$ of $X$, 
defined by Givental \cite{Giv} and Lee \cite{Lee}, is 
(as a module over $R(T)\bra{\sQ}$) $K_T(X) \otimes_{R(T)} R(T)\bra{\sQ}$, 
where $R(T)\bra{\sQ}$ is the ring of formal power series 
with coefficients in $R(T)$ in the (Novikov) variables 
$\sQ_j := \sQ^{\alpha_j^{\vee}}$, $j \in I$. 
The quantum $K$-metric $\Kmet{\,\cdot\,}{\cdot\,}$ on $QK_T(X)$ is 
the $R(T)\bra{\sQ}$-bilinear (perfect) pairing 
$QK_T(X) \times QK_T(X) \rightarrow R(T)\bra{\sQ}$ defined as: 
%
\begin{equation*}
\Kmet{\gamma_1}{\gamma_2} = 
\sum_{d \in Q^{\vee,+}} \sQ^d \langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_d
\end{equation*}
%
for $\gamma_1, \gamma_2 \in K_T(X)$, 
where $\sQ^d := \prod_{j \in I} \sQ_j^{d_j}$ for 
$d = \sum_{j \in I} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}$. 
The quantum product $\star$ in $QK_T(X)$ is determined by:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qproduct}
\Kmet{\gamma_1 \star \gamma_2}{\gamma_3} = 
\sum_{d \in Q^{\vee,+}} \sQ^d \langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \gamma_3 \rangle_d
\end{equation}
%
for $\gamma_1, \gamma_2, \gamma_3 \in K_T(X)$; 
note that the quantum product is $R(T)\bra{\sQ}$-bilinear, 
commutative, and associative, with $1 \in K_T(X)$ the multiplicative identity; 
also, it is known that $\Kmet{\gamma_1 \star \gamma_2}{\gamma_3} = 
\Kmet{\gamma_1}{\gamma_2 \star \gamma_3}$ 
for classes $\gamma_1, \gamma_2, \gamma_3 \in K_T(X)$. 

%One may use \eqref{eq:kgwdiv} and \eqref{eq:qproduct} to deduce relations invol%ving quantum multiplication by $\cO^{s_i}$ in $QK_T(X)$ from relations in $K_T(%X)$; such relations are obtained for flag manifolds of type $A_{n-1}$, i.e., fo%r $G = \SL_n(\BC)$, in \cite{GMSXZZ}. In fact, using the $K$-theoretic divisor %axiom and the methods in \cite{GMSXZZ} and \cite{AHKMOX}, one easily obtains pr%esentations of all $QK_T(\SL_n(\BC)/P)$ without replying on a known presentatio%n of $QK_T(\SL_n(\BC)/B)$. Such an appplication may be generalizable to other L%ie types, where presentations of the quantum $K$-theory rings are mostly unprov%ed. 

Now, the structure constants with respect to 
the (opposite) Schubert classes $\CO^w$, $w \in W$, 
for the quantum multiplication in $QK_T(X)$ are defined by: 
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qcoefficient}
\CO^{u^{\prime}} \star \CO^u = 
\sum_{ \substack{w \in W \\ d \in Q^{\vee,+}} } N_{u^{\prime},u}^{w,d} \sQ^d \CO^w,
\end{equation}
%
with $N_{u^{\prime},u}^{w,d} \in R(T)$ 
for $u^{\prime}, u, w \in W$ and $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$. 
Hence, from the definition \eqref{eq:qproduct} of 
the quantum product $\star$, we deduce that for for $i \in I$, $u, v \in W$, 
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGWinvariants}
\sum_{d \in Q^{\vee,+}} \sQ^d \thp{s_i}{u}{v}_d = 
\Kmet{\CO^{s_i} \star \CO^u}{\CO_v} = 
\sum_{w \in W} a_w(\sQ) 
\sum_{\xi \in Q^{\vee,+}} \sQ^{\xi} \twp{w}{v}_{\xi},
\end{equation}
%
where we set $a_w(\sQ) := 
\sum_{d \in Q^{\vee,+}} N_{s_i, u}^{w, d} \sQ^d \in R(T)\bra{\sQ}$ 
for $w \in W$ for simplicity of notation. 
Here, the 2-point KGW invariants $\twp{w}{v}_{\xi}$ 
for $\xi \in Q^{\vee,+}$ can be explicitly described 
in terms of the quantum Bruhat graph $\QBG(W)$ on the Weyl group $W$. 
To be more precise (see Section~\ref{subsec:QBG} for details), 
for $w, v \in W$, let $\qwt(w \Rightarrow v)$ denote 
the quantum weight, given as the quantum weight $\qwt(\bp)$ of 
a shortest directed path $\bp$ from $w$ to $v$ in $\QBG(W)$ 
(not depending on the choice of $\bp$); 
here, for a directed path $\bp$ in $\QBG(W)$ of the form
%
\begin{equation*}
\bp: y_{0} \edge{\beta_{1}} y_{1} \edge{\beta_{2}} \cdots \edge{\beta_{r}} y_{r}, 
\end{equation*}
%
with $y_k \in W$ for $0 \leq k \leq r$ and 
$\beta_k \in \Delta^+$ for $1 \leq k \leq r$, 
$\qwt(\bp) \in Q^{\vee,+}$ is defined by:
%
\begin{equation*}
\qwt(\bp):=\sum_{ \begin{subarray}{c} 1 \le k \le r \\ 
\text{$y_{k-1} \edge{\beta_k} y_{k}$ is} \\
\text{a quantum edge} \end{subarray} } \beta_{k}^{\vee}. 
\end{equation*}
%
Then, we know from \cite[Lemma~4 and Proposition~6]{BCLM} that
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2ptKGW}
\twp{w}{v}_{\xi} = 
%
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{\rm if $\xi \ge \qwt(w \Rightarrow v)$}, \\
0 & \text{\rm otherwise}.
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation}

Moreover, the structure constants $N_{s_i, u}^{w, d} \in R(T)\bra{\sQ}$ 
for $i \in I$, $u, w \in W$, and $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$ can be explicitly given 
in terms of quantum Lakshmibai-Seshadri (QLS) paths by 
the Chevalley formula for $QK_T(X)$(see Theorem~\ref{thm:NOS} below), 
obtained in \cite{NOS} and \cite{LNS}. 
To be more precise (see Section~\ref{subsec:Monk} along with Section~\ref{subsec:QLS}), 
let $\QLS(\vpi_i)$ denote the set of QLS paths of shape $\vpi_i$. 
We set $\J = \J_{\vpi_i} := \{ j \in I \mid \pair{\vpi_i}{\alpha_j^{\vee}} = 0 \} = 
I \setminus \{ i \}$, and $\WJs := \langle s_j \mid j \in J \rangle \subset W$; 
let $\WJ$ be the set of minimal-length coset representatives for the cosets in $W/W_J$. 
For $w \in W$ and an element $\eta \in \QLS(\vpi_i)$ of the form
%
\begin{equation*}
\eta = (v_{1},\,\dots,\,v_{s} \,;\, 
a_{0},\,a_{1},\,\dots,\,a_{s}) \in \QLS(\vpi_{i}), 
\end{equation*}
%
with $v_k \in \WJ$ for $1 \leq k \leq s$ and $a_k \in \BQ$ 
for $0 \leq k \leq s$ such that $0 = a_0 < a_1 < \cdots < a_{s-1} < a_s = 1$, 
we set 
%
\begin{equation*}
\iota(\eta) := v_1,
\end{equation*}
%
and define recursively
%
\begin{equation*}
%
\begin{cases}
\ha{v}_{0}:=w, & \\[2mm]
\ha{v}_{k}:=\tbmax{v_{k}}{\J}{\ha{v}_{k-1}} & \text{\rm for $1 \le k \le s$}, \\[2mm]
\kappa(\eta,w):=\ha{v}_{s}, 
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation*}
%
where for $1 \leq k \leq s$, $\tbmax{v_{k}}{\J}{\ha{v}_{k-1}}$ is 
the unique maximal element in the coset $v_k W_J$ 
with respect to the dual tilted Bruhat order $\dtb{\ha{v}_{k-1}}$ on $W$. 
In addition, we set 
%
\begin{equation*}
\wt(\eta) := 
\sum_{k=1}^s (a_k - a_{k-1}) v_k \vpi_i \in \Lambda,
\end{equation*}
%
and
%
\begin{equation*}
\zeta(\eta,w):=
\sum_{k=0}^{s-1} \qwt (\ha{v}_{k+1} \Rightarrow \ha{v}_{k}).
\end{equation*}
%
With the notation above, the Chevalley formula implies 
the following equation in $QK_T(X)$ (see Theorem~\ref{thm:NOS}): 
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:chevalley}
\CO^{s_{i}} \star \CO^{u} = 
\CO^{u} + 
\sum_{w \in W}
\sum_{
  \begin{subarray}{c}
   \eta \in \QLS(\vpi_{i}) \\
   \kappa(\eta,w) = u
  \end{subarray}}
  (-1)^{\ell(w) - \ell(u) + 1} \be^{-\vpi_{i}+\wt(\eta)}
  \sQ^{\zeta(\eta, w)} \CO^{w}.
\end{equation}
%
Note that the right-hand side of the equation above is 
cancellation-free (see \cite[Remark~8]{KLNS}); 
also, it follows that $a_w(\sQ) = 
\sum_{d \in Q^{\vee,+}} N_{s_i, u}^{w, d} \sQ^d \in R(T)[\sQ] 
\subset R(T)\bra{\sQ}$ for all $w \in W$, 
where $R(T)[\sQ]$ is the polynomial ring 
with coefficients in $R(T)$ in the variables $\sQ_j$, $j \in I$. 

By making use of equations \eqref{eq:2ptKGW} and \eqref{eq:chevalley}, 
we can explicitly compute the rightmost-hand side of 
equation~\eqref{eq:KGWinvariants} to deduce Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} 
after the necessary cancellations 
(see Sections~\ref{subsec:prf-qk1} and \ref{subsec:prf-qk2}); 
note that the constants $N_{s_i, u}^{w, 0}$ for $u, w \in W$ are 
just the structure constants for 
the ordinary product $\CO^{s_i} \cdot \CO^u$ in $K_T(X)$.
Moreover, we can prove the following theorem 
(which is the combination of 
Theorem~\ref{thm:qk2} and Proposition~\ref{prop:qk2-QLS}), 
which holds for an arbitrary $i \in I$ (not necessarily 
satisfying the condition $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$). 
Indeed, the assertion in the case that $d_i > 0$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} 
follows as a special case of this theorem; 
we know from \cite[Lemma~2.14]{MNS} that $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$ 
if and only if $\QLS(\vpi_i)$ consists only of ordinary LS paths. 
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%% thm:correction %%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
\begin{ithm}\label{thm:correction}
Let $i \in I$. Then, for an effective degree $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$ 
such that $d_i > 0$, the following equation holds:
%
\begin{equation}
\thp{s_i}{u}{v}_{d} = 
\twp{u}{v}_{d} - 
\sum_{w \in v\WJs} \sum_{
 \begin{subarray}{c}
 \eta \in \QLS(\vpi_{i}) \\[1mm]
 \iota(\eta) \in vW_J,\,\kappa(\eta,w)=u \\[1mm]
 \pair{\vpi_{i}}{d-\zeta(\eta,w)} = 0 \\[1mm]
 \qwt(w \Rightarrow v) \le d-\zeta(\eta,w)
 \end{subarray} } (-1)^{\ell(w)-\ell(u)} \be^{-\vpi_{i}+\wt(\eta)}. 
\end{equation}
%
Here, the sum on the right-hand side of this equation turns out 
to be zero if $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$. 
\end{ithm}

We can also prove a parabolic analog of Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor}. 
To be more precise, let $\K$ be an arbitrarily fixed (non-empty) subset of $I$. 
Let $P = P_{\Kc}$ be the (standard) parabolic subgroup of $G$ containing $B$
corresponding to the subset $\Kc$ of $I$, and 
let $Y = G/P$ be the corresponding flag manifold; 
let $W_{\Kc} := \langle s_j \mid j \in \Kc \rangle \subset W$ be 
the Weyl group for $P = P_{\Kc}$. 
The $T$-equivariant $K$-theory ring $K_T(Y)$ of $Y$ 
has an $R(T)$-basis consisting of 
the (opposite) Schubert classes $\CO^w_Y := [\CO_{Y^w}]$, 
$w \in W^{\Kc}$, where $W^{\Kc}$ is the set of 
minimal-length coset representatives for the cosets in $W/W_{\Kc}$; 
note that $Y^w := \ol{B^- wP/P} \subset Y$. 
We also use an $R(T)$-basis of $K_T(Y)$ consisting of 
the Schubert classes $\CO^Y_w := [\CO_{Y_w}]$, $w \in W^{\Kc}_{\max}$, 
where $W^{\Kc}_{\max}$ denotes the set of maximal-length coset representatives 
for the cosets in $W/W_{\Kc}$; note that $Y_w := \ol{B wP/P} \subset Y$. 

The 2nd homology group $H_2(Y; \BZ)$ is identified with 
$Q^{\vee}/Q^{\vee}_{\Kc} \cong Q^{\vee}_{K}$, 
where $Q^{\vee}_{\Kc} := \sum_{j \in \Kc} \BZ \alpha_j^{\vee}$ and 
$Q^{\vee}_{\K} := \sum_{j \in \K} \BZ \alpha_j^{\vee}$, 
with $\alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee}_{\K}$ identified 
with the homology class $[Y_{s_j}] \in H_2(Y, \BZ)$ 
associated to the simple reflection $s_j$ for $j \in K$. 
Let $[\,\cdot\,] = [\,\cdot\,]_{\K} : 
Q^{\vee} \twoheadrightarrow Q^{\vee}/Q^{\vee}_{\Kc} \cong Q^{\vee}_{\K}$ be the projection, 
given by $[\sum_{j \in I} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee}] = \sum_{j \in \K} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee}$. 
For an effective degree $d \in Q^{\vee,+}_{\K} := 
\sum_{j \in \K} \BZ_{\geq 0} \alpha_j^{\vee}$, and $m \geq 1$, 
let $\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y, d)$ denote the Kontsevich moduli space 
parametrizing all $m$-point, genus 0, degree $d$ stable maps to $Y$, 
which is equipped with ($T$-equivariant) evaluation maps 
$\ev_k : \ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y, d) \to Y$ for $1 \leq k \leq m$. 
Then, for classes $\gamma_k \in K_T(Y)$, $1 \leq k \leq m$, 
the $m$-point ($T$-equivariant) $K$-theoretic Gromov-Witten (KGW) invariant is defined to be 
%
\begin{equation*}
\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \ldots , \gamma_m \rangle_d^Y := 
\chi^{T}_{\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y,d)}(\ev_1^*(\gamma_1) \cdot \cdots \cdot \ev_m^*(\gamma_m)) 
\in K_{T}(\mathrm{pt}) = R(T),
\end{equation*}
%
where $\chi^{T}_{\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y,d)}$ is 
the ($T$-equivariant) pushforward along the structure morphism 
$\ol{\CM}_{0,m}(Y, d) \rightarrow \{ \mathrm{pt} \}$; 
the KGW invariant $\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \ldots , \gamma_m \rangle_d^Y$ is 
$R(T)$-linear in each argument $\gamma_j$. 
Let $\pi = \pi_{\Kc} : X = G/B \to Y = G/P$ be 
the natural projection, which induces the pullback $\pi^* : K_T(Y) \to K_T(X)$. 
It is known that $\pi^*(\CO_{Y}^{w}) = \CO^w$ for $w \in W^{\Kc}$, 
and that $\pi^*(\CO^{Y}_{w}) = \CO_w$ for $w \in W^{\Kc}_{\max}$. 
Also, we know from \cite[Lemma~1]{Woo} (see also \cite[Remark~10.17]{LS}) 
that for each $d \in Q^{\vee}_\K \cong H_2(Y; \BZ)$, 
there exists a unique $\dl{d} \in Q^{\vee} \cong H_2(X; \BZ)$ 
which is sent to $d$ under the induced map $H_2(X; \BZ) \rightarrow H_2(Y; \BZ)$ 
(which is just the projection $[\,\cdot]\,$) and 
such that $\pair{\alpha}{\dl{d}} \in \{ 0, -1 \}$ holds for all positive roots in 
$\Delta^+ \cap Q_{\Kc}$, with $Q_{\Kc} := \sum_{j \in \Kc} \BZ \alpha_j$; 
the element $\dl{d}$ is called the Peterson lift of $d$. 
Moreover, by purely geometric arguments, 
we can show that the natural projection $\pi : X \rightarrow Y$ 
induces the (cohomologically trivial) map 
$\Phi : \ol{\CM}_{0,3}(X, \dl{d}) \rightarrow \ol{\CM}_{0,3}(Y, d)$, 
and hence that the following proposition holds (see Appendix~\ref{sec:MX}). 
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%% prop:comparison %%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
\begin{iprop} \label{prop:comparison}
Let $d \in Q^{\vee,+}_{\K} = \sum_{j \in \K} \BZ_{\geq 0} \alpha_j^{\vee}$ 
be an effective degree, and $\dl{d} \in Q^{\vee,+}$ its Peterson lift. 
For classes $\gamma_1,\,\gamma_2,\,\gamma_3 \in K_T(Y)$, 
the following equation for 3-point KGW invariants holds: 
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:comparison}
\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2, \gamma_3 \rangle_{d}^{Y} = 
\langle \pi^*(\gamma_1), \pi^*(\gamma_2), \pi^*(\gamma_3) \rangle_{\dl{d}}.
\end{equation}
%
\end{iprop}

From Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} for $X = G/B$, 
by making use of equation~\eqref{eq:comparison}, 
we immediately obtain the following theorem 
(note that $\pi^*(\CO_{Y}^{u}) = \CO^u$ and 
that $\pi^*(\CO^{Y}_{v}) = \CO_v$); 
this theorem can also be deduced from 
Theorem~\ref{thm:qk1p} and Corollary~\ref{cor:qk2p}. 
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%% thm:parabolic %%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
\begin{ithm}\label{thm:parabolic}
Let $i \in I$, and $u \in W^{\Kc}$, $v \in W^{\Kc}_{\max}$. 
Then, for an effective degree $d = \sum_{j \in K} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}_K$, 
the following holds: 
%
\begin{equation}
\langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i}, \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y =
%
\begin{cases}
\langle \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y 
  & \text{\rm if $d_i > 0$ and $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$}, \\  
\langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i} \cdot \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y 
  & \text{\rm if $d_i = 0$},
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation}
%
where $\CO_{Y}^{s_i} \cdot \CO_{Y}^{u}$ denotes the ordinary product in $K_T(Y)$. 
\end{ithm}

\begin{irem}
If $i \notin K$, i.e., $i \in \Kc$, then we have $s_i \in W_{\Kc}$, 
and hence $\CO_{Y}^{s_i} = [\CO_{Y^{s_i}}] = 1 \in K_T(Y)$; 
in this case, the equality above immediately follows from 
the (simplest case of the) string equation. 
If $i \in K$, then it is clear that 
$\pair{\vpi_i}{\dl{d}} = \pair{\vpi_i}{d} = d_i$ 
since $[\widehat{d}] = d$.
\end{irem}

Since the 2-point and 3-point KGW invariants are $R(T)$-linear in each argument, 
we immediately obtain the following from Theorem~\ref{thm:parabolic}:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:kgwdivp}
\langle \CO^{s_i}, \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d}^{Y} =
%
\begin{cases}
\langle \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d}^{Y} 
 & \text{\rm if $d_i > 0$ and $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$},  \\  
\langle \CO^{s_i} \cdot \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \rangle_{d}^{Y} 
 & \text{\rm if $d_i = 0$}
\end{cases}
%
\end{equation}
%
for all classes $\gamma_1, \gamma_2 \in K_T(Y)$ and an effective degree $d = \sum_{j \in K} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}_K$. 

One may use \eqref{eq:kgwdivp}, together with the relation between the quantum product $\star$ in $QK_{T}(Y)$ and the 3-point KGW invariants (cf. equation~\eqref{eq:qproduct} for $X$), to deduce relations involving quantum product with $\cO^{s_i}$ in $QK_T(Y)$ from relations in $K_T(Y)$; indeed, such relations are deduced for partial flag manifolds of type $A_{n-1}$, i.e., for $G = \SL_n(\BC)$, in \cite{GMSXZZ}. In fact, using the $K$-theoretic divisor axiom and the methods in \cite{GMSXZZ} and \cite{AHKMOX}, one easily obtains presentations of all $QK_T(\SL_n(\BC)/P)$ without relying on a known presentation of $QK_T(\SL_n(\BC)/B)$. Such an application may be generalizable to other Lie types, where presentations of the quantum $K$-theory rings are mostly unproved. 

Finally, the parabolic analog of Theorem~\ref{thm:correction} can be stated as 
Theorem~\ref{thm:pcorrection} below; 
the assertion in the case that $i \in K$ and $d_i > 0$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:parabolic} 
also follows as a special case of this theorem, without using equation~\eqref{eq:comparison}. 
Instead of using equation~\eqref{eq:comparison}, for the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:pcorrection}, 
we make use of the following result, due to Kato (see \cite{Kat}, and also \cite[Section~2.3]{KLNS}). 
Let $QK_T^{\mathrm{poly}}(X) := K_T(X) \otimes_{R(T)} R(T)[\sQ] \subset QK_T(X)$, 
be the polynomial version of $QK_T(X)$, where $R(T)[\sQ]$ is the polynomial ring 
with coefficients in $R(T)$ in the variables $\sQ_j$, $j \in I$. 
Also, let $QK_T^{\mathrm{poly}}(Y) := K_T(Y) \otimes_{R(T)} R(T)[\sQ_\K]$ be 
the polynomial version of $QK_T(Y) := K_T(Y) \otimes_{R(T)} R(T)\bra{\sQ_\K}$, 
where $R(T)[\sQ_{\K}]$ (resp., $R(T)\bra{\sQ_{\K}}$) 
is the ring of polynomials (resp., formal power series) 
with coefficients in $R(T)$ in the variables $\sQ_j$, $j \in \K$; 
the quantum product in $QK_T(Y)$ is defined in the same way as that in 
$QK_T(X)$, with $Y$ in place of $X$ and with $Q_K^{\vee,+}$ in place of $Q^{\vee,+}$. 
Then, we know from \cite{Kat} that the natural projection $\pi : X \rightarrow Y$ 
induces a surjective $R(T)$-module homomorphism $\pi_* : QK_T(X) \rightarrow QK_T(Y)$ 
such that $\pi_*(\CO^w \sQ^d) = \CO_{Y}^{\mcr{w}} \sQ^{[d]}$ 
for $w \in W$ and $d \in Q^{\vee,+}$, where $\mcr{w} = \mcr{w}^{\Kc} \in W^{\Kc}$ 
is the minimal-length coset representative for the coset $w W_{\Kc}$, and such that 
%
\begin{equation}
\pi_*(\CO^{s_j} \star \CO^w) = \CO_{Y}^{s_j} \star \CO_{Y}^{\mcr{w}}
\end{equation}
%
for all $j \in K$ and $w \in W$; 
note that $s_j \in W^{\Kc}$ for all $j \in K$. 
Based on this result, we can prove the following theorem 
(which is the combination of Theorem~\ref{thm:qk2p}, Proposition~\ref{prop:qk2p-QLS}, and Corollary~\ref{cor:qk2p}) 
by the same line of arguments as those for Theorem~\ref{thm:correction}. 
% 
% Recall the projection $[\,\cdot\,] = [\,\cdot\,]_{\K} : 
% Q^{\vee} \twoheadrightarrow Q^{\vee}_{\K}$. 
%
\begin{ithm} \label{thm:pcorrection} 
Let $i \in I$ be such that $i \in K$, and 
let $u \in \WK$, $v \in \WK_{\max}$; 
recall that $J = J_{\varpi_i} = I \setminus \{ i \}$. 
Then, for an effective degree $d = \sum_{j \in \K} d_j \alpha_j^{\vee} \in Q^{\vee,+}_{K}$ 
such that $d_i > 0$, the following equation holds:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Y1}
\langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i}, \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y = 
\langle \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y - 
\sum_{ w \in v \WJs } \sum_{
 \begin{subarray}{c}
 \eta \in \QLS(\vpi_{i}) \\[1mm]
 \iota(\eta) \in vW_J,\,\kappa(\eta,w)=u \\[1mm]
 \pair{\vpi_{i}}{d - [\zeta(\eta,w)]} = 0 \\[1mm]
 [\qwt(w \Rightarrow v)] \le d - [\zeta(\eta,w)]
 \end{subarray} } (-1)^{\ell(w)-\ell(u)} \be^{-\vpi_{i}+\wt(\eta)}. 
\end{equation}
%
Here, the sum on the right-hand side of this equation turns out 
to be zero if $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$. 
\end{ithm}

Theorem \ref{thm:parabolic} has been proved for cominuscule flag manifolds $G/P$ in \cite{BCMP2}, for the incidence variety $\Fl(1,n-1;n)$ in \cite{Xu}, and for $\SG(2,2n)$, the symplectic Grassmannian of lines, in \cite{BPX}. In each case, it was implied by the geometric statement ($\dagger$) below. Let 
\begin{equation*}
\CM_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v) \coloneqq \ev_1^{-1}(Y^{s_i})\cap \ev_2^{-1}(Y_v) \subset \ol{\CM}_{0,3}(Y,d)
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
\Gamma_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v) \coloneqq \ev_3(\CM_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v)) \subset Y.
\end{equation*}
Now, consider the statement: 
\begin{center}
\quad The general fibers of the map $\ev_3: \CM_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v) \to \Gamma_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v)$ are rationally connected. \hfill{($\dagger$)}
\end{center}
It is known that if ($\dagger$) holds, then 
\begin{equation*}
  \langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i}, \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y = \chi_Y^T([\cO_{\Gamma_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v)}] \cdot \cO^u_Y)
\end{equation*}
(see, for instance, \cite{Xu}).
Also, we always have 
\begin{equation*}
  [\cO_{\Gamma_d(Y^{s_i},Y_v)}] = 
  \begin{cases}
  \cO_v^Y[d] & \text{if }d_i>0,\\ \cO^{s_i}_Y\cdot (\cO_v^Y[d]) & \text{if }d_i=0,
  \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $\cO^Y_v[d] \coloneqq {\ev_2}_*\ev_1^*\cO_v^Y$. It then follows from the projection formula that if ($\dagger$) holds, then
\begin{equation}
  \langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i}, \CO_{Y}^{u}, \CO^{Y}_{v} \rangle_{d}^Y =
  %
  \begin{cases}
  \langle \CO_{Y}^{u},\CO^{Y}_{v}  \rangle_{d}^Y 
    & \text{\rm if $d_i > 0$}, \\  
  \langle \CO_{Y}^{s_i} \cdot \CO_{Y}^{u},\CO^{Y}_{v}\rangle_{d}^Y 
    & \text{\rm if $d_i = 0$}.
  \end{cases}
  %
\end{equation}
In the case where the correction term in \eqref{eq:Y1} is nonzero, our result implies that $(\dagger)$ does not hold. Motivated by our results above, we would like to pose the following. 
\begin{ique}
  Does $(\dagger)$ hold whenever $\pair{\vpi_i}{\theta^{\vee}} = 1$ or $d_i=0$?
\end{ique}

This paper is organized as follows. 
In Section~\ref{sec:review}, we fix the basic notation for root systems, and also 
recall the definitions of the quantum Bruhat graph and quantum Lakshmibai-Seshadri paths. 
In Section~\ref{sec:main}, we first recall the definitions of 
torus-equivariant $K$-theoretic Gromov-Witten invariants and 
quantum $K$-theory ring of flag manifolds, and then state our main results; 
that is, we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} above for full flag manifolds and 
Theorem~\ref{thm:parabolic} above for partial flag manifolds. 
In Section~\ref{sec:prf-main}, we first recall the Chevalley formula for 
the quantum $K$-theory ring of flag manifolds, and then, 
by using it, prove Theorem~\ref{thm:qkdivisor} for full flag manifolds. 
In Section~\ref{sec:para}, 
we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:parabolic} for partial flag manifolds. 
In Appendix~\ref{sec:MX}, we give a comparison result 
(that is, Proposition~\ref{prop:comparison} above) 
for $K$-theoretic Gromov-Witten invariants of flag manifolds. 
