We next added time masking, as described in Section \ref{sec:timemask}. In brief, time masking entails masking contiguous temporal chunks of the input neural activity during training. On average over $50$\% of the neural activity within each trial was masked out during training (Section \ref{appendix:masking_percentage}). Adding time masking to the previous modifications (LR scheduler, log transform) significantly reduced WER to $12.46 \pm 0.12\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:main_result}a) ($p < 0.001$). 

Finally, replacing the GRU architecture with a Transformer (Section~\ref{sec:arch}) yielded a further, though non-significant, improvement to $12.27 \pm 0.15\%$ ($p > 0.05$) (Figure~\ref{fig:main_result}a). The masked Transformer overall reduced WER by $19.5\%$ relative to the baseline. Additionally, replacing the GRU with the Transformer also substantially reduced computational costs: the Transformer used $77.25\%$ fewer parameters, cut peak GPU memory by $35.56\%$, reduced FLOPs by $21.2\%$ (Section~\ref{ref:FLOPS}), while shortening per-epoch training time by $47.5\%$ (Table~\ref{table:memory}). These efficiency gains make the architecture well-suited for on-device test-time adaptation \citep{Cai2020-ch} and motivate the experiments in Section~\ref{memo_section_results}. Beam search decoding times—measured after producing logits—were also approximately $3.5 \times$ faster when using the time-masked Transformer compared to the baseline GRU (Section~\ref{appendix:beam_search}).

We next performed leave-one-out ablations on the masked Transformer to explore the factors contributing to its high performance (Figure~\ref{fig:main_result}b). Ablating the learning rate scheduler and log transform increased the average WER across seeds by $10.01\%$ and $10.81\%$ respectively, suggesting these modifications were more crucial for the masked Transformer relative to the baseline GRU. We next ablated time masking, and used the baseline hyperparameter settings to train the Transformer (Section \ref{appendix:hyperparameters}). WER increased by $18.07\%$, highlighting the importance of time masking. Since past BCI studies used absolute sinusoidal embeddings \citep{Costello2023-or,Wairagkar2024-oo}, we finally replaced our relative positional embeddings with sinusoidal embeddings and observed a large $24.05\%$ increase in WER. Therefore, relative positional embeddings may be important in helping the Transformer focus on local information that is relevant for phoneme decoding. 

%We first experimented with ablating the learning rate scheduler and log transform, which increased WER by  We next removed time masking and found that WER increased by $18.1\%$. While this was a sizable increase, the Transformer without time masking outperformed the baseline GRU ($14.49\%$ versus $15.39\%$), indicating the Transformer is an effective architecture even without time masking. 

In order to test whether time masking mitigates overfitting as hypothesized earlier, we plotted CTC-loss curves for a representative seed of the Transformer trained with and without masking (Figure~\ref{fig:main_result}c). Time masking narrows the gap between training and validation loss curves (purple versus gray), supporting the hypothesis that it improves generalization.

\subsection{Generalization to offline language modeling setup}
\label{juiced_up_decoding}
We next compared the masked Transformer to the baseline GRU when using the offline language modeling setup. The offline setup is better optimized, yet also substantially increased latency and memory requirements. Across $10$ random seeds, the masked Transformer achieved a WER of $8.54 \pm 0.11\%$ while the baseline GRU achieved a WER of $11.12 \pm 0.13$. This is a $23.25\%$ reduction in WER which was significant ($p < 0.001$), indicating that our contributions are beneficial even when using stronger language modeling. The unidirectional masked Transformer even outperformed the bidirectional baseline GRU reported in \citet{Willett2024-ki} by $11.96\%$, highlighting the importance of algorithmic developments. We also compared the masked Transformer to our attempted replication of the modified GRU proposed by the "Linderman Lab" entry \citep{Willett2024-ki}, which was the previous largest accuracy gain obtained by modifying the GRU, in Section \ref{appendix:modified_GRU}.

\subsection{Evaluating robustness to lower channel count, training data, and held-out days}
\label{robustness_section}
\begin{figure}[t!]
