While the masked Transformer outperformed the baseline GRU under the standard Brain-to-Text Benchmark splits, Transformers have been reported to be more data-hungry than other architectures \citep{Dosovitskiy2020-lu}. We therefore asked whether the masked Transformer remains robust under three data-constrained scenarios: (i) halving the channel count, (ii) reducing the number of training days, and (iii) evaluating on days with no training data (held-out days).

First, we limited the input to the ventral 6v microelectrode array—previously shown by \citet{Willett2023-an} to carry most of the decoding signal—thereby cutting channels from $128$ to $64$. The average word-error rate (WER) across seeds increased by an absolute $5.97$ percentage points for the baseline GRU and $5.60$ percentage points for the masked Transformer (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}a), indicating a comparable degradation for both models. Second, we reduced the training set from $24$ days to $5$ days (Section \ref{sec:splits}). This produced an absolute WER increase of $11.62$ percentage points for the GRU and $10.89$ percentage points for the masked Transformer (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}b), again with no meaningful gap between architectures. Finally, we trained on the first $15$ days and evaluated on the next $5$ competition (held-out) days (Section \ref{sec:splits}). The masked Transformer outperformed the GRU on every held-out day (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}c). Under the more challenging setting of using $12$ training days and $8$ held-out days, both models deteriorated markedly with the Transformer still maintaining its advantage (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}d). Therefore, the masked Transformer is at least as robust as the baseline GRU under data-constrained conditions.

