\end{table}

Performance dropped sharply across held-out days, likely due to distribution shifts in neural activity. Prior work (CORP) demonstrated that calibrating with LM-refined pseudo-labels can largely avert this decline without additional labeled data. We therefore evaluated a lightweight variant, DietCORP, which adapts the decoder in a single gradient step using multiple augmentations of the current trial (Section~\ref{TTA_methods}). We used the exact same augmentations as during training, with time masking serving as the main augmentation. All analyses focused on the masked Transformer. After training on $15$\,days and testing on the next $5$ competition days, DietCORP kept WER nearly flat (ranging from $22.91\%$--$23.98\%$), whereas WER without adaptation rose from $23.89\%$ to $32.28\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:dietCORP}a). A similar pattern emerged when the model was trained on $12$\,days and evaluated on the subsequent $8$ competition days: DietCORP constrained WER between $26.97\%$--$30.53\%$, whereas WER without adaptation exhibited a wide range between $30.75\%$--$64.86\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:dietCORP}b).

We next evaluated how DietCORP’s benefit scales with the number of augmentations ($Z$). Reducing the augmentation count progressively diminished its impact, supporting the idea that adaptation across multiple views helps buffer against distribution shifts in neural activity (Figure~\ref{fig:dietCORP}c). Nevertheless, DietCORP still outperformed the no adaptation setting even when using only a single augmentation. We finally quantified the memory requirements and adaptation times for DietCORP. When using the masked Transformer, DietCORP required $18$ ms per trial and its peak memory usage was $1.4$ GiB (Table \ref{table:memo_memory}). Applying DietCORP to the baseline GRU increased adaptation times by $54.7\%$ and peak memory usage by $109\%$, underscoring the utility of the Transformer for on-device test-time adaptation. We expect that adaptation times and memory requirements using CORP are several-fold higher than DietCORP since CORP uses multiple gradient steps and requires storing previous data. 

\section{Discussion}
There are three noteworthy limitations to our results. First, all results are on a single participant. To the best of our knowledge, there are no other open-source microelectrode array datasets for speech neuroprostheses available at this time, and evaluation on one participant is standard of the field. However, it is still important to evaluate the effectiveness of masked Transformers and DietCORP across multiple participants. Second, incorporating time masking increased the number of epochs for training by several-fold ($73 \rightarrow 600$). Although this increase may be inconvenient for researchers aiming to rapidly iterate on model designs, it is less critical for patients, as model training is typically performed offline and only once. In contrast, the speed of test-time adaptation is more relevant in clinical settings, where adaptation occurs frequently and during real-time use. Third, our use of beam search allows previously decoded text to be revised, which complicates integration with text-to-speech systems.

In summary, this work delivers three practical advances for the Brain-to-Text Benchmark '24 and, more broadly, for the development of speech neuroprostheses. First, we showed that large amounts of time masking is a highly effective data augmentation method for training neural networks to decode speech from neural activity. Second, we replaced the baseline GRU with a compact Transformer that is faster to train and is more suitable for test-time adaptation. Third, we introduced DietCORP, a simple, lightweight, and fast method that utilizes multiple time-masked augmentations of the same trial to effectively adapt the Transformer at test-time. Together, these innovations lower word error rate while cutting resource demands, moving us closer to building robust, on-device speech neuroprostheses that restore fluent communication for patients with severe paralysis. 

There are several exciting avenues for further improving speech neuroprostheses beyond those mentioned in \citet{Willett2024-ki}. First, the $3$-gram LM used by \citet{Willett2023-an} requires around $60$GB of CPU memory to run, making the system infeasible for on-device applications. Although we focused on advancing the phoneme decoding process here, future work should investigate ways to reduce memory costs associated with the $N$-gram LM or explore alternative strategies to integrate text-based knowledge. Second, current test-time adaptation methods require storing activations for all model layers during backpropagation, which is not ideal for adaptation on resource-limited devices. Future work should investigate cutting memory costs by incorporating memory-efficient test-time adaptation methods \citep{Song2023-sf}. Third, integrating Transformers with CNNs appears to yield benefits for ASR \citep{Gulati2020-uo}. Although we did not experiment with hybrid architectures here, adding convolutional blocks or other modules with local biases to the masked Transformer may further boost performance. Finally, future work should explore achieving similar accuracy gains with greedy decoding or other systems that are compatible with real-time audio decoding \citep{Littlejohn2025-hw}.

We hope that our study encourages future speech neuroprostheses benchmarks, such as the soon-to-be released Brain-to-Text Benchmark '25, to offer a more holistic evaluation criteria for decoding algorithms beyond only accuracy. By evaluating decoding algorithms along multiple criteria such as real-time decoding compatibility, computational costs, and integration with existing test-time adaptation methods, these benchmarks can more effectively spur innovation and assist paralyzed patients with communication impairments. 

