We additionally experimented with masking entire channels (channel masking) instead of time masking. In order to do so, we applied $N$ channel masks for each microelectrode array. For each mask, we selected a starting channel $i$. Then, we masked (replaced with $0$) the closest $p$ channels from channel $i$, where "closest" was quantified by the euclidean distance from channel $i$. $p$ was uniformly sampled between $0$ and $M \times 64$ (there were $64$ channels per microelectrode array), and $M$ could range from $0$ to $1$. Our initial results suggested that channel masking was not as effective as time masking on this dataset. Specifically, we achieved a validation PER of $17.87\%$ when $N=15$ and $M=5\%$, and a PER of $20.54\%$ when $N=25$ and $M=5\%$ on a single seed. By contrast, time masking achieves a PER of $13.79\%$ when using the same seed. We also explored incorporating channel masking with time masking, and found that while this reduced the validation CTC loss, it did not lead to a significant decrease in validation PER or WER.



