\section{Methodology}

\subsection{4-channel Bilateral Control}
We will explain a 4-channel bilateral control system that uses a rigid serial link model.
The block diagram is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:block_diagram}.
Although many studies on 4-channel bilateral control have been published in the past, the major feature of the control system used in this study is that it utilizes parameter identification of the nonlinear dynamics of the manipulator's rigid body model and incorporates compensation for the nonlinear term, as well as state estimation based on this.

In general, assuming that each link is a rigid body, the dynamics of the manipulator can be expressed as a rigid serial link model.
\begin{align}
\bm{\tau}_{ref} + \bm{\tau}_{ext} = \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})\ddot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{c}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}})\dot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{D}\dot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{g}(\bm{\theta})
\end{align}
where
$\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})$ is the inertial matrix,
$\bm{c}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}})\dot{\bm{\theta}}$ is the centrifugal and Coriolis forces,
$\bm{D}$ is the viscous friction coefficient,
$\bm{g}(\bm{\theta})$ is the gravitational forces
and $\bm{\tau}_{ref}, \bm{\tau}_{ext}$ are the reference and external torques of each joint, respectively.
After compensating, the dynamics can be written as follows:
\begin{align}
\bm{\tau}_{ref}
&=
\bm{\tau}_{u}
 + \tilde{\bm{c}}(\bm{\theta}, \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}) \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} + \tilde{\bm{D}} \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} + \tilde{\bm{g}}(\bm{\theta}) \label{eq:compensation} \\
\bm{\tau}_{u} &= \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})\ddot{\bm{\theta}} - \bm{\tau}_{ext} \label{eq:tau_u}
\end{align}
where $\bm{\tau}_{u}$ is the reference torque other than compensation, $\tilde{\bigcirc}$ denotes parameter-identified manipulator dynamics functions, and $\hat{\bigcirc}$ denotes values estimated by an observer, which will be explained later.
Conventionally, it was difficult to perform bilateral control using strict dynamic models such as (\ref{eq:compensation}) and~(\ref{eq:tau_u}). We propose to conduct parameter identification using the regressor matrix based on OpenSYMORO~\cite{khalil2014OpenSYMORO}, which is software to derive efficient robot dynamics parameters, and the least squares method.
Here, we consider the coordinate transformation of the joint angler velocity of the leader and follower into differences and averages.
\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{-} \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{+} \\
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{I} & -\bm{I} \\
\frac{1}{2} \bm{I}  & \frac{1}{2}  \bm{I} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_l \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}
\coloneqq
\bm{J}_{-+}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_l \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}
where $\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{-}$ and $\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{+}$ are difference and average of the joint angler velocity.
This transformation allows us to think of position and force control independently.
In other words, if the same amount of velocity is applied to both the leader and the follower in opposite directions, only the difference in the joint angle will change, not the average.
Conversely, if the same amount of velocity is applied to both the leader and the follower in the same direction, only the average joint angle will change, not the difference.
Ideally, these do not influence each other, i.e., they are orthogonal, so control systems can be designed separately for each.
Since the coordinate transformation matrix is time-invariant, the coordinate transformation of the angle and angular acceleration can be described by the same matrix $\bm{J}_{-+}$.
The dynamics of the difference and the average of the joint angles are as follows.
\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{u}
&= \begin{bmatrix}
\bm{M}_l(\bm{\theta}_l) & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{M}_f(\bm{\theta}_f) \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_l \\
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_f \\
\end{bmatrix} 
- 
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
% \\ &= 
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{M}_l(\bm{\theta}_l) & \bm{0}\\
\bm{0} & \bm{M}_f(\bm{\theta}_f) \\
\end{bmatrix}
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \frac{1}{2}\bm{I} & \bm{I} \\
% -\frac{1}{2}\bm{I} & \bm{I} \\
% \end{bmatrix}
\bm{J}_{-+}^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_- \\
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix} 
- 
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
\label{eq:two-manipulator-dynamics2}
\end{align}
For teleoperation, the desired dynamics for the joint angle difference are fixed, which means heavy weight, significant friction, and high stiffness.
On the other hand, the desired dynamics for the average joint angle are light and have no spring or damper.
The desired dynamics can be written as a spring-mass-damper system like impedance control~\cite{hogan1984impedance} on transformed coordinates~\cite{tsuji2006controller} as below:
\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_- \\
\bm{\tau}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
=
\bm{J}_{-+}^{-\top}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{1}{2} \bm{I} & -\frac{1}{2} \bm{I} \\
\bm{I} & \bm{I} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{M}_{-} & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{M}_{+} \\
\end{bmatrix}_{des}
\begin{bmatrix}
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_- \\
\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{D}_{-} & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}_{des}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_- \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{K}_{-} & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}_{des}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\theta}_- \\
\bm{\theta}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}
where $\bigcirc_{des}$ are desired dynamics parameters, and $\bm{\tau}_{-, ext}, \bm{\tau}_{+, ext}$ are external forces corresponding to the difference and average of the joint angle.
% The negative sign of $\bm{\tau}_{-, ext}, \bm{\tau}_{+, ext}$ is due to the external force being defined as the opposite sign to the reference force.
Since the system has position control and force control on orthogonal axes, the system can also be interpreted as position/force hybrid control~\cite{raibert1981hybrid} on transformed coordinates~\cite{sakaino2011multi-DOF}.
The control law can be derived by substituting the desired dynamics into the transformed dynamics:
\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{u}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{M}_l(\bm{\theta}_l) & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{M}_f(\bm{\theta}_f) \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{1}{2}\bm{I} & \bm{I} \\
-\frac{1}{2}\bm{I} & \bm{I} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
-\bm{M}_{-des}^{-1}\bm{D}_{-des}\dot{\bm{\theta}}_- - \bm{M}_{-des}^{-1}\bm{K}_{-des}\bm{\theta}_- \\
\bm{M}_{+des}^{-1}\bm{\tau}_+ \\
\end{bmatrix}
- 
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
\\ &=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{M}_l(\bm{\theta}_l) & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{M}_f(\bm{\theta}_f) \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{K}_{p}(\bm{\theta}_f - \bm{\theta}_l)
+ \bm{K}_{d}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_f - \dot{\bm{\theta}}_l)
+ \bm{K}_{f}(\bm{\tau}_{l, ext} + \bm{\tau}_{f, ext})
\\
\bm{K}_{p}(\bm{\theta}_l - \bm{\theta}_f)
+ \bm{K}_{d}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_l - \dot{\bm{\theta}}_f)
+ \bm{K}_{f}(\bm{\tau}_{l, ext} + \bm{\tau}_{f, ext})
\\
\end{bmatrix}
- 
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\tau}_l \\
\bm{\tau}_f \\
\end{bmatrix}_{ext}
\end{align}
where $\bm{K}_{p}=\frac{1}{2}\bm{M}_{-des}^{-1}\bm{K}_{-des}$, $\bm{K}_{d}=\frac{1}{2}\bm{M}_{-des}^{-1}\bm{D}_{-des}$, $\bm{K}_{f}=\bm{M}_{+des}^{-1}$.
Finally, the control law for each manipulator can be as follows:
\begin{align}
% \bm{\tau}_{u}  &=
\bm{\tau}_{ref}  &=
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})
\left \{
\bm{K}_{p}(\bm{\theta}_{des} - \bm{\theta})
+ \bm{K}_{d}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{des} -\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}})
+ \bm{K}_{f}(\bm{\tau}_{des} + \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext})
\right \}
- \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext}
 + \tilde{\bm{c}}(\bm{\theta}, \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}) \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} + \tilde{\bm{D}} \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} + \tilde{\bm{g}}(\bm{\theta})
\end{align}
where 
% $m(\bm{\theta})$ is diagonal element of inertia matrix, $\bm{K_{p}, K_{d}, K_{f}$ are diagonal element of $\bm{K}_{p}, \bm{K}_{d}, \bm{K}_{f}$, and 
$\bm{\theta}_{des}, \dot{\bm{\theta}}_{des}, \bm{\tau}_{des}$ are opposite side states between leader and follower.
% Although the inertia matrix has nondiagonal elements, it is simplified to 0 because nondiagonal elements are tiny compared to the diagonal elements in practice.
% Including nonlinear compensation (eq ~\ref{eq:compensation}), 
The result can be understood as a combination of the computed torque method, position PD control, and force control.

\subsection{Velocity and External Force Observer}
To implement the above 4-channel bilateral control, the joint angles, joint angular velocities, and external forces of the leader and follower are required.
However, the manipulator used in this study is equipped only with 12-bit rotary encoders to measure joint angles.
Therefore, the joint angular velocities and external forces must be estimated by an observer.
% Although many method for velocitiy estimation on manipulator are proposed~\cite{liu2022velocity}, here we introduce the observer that estimate both velocity and external force simultaneously.
Although many methods for estimating the velocity of a manipulator have been proposed~\cite{liu2022velocity}\cite{arteaga2022local}, here we introduce an observer that can estimate both the velocity and the external forces simultaneously.

Assuming that external forces remain constant ($\frac{d}{dt}\left\{\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{ext}\right\}=0$), the dynamics of the manipulator can be described in the following state equation form.
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\theta} \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}} \\
\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{0} & \bm{I} & \bm{0} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} & \bm{I} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\theta} \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}} \\
\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{0} \\
\bm{I} \\
\bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u}
% \\
,\quad
\bm{y}&=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{I} & \bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{\theta} \\
\dot{\bm{\theta}} \\
\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}
Minimal-order observers are often used to minimize the phase delay of external force estimation in disturbance observers~\cite{ohnishi1996motion}\cite{murakami1993torque}.
Although several methods have been proposed for constructing minimal-order observers, here we explain a specific, simplified method.
First, assuming that velocity can be observed, we will construct a full-order observer using a state-space model with only velocity and external force.
% Until now, we have used vectors to describe all joints collectively, but for simplicity, we will focus on a single joint and describe it using scalars.
% \begin{align}
% s
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
% \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
% \end{bmatrix}
% =
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \bm{0} & -\bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1} \\
% \bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
% \end{bmatrix}
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
% \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
% \end{bmatrix}
% +
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1} \\
% \bm{0} \\
% \end{bmatrix}
% \bm{\tau}_{ref}
% +
% \begin{bmatrix}
% \bm{g}_1 \\
% \bm{g}_2 \\
% \end{bmatrix}
% (s\bm{\theta}-\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}})
% \end{align}
\begin{align}
% s
\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{0} & \bm{I} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{I} \\
\bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
2\zeta\omega_c \bm{I} \\
\omega_c^2 \bm{I} \\
\end{bmatrix}
(\frac{d}{dt}\bm{\theta}-\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}})
\label{eq:vfob}
\end{align}
Here, the observer gain is set using $\omega_c$, corresponding to the cut-off angular frequency in the frequency domain.
The critical point is that inertia is not a constant but a function of the joint angle vector.
Conventional disturbance observers use constants for inertia, and the effects of modeling errors are included in the estimated external force.
Therefore, it was assumed that inertia could be nominalized by feedback compensation of the external force. However, the extent to which modeling errors can be compensated depends on how high the cut-off frequency (observer gain) can be set. In practice, when using control equipment capable of achieving high-precision rotary encoders and high sampling frequencies, it is possible to set a fairly high cut-off frequency, and by combining fast external force estimation and compensation for modeling errors, it is practically feasible to ignore modeling errors. However, in the case of low-cost hardware like the one used here, a high cut-off frequency cannot be set, so the error in inertia has a significant impact that cannot be ignored. By pre-identifying the nonlinear dynamics of the manipulator with 38 parameters, including the inertial matrix, and using this to always employ accurate inertial parameters, it is possible to improve control performance while maintaining a low cost.

Furthermore, using the identified inertia justifies the use of a velocity observer. In many studies of 4-channel bilateral control, pseudo-differentiation ($\frac{\omega_c s}{s+\omega_c}$) is used for velocity estimation, but pseudo-differentiation is known to cause estimation delays~\cite{liu2022velocity}. Nevertheless, pseudo-differentiation is widely used because the inertia is not accurately known, and methods that can calculate using only angle response values without using inertia are preferred. If accurate inertia can be obtained, it becomes possible to use not only the information from the angular response values but also predictions from the torque reference values, thereby improving the delay in speed estimation. Improving the delay in speed estimation allows the D gain of the position PD control to be set higher, and setting the D gain higher enables the P gain to be set higher as well, leading to a significant improvement in position control performance.
% The estimated values of angular velocity and external force over a continuous period of time are as follows.

Converting this to a transfer function expression using the Laplace transform, we can write it as follows.
\begin{align}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}\frac{1}{s}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} + \frac{2\zeta\omega_c}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}s\bm{\theta} \qquad \left ( \ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} \coloneqq \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}(\bm{\tau}_{u}+\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext}) \right ) \\
% s\hat{\tau}_{ext} &= \omega_c^2 \left ( s\theta - \hat{\dot{\theta}} \right ) \\
% &= \omega_c^2 \left \{ s\theta - \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}\frac{1}{s}m(\bm{\theta})^{-1}(\tau_{u}+\hat{\tau}_{ext}) - \frac{2\zeta\omega_c}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}s\theta \right \} \\
% \left ( s+\frac{\omega_c^2}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}m^{-1} \right ) \hat{\tau}_{ext} &= \omega_c^2 \left ( \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} s\theta - \frac{1}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}m(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\tau_{u} \right ) \\
\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &=\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \frac{\omega_c^2}{s^2 + 2\zeta\omega_c s + \omega_c^2} (s^2 \bm{\theta} - \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\tau}_{u})
\end{align}
We implemented the algorithm using a bilinear transformation ($s = \frac{2}{T} \cdot \frac{1 - z^{-1}}{1 + z^{-1}}$).
% Mathematically, since our model treats inertia as a function of joint angle, it is necessary to perform a Laplace transform here as well. However, for simplicity, we treat it as a constant. If the change in inertia is relatively fast compared to the sampling frequency, it is necessary to treat it more rigorously. However, in manipulator dynamics, the change in inertia is sufficiently slow compared to the sampling frequency, so it works well in practice.

% This format tells us a lot about the behavior of the observer. First, regarding the velocity estimate, the first term on the right-hand side is the term that predicts the velocity. The difference between the torque reference value and the external force estimation value is divided by inertia to convert it to acceleration units, integrated to convert it to velocity, and passed through a high-pass filter. In a velocity observer that does not use external disturbance estimation, there is a problem where an offset occurs in the velocity estimation value when an external force is applied. However, this offset can be prevented by using the external force estimation value in the prediction. The second term on the right-hand side is a feedback term using angle measurement values, corresponding to a pseudo-derivative. The cut-off frequency used in the low-pass filter portion of the pseudo-derivative corresponds to the high-pass filter of the first term on the right-hand side, forming a complementary filter configuration. In other words, the velocity observer is structured such that the predicted values compensate for the low-frequency components cut by the low-pass filter of the pseudo-derivative.

