\subsection{Teleoperation Performance}
We implemented the proposed 4-channel bilateral control on CRANE-X7, a 7 DoF low-cost manipulator manufactured by RT Corporation.
We compared teleoperation performance by repeatedly swinging the joint closest to the base as quickly as possible at a 90-degree angle 10 times.
The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:teleoperation_comparison}.
We compared three other teleoperation techniques and conducted three ablation studies.


% \subsubsection{vs. Unilateral}
\textbf{vs. Unilateral Control.}
Unilateral control is a teleoperation method that controls the position of the follower by using the leader's position as the target position.
The biggest drawback of unilateral control is the lack of force feedback for the operator, though position tracking performance is also an issue.
Compared to the 4-channel type, which uses position and force control for both the leader and follower robots, unilateral control uses only position control for the follower, resulting in limited tracking performance at the same gain.
As a result, the position error became larger than that of the proposed method.
% In unilateral control, the follower could not keep up with the rapid movements of the leader.
% The MAE of the position tracking error was 10.3 for unilateral control and 1.78 for proposed 4-channel bilateral control.
% Even though the follower side has not been touched, the force estimation value has become unrealistically large. This is thought to be due to the increase in position error, which causes the reference torque value to become very large. As a result, the actual torque that can be generated is limited by the actuator's limits, preventing the acceleration from increasing. These gaps are believed to be the cause of significant estimation errors.
% If there is force error feedback, the force reference value on the follower side is pulled not only by the position on the leader side but also by the estimated force value, thereby avoiding such extreme situations. This is why force feedback is essential not only for contact-rich tasks but also for high-speed motion.

% \subsubsection{vs. Symmetric Position Type}
\textbf{vs. Symmetric Position Type Bilateral Control.}
Symmetric position type bilateral control is a method in which both the leader and follower control their positions based on each other's positions as targets.
It is known that this method causes the operation to feel very heavy due to the force that acts to keep the current position unchanged.
In this case, since the manipulator was relatively lightweight, the heavy operation problem did not seem so serious.
On the other hand, there was a difference in the position tracking error.
In the case of a 4-channel type, the large operating force applied to the leader when the leader changes direction is also applied to the follower by force control, enabling rapid directional changes. On the other hand, in a symmetric position type that relies solely on position control, large feedback was not generated unless a large position error occurred, making it difficult to follow rapid directional changes.
% Although it felt heavier in this experiment than the 4-channel type, it showed good behavior among the remote control methods.
% The MAE of the position tracking error was 4.60 for symmetric position type bilateral control, and 1.78 for proposed 4-channel bilateral control.


% \subsubsection{vs. Force Feedback Type}
\textbf{vs. Force Feedback Type Bilateral Control.}
Force feedback type bilateral control is a method in which the follower side performs position error feedback and the leader side performs force error feedback.
Like unilateral control, this type also relies solely on the position control of the follower to reduce position error.
Consequently, its position tracking performance was lower than that of the 4-channel type and symmetric position type at the same gain. 
However, due to the force control on the leader side, its tracking performance is slightly higher than that of unilateral control.
% The MAE of the position tracking error was 4.72 for symmetric position type bilateral control, and 1.78 for the proposed 4-channel bilateral control.
% This causes significant position errors and large force estimation errors similar to those seen in unilateral control.


% \subsubsection{vs. fixed inertia}
\textbf{vs. 4ch Bilateral Control using fixed inertia.}
% We compared the results obtained using fixed inertial parameters without considering inertial variations.
% Control systems using fixed inertia (fixed gain) are simple and easy to stabilize, so they are often used in manipulator control, but their performance depends on posture and is not constant.
% As a result, we confirmed that using a model that considers inertial variations significantly reduces position errors.
We compared the results obtained using fixed inertial parameters to those obtained when inertial variations were considered.
Although control systems with fixed inertia (fixed gain) are simple and easy to stabilize and they are often used in manipulator control, their performance depends on posture and is not constant.
The results of the experiment showed that considering inertial variations significantly reduced position errors.
% Controlling a manipulator with fixed inertia assuming that the inertia changes causes errors in speed and external force estimation due to modeling errors, and also makes it impossible to keep the actual gain constant.
% This is a conventional 4-channel bilateral controller.
