\subsection{Imitation Learning}
We experimented to collect demonstration data using the proposed 4-channel bilateral teleoperation method and perform imitation learning. 
We used Action Chunking with Transformer (ACT)~\cite{zhao2023learning} for neural network architecture.
The combination of ACT and 4-channel bilateral control is called Bi-ACT~\cite{buamanee2024bi}, and has already been validated on Pick-and-Place and Put-in-drawer tasks.
With 4-channel bilateral control, data on the position and force of the leader and follower can be obtained. In addition to using all of this data, we also performed learning using only position information and force information only on the input side. We investigated the effects of using force information on both the input and output sides.

It should be noted that a control system using position and force command values was employed during data collection.
Since the control system differs between data collection and autonomous operation, a decrease in success rate when force information is not used in the output of policy was expected.
However, differences in control systems or dynamics between data collection and autonomous operation are common when using data collection methods other than teleoperation, and this is not uncommon in imitation learning.
Additionally, since the dataset's quality can significantly affect imitation learning results, it is preferable to compare using the same dataset. Considering these points, we conducted this comparison.

% \subsubsection{Task1: Dual-arm Pick-and-Place}
\textbf{Task1: Dual-arm Pick-and-Place.}
We performed a task of grasping blocks of different widths between two arms.
We prepared five types of blocks, ranging from 10 mm to 50 mm in width, and collected a total of 10 demonstration data sets, two for each type.
The blocks are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:object_pick_and_place}, and the results are shown in Table~\ref{table:dual_arm_pick_and_place}.
As a result, when no force information was used for either input or output, picking was not possible in cases with small widths of 10 mm and 20 mm. In cases with relatively large widths of 30 mm to 50 mm, picking was possible once, but problems such as dropping the object immediately or the angle changing while holding it occurred, and the object could not be placed in the correct position.
When force information was used in the input, the success rate of picking improved significantly, with all trials resulting in successful picking. However, instability after picking persisted, and placement failures occurred frequently in cases where the width was farthest from the average of 10mm and 50mm.
When both input and output used force information, both pick and place were successful in all trials. The stability of holding after picking improved, with no instances of dropping midway or misalignment.

