\subsection{Implementation}
CRANE-X7, a manipulator manufactured by RT Corporation, was employed.
The manipulator exhibits seven degrees of freedom, while the gripper exhibits one degree of freedom, thereby providing a total of eight degrees of freedom.
We also used an Intel RealSense D435i to capture RGB images.
The manipulators and camera were connected to a PC running Ubuntu 22.04 LTS.
The PC was equipped with 32 GB RAM, an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X 16-Core Processor as CPU, and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 as GPU.
The control system is implemented in C++, with each manipulator running on a separate thread.
Periodic execution is implemented using ``timerfd''.
Each thread was set to have a specific CPU affinity and priority on Ubuntu, enabling a soft real-time 1 kHz loop.
The imitation learning policy is implemented in Python, and inter-process communication with the C++ control system is achieved using shared memory.

\subsection{Cross-Structure Hand}
We used an improved version of the Cross-Structure Hand~\cite{yamane2023soft}.
The figure is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cross_structure_hand}.
The Cross-Structure Hand is a simple one-degree-of-freedom rotating hand, but thanks to its crossed structure, it can close down to the base of the fingers, which makes it possible to perform powerful grasping of tools in particular.
The hand used in this experiment has a narrower finger spacing of 5mm, which allows it to grip even more delicate objects.
In addition, the shape of the hand, which narrows in width towards the tip, allows it to apply pinpoint force when necessary, and also reduces interference between the hands when performing dual-arm tasks.

\subsection{Parameter Identification of Rigid Serial Link Model}
The rigid serial link model can be converted to linear equations by performing a variable transformation.
The matrix obtained by transforming position, velocity, and acceleration is referred to as the regressor matrix, and the corresponding constant parameters are termed parameter vectors.
The parameter vectors can be estimated using linear least squares.
% We identified parameters of manipulator dynamics using the Regressar matrix and the least squares method.
The regressor matrix~$\bm{Y}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}}, \ddot{\bm{\theta}})$ and the parameter vector~$\bm{\phi}$ of the manipulator dynamics are written as follows:
\begin{align}
\bm{\tau} &= \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})\ddot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{c}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}})\dot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{D}\dot{\bm{\theta}} + \bm{g}(\bm{\theta})
\\  &= \bm{Y}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}}, \ddot{\bm{\theta}}) \bm{\phi}
.
\end{align}
% The estimated values of the parameter vector can be obtained using the constrained linear least squares method as follows:
% \begin{align}
% \min_{\bm{\phi}} || \bm{Y}(\bm{\theta}, \dot{\bm{\theta}}, \ddot{\bm{\theta}}) \bm{\phi} - \bm{\tau} ||^2 \\
% \text{subject to} \quad  \bm{E} \bm{\phi} \geq \bm{f}
% % .
% \end{align}
% where constraint $\bm{E} \bm{\phi} \geq \bm{f}$ is typically used to avoid negative inertia.
The estimated values of the parameter vector can be obtained using the linear least squares method as follows:
\begin{align}
\hat{\bm{\phi}} = (\bm{Y}^{\top}\bm{Y})^{-1}\bm{Y}^{\top} \bm{\tau}
.
\end{align}
Note that the regressor matrix and parameter vector are arbitrary, and the parameter vector is an abstract parameter that is a combination of various physical parameters such as weight and link length, and therefore does not have a clear physical meaning.

We implemented MATLAB~\cite{matlab2024b} scripts for identification 
% using the ``lsqlin'' function, 
based on OpenSYMORO~\cite{khalil2014OpenSYMORO}. We then translated OpenSYMORO's manipulator dynamics code into C++ and implemented the controller in C++ using the identified parameters.

We used data collected by unilateral teleoperation for parameter identification.
Mathematically, the rank of the regressor matrix shouldn't decrease as much as possible.
Therefore, the input is typically designed using a target trajectory that minimizes the drop in rank as much as possible.
However, in reality, only a portion of the entire space of position, velocity, and acceleration is utilized for teleoperation, and the accuracy of the areas that rarely appear in human operations is not essential.
Teleoperated data encompasses many elements of human operation, making it one of the most efficient methods for identifying parameters of manipulator dynamics in teleoperation.
However, data from teleoperation depends on the operator and is difficult to reproduce.
Autonomous data collection for identification is one of our important future works.
The data of unilateral teleoperation are obtained at 500 Hz and downsampled to 25 Hz for parameter identification using ``resample'' function of MATLAB.

Although the inertia matrix has nondiagonal elements in the identification result, it is simplified to 0 in the controller implementation because the nondiagonal elements are typically tiny compared to the diagonal elements in practice.

\subsection{Velocity and External Force Observer}
We will explain in detail how to derive the velocity and external force observers. From equation~\ref{eq:vfob},
\begin{align}
s
\begin{bmatrix}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{0} & \bm{I} \\
\bm{0} & \bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \\
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} \\
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{I} \\
\bm{0} \\
\end{bmatrix}
\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u}
+
% \begin{bmatrix}
% 2\zeta\omega_c \bm{I} \\
% \omega_c^2 \bm{M}(\bm{\theta}) \\
% \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{H}_1 \\
\bm{H}_2 \\
\end{bmatrix}
(s\bm{\theta}-\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}})
\\
s\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} + \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u} +\bm{H}_1(s\bm{\theta}-\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}})
\\
(s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1} (\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} + \bm{\tau}_{u}) + \bm{H}_1 s\bm{\theta}
\\
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= (s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1} \left \{ \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1} (\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} + \bm{\tau}_{u}) + \bm{H}_1 s\bm{\theta} \right \}
\\
% \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}\frac{1}{s}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} + \frac{2\zeta\omega_c}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}s\bm{\theta} \qquad \left ( \ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} \coloneqq \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}(\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext}+\bm{\tau}_{u}) \right ) \\
s\tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \bm{H}_2 \left ( s\bm{\theta} - \hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} \right ) \\
&= \bm{H}_2 \left [ s\bm{\theta} - (s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1} \left \{ \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1} (\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} + \bm{\tau}_{u}) + \bm{H}_1 s\bm{\theta} \right \} \right ] \\
\left \{ s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_2(s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1}\right \} \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta}) \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \bm{H}_2 \left [ \left \{ \bm{I} - (s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1}\bm{H}_1 \right \} s\bm{\theta} - (s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1} \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u} \right ] \\
\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left \{ s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_2(s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1}\right \}^{-1} \bm{H}_2 \left [ \left \{ \bm{I}-(s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1} \bm{H}_1 \right \} s\bm{\theta} - (s\bm{I}+\bm{H}_1)^{-1} \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u} \right ]
% \hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \frac{\omega_c^2}{s^2 + 2\zeta\omega_c s + \omega_c^2} (s^2 \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\theta} - \bm{\tau}_{u})
\end{align}
where $\bm{H}_1, \bm{H}_2$ are the observer gains 
and $s$ is the Laplace variable.
If the observer gains are setted by cut-off anguler frequency $\omega_c$ and damping coefficient $\zeta$ like $\bm{H}_1 = 2\zeta\omega_c \bm{I}$ and $\bm{H}_2 = \omega_c^2 \bm{I}$,
\begin{align}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}\frac{1}{s}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} + \frac{2\zeta\omega_c}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}s\bm{\theta} \qquad \left ( \ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} \coloneqq \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}(\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext}+\bm{\tau}_{u}) \right ) \\
\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left ( s+\omega_c^2 \frac{1}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} \right )^{-1} \omega_c^2 \left \{ \left ( 1 - \frac{2\zeta\omega_c}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} \right ) s\bm{\theta} - \frac{1}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u} \right \} \\
&= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})  \frac{\omega_c^2}{s+ \frac{\omega_c^2}{s+2\zeta\omega_c}} \left ( \frac{s}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} s \bm{\theta} -  \frac{1}{s+2\zeta\omega_c} \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta}) \bm{\tau}_{u} \right ) \\
&=  \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta})\frac{\omega_c^2}{s^2 + 2\zeta\omega_c s + \omega_c^2} (s^2\bm{\theta} - \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\tau}_{u})
.
\end{align}
% The damping coefficient $\zeta$ can be adjusted, but $\zeta=1$ (double pole) is a useful option because it achieves the shortest response time without vibration, and the formula is simple.
Although the damping coefficient $\zeta$ can be adjusted, the value $\zeta=1$ (double pole) is useful because it achieves vibration-free response and the formula is simple.
If $\zeta=1$,
\begin{align}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \frac{s}{s+2\omega_c}\frac{1}{s}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} + \frac{2\omega_c}{s+2\omega_c}s\bm{\theta} \label{eq:theta_dot_hat} \\
\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} &= \bm{M}(\bm{\theta}) \left ( \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} \right) ^{2} (s^2 \bm{\theta} - \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})^{-1}\bm{\tau}_{u})
.
\label{eq:tau_ext_hat}
\end{align}
For computer implementation, equations \ref{eq:theta_dot_hat} and \ref{eq:tau_ext_hat} can be reformulated in derivative-free form.
For example,
\begin{align}
\hat{\dot{\bm{\theta}}} &= \frac{1}{s+2\omega_c}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{ref} + 2\omega_c \left ( 1-\frac{2\omega_c}{s+2\omega_c} \right ) \bm{\theta} \\
\hat{\bm{\tau}}_{ext} 
% &= \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} \left( s \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}s \bm{M}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\theta} - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\bm{\tau}_{u} \right ) \\
&=  \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left \{ \left ( \frac{\omega_c s}{s + \omega_c} \right)^2 \bm{\theta} - \left(\frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right)^2  \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\tau}_{u} \right \} \\
&= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left \{ \omega_c \left(1 - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right) \frac{\omega_c s}{s + \omega_c} \bm{\theta} - \left(\frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right)^2  \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\tau}_{u} \right \} \\
&= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left \{ \omega_c \frac{\omega_c s}{s + \omega_c} \bm{\theta} - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} \left ( \omega_c \frac{\omega_c s}{s + \omega_c} \bm{\theta} + \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta}) \bm{\tau}_{u} \right ) \right \}  \\
&= \tilde{\bm{M}}(\bm{\theta}) \left \{ \omega_c \cdot \omega_c \left(1 - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right) \bm{\theta} - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} \left ( \omega_c \cdot \omega_c \left(1 - \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right) \bm{\theta} + \left(\frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c}\right) \tilde{\bm{M}}^{-1}(\bm{\theta})\bm{\tau}_{u} \right )  \right \} 
.
\end{align}
We can implement this equation on computer if $\frac{\omega_c}{s+\omega_c}$, $\frac{2\omega_c}{s+2\omega_c}$ and $\frac{1}{s+2\omega_c}$ are discretized.
When using the bilinear transformation,
\begin{align}
y &= \frac{\omega_c}{s + \omega_c} x \qquad \left (s = \frac{2}{T} \cdot \frac{1 - z^{-1}}{1 + z^{-1}} \right) \\
&= \frac{\omega_c}{\frac{2}{T} \cdot \frac{1 - z^{-1}}{1 + z^{-1}} + \omega_c} x \\
&= \frac{\omega_c T(1 + z^{-1})}{2(1 - z^{-1}) + \omega_c T(1 + z^{-1})} x \\
&= \frac{\omega_c T(1 + z^{-1})}{(2+\omega_c T) - (2 - \omega_c T )z^{-1}} x \\
\left \{ (2+\omega_c T) - (2 - \omega_c T )z^{-1} \right\} \cdot y &= \omega_c T(1 + z^{-1}) \cdot x \\
(2+\omega_c T) \cdot y &= (2 - \omega_c T )z^{-1}\cdot y + \omega_c T(1 + z^{-1}) \cdot x \\
y &= \frac{2 - \omega_c T}{2+\omega_c T}z^{-1}\cdot y + \frac{\omega_c T}{2+\omega_c T}(1 + z^{-1}) \cdot x
\end{align}
where $T$ is the sampling period.
Similarly,
\begin{align}
y &= \frac{1}{s + \omega_c} x \qquad \left (s = \frac{2}{T} \cdot \frac{1 - z^{-1}}{1 + z^{-1}}\right) \\
y &= \frac{2 - \omega_c T}{2+\omega_c T}z^{-1}\cdot y + \frac{T}{2+\omega_c T}(1 + z^{-1}) \cdot x
.
\end{align}
Strictly speaking, we need to use pre-warping from cut-off angular frequency on the continuous space $\omega_c$ to that on the discrete space $\omega_d$ ($\omega_c = \frac{2}{T}\tan\left(\frac{\omega_dT}{2}\right)$).
However, when using a low cut-off angular frequency, they are almost the same, and it is not necessary in practice.

