Finally, level-$3$ squares can be put together to form level-$3$ polyominoes. When putting two level-$3$ squares of order $(a,b)$ together, the vector that points from the bottom corner of one level-$3$ square to the bottom corner of a neighboring level-$3$ square is one of the following two vectors:
$$\textbf{I}=(ab,(a-1)b), \textbf{J}=(-(a-1)b,ab).$$
Figure \ref{fig_d3} shows an example of a level-$3$ triomino of order $(7,2)$, which is made up of three level-$3$ squares of order $(7,2)$. The bottom corners of the three level-$3$ squares are marked by red dots, and the vectors that point from the lower red dot to the upper red dots are $(14,12)$ and $(-12,14)$.

