\begin{abstract}
% 非線形Seebeck効果は
The nonlinear Seebeck effect, nonlinear conversion of a temperature gradient into an electric current, was observed at room temperature. Based on a second-harmonic lock-in method combined with an a.c. temperature gradient, $\nabla T$, we measured a nonlinear Seebeck voltage in NiFe|Pt bilayers at $300\ \rm K$, the amplitude of which increases in proportion to $(\nabla T)^2$. We also observed that the nonlinear Seebeck voltage increases as the sample length along the $\nabla T$ direction decreases, showing a characteristic scaling law distinct from the conventional linear Seebeck effect. We developed a phenomenological model for the nonlinear Seebeck effect incorporating the spin-current induced modulation of the Seebeck coefficient, which well reproduces the experimental results.
% The nonlinear Seebeck effect, nonlinear conversion of temperature gradient into an electric current, was observed at room temperature.
% Using a recently developed technique to create AC heat current, we measure the second harmonic Seebeck signal in a junction system of a ferromagnetic metal NiFe and a heavy metal Pt.
% We observed the magnetic field, temperature gradient, sample length dependence of this nonlinear signal. Furthermore, we constructed a phenomenological model consistent with the experimental data.
% By shortening sample length, the nonlinear Seebeck signals improved to be different with linear one, consistent with the theoretical model.
\end{abstract}

