Then, ${\delta \bm \sigma}_{\rm s}$ interacts with the magnetization ${\bf M}$ in the ferromagnet, which modulates ${\delta \bm \sigma}_{\rm s}$, or the electron spin-dependent chemical potential $\mu_{\rm e}^{\uparrow(\downarrow)}$, affecting the electron conductivity \cite{USMR_1,USMR_2}. Since ${\delta \bm \sigma}_{\rm s}$ depends on the applied ${\bf E}$ direction, this leads to the nonreciprocal or nonlinear conductivity $\sigma_2$, whose sign can be modulated by the ${\bf M}$ direction. USMR has been observed in various junction systems at room temperature\cite{USMR_1,USMR_2}.
