Recently, the second-order nonlinear Nernst effect, which generates a thermoelectric voltage in proportional to the square of $\nabla T$, was observed in a bilayer film composed of a type-II superconductor and a ferromagnetic insulator\cite{arisawa_natcomm}.
This nonlinear effect, however, has been limited at an extremely low temperature $(<4\ \rm{K})$ to occur.
For the practical application, it has been eagerly awaited to explore nonlinear thermopower at room temperature.

Here, we report the observation of a nonlinear Seebeck effect at room temperature in a junction system composed of a ferromagnetic metal(FM) NiFe and a heavy metal(HM) Pt, which corresponds to the thermoelectric counterpart of the USMR.
