\begin{abstract}
% Registering airborne SAR with spaceborne optical images is crucial for SAR image interpretation and geo-localization. It is challenging for this cross-platform heterogeneous image registration due to significant geometric and radiation differences, which current methods fail to handle. To tackle these challenges, we propose a novel grid-based multimodal registration framework (Grid-Reg) across airborne and space-born platforms, including a new domain-robust descriptor extraction network, Hybrid Siamese Correlation Metric Learning Network (HSCMLNet) and a grid-based solver (Grid-solver) for transformation parameters estimation. Our Grid-Reg is based on detector-free patch correspondence rather than accurate key-point correspondence, which is inherently difficult in heterogeneous images with large geometric deformation. By Grid-Solver, our Grid-Reg estimates transformation parameters by optimizing robust global matching loss-based patch correspondences of whole images in a coarse-to-fine strategy. To robustly calculate the similarity between patches, specifically that have noise and change objects, we propose HSCMLNet, including a hybrid Siamese module to extract high-level features of multimodal images and a correlation learning module (CMLModule) based equiangular unit basis vectors (EUBVs). Moreover, we propose a manifold loss EUBVsLoss to constrain the normalized correlation between local embeddings of patches and EUBVs. Furthermore, we curate a new challenging benchmark dataset of SAR-to-optical registration using real-world UAV MiniSAR data and optical images from Google Earth. We extensively analyze factors affecting registration accuracy and compare our method with state-of-the-art techniques on this dataset, showing superior performance.
Registering airborne SAR with spaceborne optical images is crucial for SAR image interpretation and geo-localization. It is challenging for this cross-platform heterogeneous image registration due to significant geometric and radiation differences, which current methods fail to handle. To tackle these challenges, we propose a novel grid-based multimodal registration framework (Grid-Reg) across airborne and space-born platforms, including a new domain-robust descriptor extraction network, Hybrid Siamese Correlation Metric Learning Network (HSCMLNet) and a grid-based solver (Grid-solver) for transformation parameters estimation. Our Grid-Reg is based on detector-free and global matching loss rather than accurate keypoint correspondences. These accurate correspondences are inherently difficult in heterogeneous images with large geometric deformation. By Grid-Solver, our Grid-Reg estimates transformation parameters by optimizing robust global matching loss-based patch correspondences of whole images in a coarse-to-fine strategy. To robustly calculate the similarity between patches, specifically that have noise and change objects, we propose HSCMLNet, including a hybrid Siamese module to extract high-level features of multimodal images and a correlation learning module (CMLModule) based equiangular unit basis vectors (EUBVs). Moreover, we propose a manifold loss EUBVsLoss to constrain the normalized correlation between local embeddings of patches and EUBVs. Furthermore, we curate a new challenging benchmark dataset of SAR-to-optical registration using real-world UAV MiniSAR data and optical images from Google Earth. We extensively analyze factors affecting registration accuracy and compare our method with state-of-the-art techniques on this dataset, showing superior performance.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Multimodal Image Registration, Deep Learning, SAR
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{S}ynthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) is a versatile active microwave imaging sensor for various earth observation tasks which can work under all-day and all-weather conditions. SAR images reflect electromagnetic scattering characteristics of the surface, which are quite difficult for human visual perception due to speckle noise, geometric distortion and huge differences from optical images. Corresponding optical images are usually needed to provide complementary visual details for SAR image interpretation. Hence, accurate SAR-optical image registration is a vital prerequisite for tasks like multi-modal remote sensing image fusion\cite{intr_1}, change detection\cite{intr_3}, object detection and geo-localization\cite{intr_5}.
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{factors.pdf}
	\caption{\centering{Huge differences of UAV SAR and Optical Images from Google Earth}}
	\label{factors}
\end{figure}
 Recent advancements \cite{76,70,44} in multimodal remote sensing image registration concentrate on spaceborne platforms from which the images are usually geolocalized and geocoded so that the geometric differences between them are eliminated to a large extent before the registration process. Then, feature-based matching approaches are usually adopted, which require accurate keypoint matching across images to establish transformations. However, as shown in Fig. \ref{factors}, current registration methods only consider small differences, failing when there are significant geometric differences and large coverage disparities between SAR and optical reference images, especially crossing airborne SAR platforms and spaceborne ones. Compared to spaceborne platforms, it is more difficult to obtain accurate flight POS (position and orientation system) data for SAR image geographical calibration and encoding, even resulting in poorer imaging quality. It is quite challenging to register airborne SAR images and satellite optical images due to the significant appearance and geometric variations, which result from:
\begin{itemize}
\item Firstly, SAR and optical image appearance are very distinct due to the different imaging mechanisms, making it very difficult to find the common features between them for matching. Moreover, the defocusing, speckle noise and geometric distortion in airborne SAR images make it more complicated for robust common feature extraction. 
\item Secondly, the flight POS information is usually not sufficiently precise and even lost in some denied environments, limiting the calibration of geometric disparities between SAR and optical images using POS data. Large differences in geometry and coverage regions often occur. Moreover, the inaccurate POS information of the dynamic airborne platform can result in poor SAR imaging quality.
\item Thirdly, SAR and optical images, captured at different times, often show changes in land cover and land use between them. For example, in Fig. \ref{factors}, the SAR image exhibits new buildings which are absent in the corresponding optical image.
\end{itemize}
