% However, on moving airborne platforms like UAVs, SAR images often differ significantly from reference images in geometry, radiometry, and coverage, especially without precise flight attitude and GNSS data. 
Consequently, these difficulties hinder obtaining sufficient correct point correspondences between airborne SAR and spaceborne optical images. To tackle these challenges, this paper instead introduces a grid-based registration framework for heterogeneous remote sensing image registration across platforms. Our method identifies patch correspondences with a novel multimodal feature extraction network to match airborne SAR and optical image patches and then estimates transformation parameters by optimizing a global grid-matching loss in a coarse-to-fine strategy. Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a grid-based registration framework Grid-Reg that avoids fragile keypoint detection and matching from SAR and optical images with the excellent ability of transformer parameter estimation through minimizing proposed global grid-based matching loss, even in the case of significant appearance and geometric variations. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to address airborne SAR and spaceborne optical image registration at a large scale.
\item We design a novel multimodal feature extraction network HSCMLNet that integrates a hybrid siamese and a correlation metric learning module (CMLModule), which can robustly measure the similarity between patches with noise and change objects. Different from current descriptors networks, our HSCMLNet pay more attention to each local region of patches by the CMLModule and a manifold loss EUBVsLoss. CMLModule can make each local embedding contribute to patch-level descriptor. And EUBVsLoss ensure geometric and modality invariance in local embeddings while maintaining discriminative capabilities.
\item We propose a new transformation estimation method  Grid-Solver. Compared with feature-based methods that are susceptible to a large number of mismatched point pairs, Grid-Solver can robustly estimate parameters
guided by grid-based matching loss, which doesn't rely on the number of accurate correspondences.
\item Due to the lack of such multimodal remote sensing image registration datasets across platforms, we curate the first benchmark dataset with our drone-based MiniSAR platform and conduct extensive experimental analysis and comparison, demonstrating the success of our proposed registration technique.
\end{itemize}
\section{Related Work}
Multimodal remote sensing image registration is extremely important for data fusion and geo-location, \textit{etc.}, gaining significant research attention. Image registration methods can be categorized into feature-based, area-based, and deep learning methods \cite{35}.
\subsection{Feature-based Image Registration}
Feature-based methods usually involve three key steps: salient point detection, descriptor extraction and matching, and robust transformation parameters estimation. It is desired that the salient key points are invariant to modality, geometry, and perspective variations. Traditional methods rely on manually crafted features like SIFT, Harris, and SURF. Recent Deep neural networks \cite{10,11,12,49} dominate learning local descriptors for image registration, surpassing traditional methods.  Nina et.al \cite{75} used modified HardNet features to align SAR and optical images. Cnet \cite{76} introduced a feature learning network with convolutional layers and an attention module to enhance feature representation. To retain discriminative information while eliminating speckle noise in SAR images, Xiang et.al\cite{25} proposed a feature decoupling network (FDNet) that combined a residual denoising network and a pseudo-siamese fully convolutional network. To handle mismatches for parameter estimation, some robust estimation algorithms, for example RANSAC, GMS \cite{30}, VFC \cite{Ma2014RobustPM} were proposed. SuperGlue \cite{31} simultaneously matches features and removes outliers using a graph neural network-based loss function. Zhao et al. \cite{33} adopted a gravity centre coordinate system for geometrically invariant keypoint detection, achieving high-quality matching pairs with their SMFNet feature matching network. To estimate transformation parameters, Brian et al. \cite{55} proposed a method to estimate global transformation parameters by first estimating local geometric transformations to align large-scale images. Cédric et al. \cite{71} adopted entropy and mutual information to estimate parameters for low-overlap image registration.

The success of local feature-based methods largely relies on high-quality matches, which are vulnerable to significant geometric, appearance, and coverage region variances as well as image quality. Our approach eases the vulnerability of local mismatches by optimizing a global matching loss.

\subsection{Area-based Image Registration}
Area-based methods utilize entire images to estimate transformation parameters \cite{34}, differing from local feature-based methods where image feature extraction and similarity measurement are crucial. Hua et al. \cite{36} adapted mutual information to quantify similarity between different modalities. Xu et.al\cite{38} introduced a metric based on Jeffrey divergence to overcome limitations of mutual information based on KL divergence. Some methods measure the similarity in the frequency domain. Xue et.al\cite{39} proposed a frequency-based terrain feature alignment algorithm. To reduce data volume and computational costs in registration, Eldad et al. \cite{52} explored octrees. Self-similarity is also employed, for example, Hong et al. \cite{41} introduced a local self-similarity descriptor and shape similarity between objects, enhancing images resilient to radiation changes.

% Fan et.al\cite{40} combined nonlinear diffusion and spatial consistency strategies to extract features and encode multi-scale phase-consistent features with an adaptive binning spatial structure, enhancing the robustness of feature descriptions


% Fan et al. \cite{40} combined nonlinear diffusion and spatial consistency to enhance feature extraction and encode phase-consistent features at multiple scales with adaptive binning. Eldad et al. \cite{52} explored octrees to reduce data volume and computational costs in registration. Self-similarity methods are also employed, such as the local self-similarity descriptor by Hong et al. \cite{41}, which describes shape similarity between objects from multimodal images resilient to radiation changes. %Fan et al. \cite{42} integrated phase coherence into self-similarity, termed as POSS, to better encode structural information.% 

However, area-based methods are time-consuming and memory-intensive. Our approach is memory efficient by titling images into patches and computing descriptors of patches by a novel network robust against geometric and modality variations.

\subsection{End-to-End-based Image Registration}
End-to-end methods for multimodal image registration differ from traditional feature-based and area-based approaches, which directly regress transformation parameters through trained neural networks. Li et.al\cite{44} unified all matching tasks, including feature detection, feature descriptor, and parameter estimation, under an end-to-end framework through a multimodal image fusion network with a self-attention mechanism. Ye et.al\cite{43} devised a multi-scale image registration framework MU-Net based on unsupervised learning, comprising multiple stacked DNNs to predict registration parameters from coarse to fine levels. For end-to-end multimodal image registration, the choice of loss function is very important. Czolbe et.al\cite{74} proposed a new metric to measure the similarity of semantics, which is based on learned semantic features. 

% However, deep learning end-to-end methods have not yet been applied to large-scale remote sensing image registration tasks due to the limitation of memory and lack of datasets. 

However, current methods still find it very difficult to deal with large-scale image registration with large geometric and radiometric differences, especially across airborne and spaceborne platforms. Current methods for SAR and optical image registration are mainly applied for spaceborne platforms where the images are geocoded to eliminate large geometric differences. To address SAR and optical image registration across platforms, we propose a new multimodal image registration framework, Grid-Reg, for SAR and optical image registration across platforms without flight POS information. Our proposed method can not only process images of any size but also achieve higher registration accuracy. 

% reduce memory consumption by griding images and cropping them into patches. Moreover, since our method only needs to train the local descriptor learning network, the amount of training data required is greatly reduced.



% Sun et.al\cite{53} introduced an image detector-free LoFTR method to establish dense matches at both coarse and fine levels. To enhance time efficiency and reduce the algorithm's sensitivity to radiation differences,  Li et.al\cite{45} proposed a new registration framework based on template matching, capable of utilizing affine invariants to map the template to the semantic distribution position of the reference image. 


% Li et al. \cite{45} introduced a template matching-based framework to improve efficiency and reduce sensitivity to radiation differences. Ye et al. \cite{43} developed MU-Net, a multi-scale registration framework using unsupervised learning with stacked DNNs for predicting parameters across different scales. Li et al. \cite{44} unified tasks under an end-to-end multimodal image fusion network with a self-attention mechanism. The choice of loss function is crucial in these methods; Czolbe et al. \cite{74} introduced a metric based on learned semantic features to measure similarity effectively. Deep learning for large-scale image registration faces memory constraints and multimodal dataset complexity. Our method efficiently handles any image size by gridding and cropping into patches, reducing memory use. We also prioritize training the local descriptor learning network to minimize the required data.

% Current multimodal registration methods struggle with aligning large-scale images due to significant geometric and radiometric differences. SAR and optical methods for stable spaceborne platforms rely on precise attitude and GNSS data for accurate geocoding, mitigating disparities. However, UAV platforms lack stability and data precision, posing challenges. To address this, we propose Grid-Reg, a novel framework for multimodal SAR and optical image registration without relying on flight attitude and GNSS information, providing a robust solution.

\section{Methodology}
\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{overview.pdf}
\caption{\centering{Overview of Grid-Based Multimodal Image Registration Framework(Grid-Reg), which includes three parts: preprocessing, HSCMLNet trained by manifold and contrative loss, and Grid-Solver.
The detail of CMLModule and Grid-Solver are shown in Fig. \ref{metric_network} and Fig. \ref{got}}}
\label{overview_fig}
\end{figure*}
In Fig. \ref{overview_fig}, our grid-based image registration framework is detector-free and comprises two main components: the multimodal feature extraction network (HSCMLNet) and the grid-based transformation parameter solver (Grid-Solver). SAR and optical images are initially cropped into patches $P_S \in \mathbb{R}^{N_P^S \times H^P \times W^P}$ and $P_O \in \mathbb{R}^{N_P^O \times H^P \times W^P}$ using a predefined sliding window $H^P \times W^P$ with a fixed step size. HSCMLNet extracts descriptors $F_P^S \in \mathbb{R}^{N_P^S \times C}$ and $F_P^O \in \mathbb{R}^{N_P^O \times C}$ from SAR and optical patches, and compute their similarity. Grid-Solver then estimates optimal transform parameters $T_{optimal}$ by iteratively minimizing the matching loss $L_curr$, which globally evaluates patch similarity, contrasting with local nearest neighbour matches in traditional methods. 

% This approach effectively addresses a large number of local mismatches. %In subsequent sections, we detail HSCMLNet's network architecture in Subsection \ref{subsectionA} and introduce Grid-Solver in Subsection \ref{subsectionB}.
% The hybrid Siamese module can not only overcome the problem of large differences in low-level features between SAR and optical images but also reduce network parameters.
\subsection{Hybrid Siamese Correlation Metric Learning Network}\label{subsectionA}
In Fig. \ref{overview_fig}(B), HSCMLNet includes the hybrid siamese module (HSModule) and correlation metric learning module (CMLModule). Due to large radiometric differences between SAR and optical images, HSModule uses two pseudo-siamese encoders and a shared encoder to extract features from optical and SAR patches separately. The resulting feature maps are input through CMLModule to generate final descriptors $F_P^S$ and $F_P^O$ for SAR and optical patches. Traditional patch-level descriptor learning methods consider the same patch and its augmented version as positive samples and different as negative samples. The feature networks are then trained by forcing the positive patch similarities to exceed those of the hardest negative samples by a fixed margin. This may lead to descriptors being overly restricted to specific local regions of the patch and failing to effectively capture discriminative features that are essential for accurate matching. As a result, the similarity measurement is very fragile, particularly when the patches are noisy or object-changing. To address these issues, for each patch, our CMLModule firstly input the high-level feature map $F$ into $\phi$(shown in Fig. \ref{metric_network}) to obtain local embeddings $E$,  which are utilized to compute the patch-level descriptor $G$. Additionally, we employ EUVBsLoss to constrain local embeddings in the space defined by equiangular unit basis vectors (EUBVs), promoting geometric and modality invariance. By doing so, our approach can enhance the robustness of learned patch-level descriptors $G$ against significant geometric and radiometric variations.
\subsubsection{Hybrid Siamese Module(HSModule)}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{HSNet1.pdf}
\caption{\centering{The architecture of hybrid siamese module. SAR and optical encoders have similar architecture. Convblock1 and Convblock2 are residual convolution block\cite{He2015DeepRL}}}
\label{HSNet}
\end{figure}
Due to the different imaging mechanisms between SAR and optical sensors, SAR and optical patches exhibit significant differences in low-level features. It is important to note that SAR and optical patches covering the same geographical area inherently contain identical objects, thereby sharing common high-level features. To leverage this, we adopted a hybrid Siamese module to obtain features of SAR and optical patches in a common domain\cite{24, 69}. As shown in Fig. \ref{HSNet}, we design a new hybrid siamese module, which combines two pseudo-siamese encoder $En^{S}$ $En^{O}$ to extract low-level features and a shared siamese encoder $En^{Sh}$ to obtain feature $F_{SAR},F_{Opt} \in R^{B\times C_F \times H_F \times W_F}$ of SAR and optical patches respectively.
\subsubsection{\textbf{Correlation Metric Learning Module(CMLModule)}}
\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{metric_network.pdf}
\caption{\centering{The overview of Correlation Metric Learning Module based Local Manifold Equivalence Constraint(CMLModule)}}
\label{metric_network}
\end{figure*}
To compute the patch-level descriptor and similarity robustly, we developed a novel correlation metric learning module named CMLModule, depicted in Fig. \ref{metric_network}. Unlike previous methods that mainly emphasize local embeddings for distinguishing positive patches from the most challenging negative ones, our CMLModule extracts holistic patch-level descriptors using the local embeddings of $F_{SAR}$ and $F_{Opt}$ for robust matching. We employ equiangular unit basis vectors (EUBVs) representation for local embedding and descriptor computation, which has been shown to improve performance for metric learning more than fully connected classifiers \cite{7,73}. EUBVs $V \in \mathbb{R}^{N_A \times C_V}$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
V = L2Norm(I_{N_A}-1_{N_A\times N_A}/N_A)
\end{equation}
where L2Norm() computes the 2-norm of the row vectors, $I_{N_A}$ is identity diagonal matrix with $N_A$ dimensions, and $1_{N_A\times N_A}$ is a all-ones matrix with the size of $N_A\times N_A$. The angle between any two unit row vectors of $V$ is equal and close to 90 degrees. Therefore, for each patch, we can use EUBVs to represent the patch-level local descriptor based on the normalized similarity between each local embedding and $V$. As shown in Fig. \ref{metric_network}, we first adopt a shared conv block $\phi$ to obtain the local embedding $E$, which can reduce the resolution of the feature map to decrease memory cost. Then, $E$ is utilized to obtain the patch-level descriptor $G$. The local embeddings of $F_{SAR}$ and $F_{Opt}$: $E_{SAR}$ and $E_{Opt}$ are first input into the correlation coefficient calculation block(Fig. \ref{metric_network}(c)) to derive the normalized
correlation coefficients $A \in R^{N_L \times N_A }$ between $E$ and $V$, which is given by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq_A}
\centering
A = \frac{exp(\frac{EV^{T}}{\tau_A})}{\sum_{i=1}^{i=N_A}exp(\frac{Ev_i^T}{\tau_A})}
\end{equation}   
where $\tau_A$ represents the scale factor, with smaller values indicating greater attention to the most relevant EUBV. Then, $A$ is utilized to obtain the patch-level descriptors $G$ through the Patch-Des Block(Fig. \ref{metric_network}(d)), which combines EUBVs based on average normalized correlation coefficients. 
As shown in Fig. \ref{metric_network}(e), for $j$ patch, the $G$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
    G = \sum_{i=1}^{N_A} g_iv_i
\end{equation}
where $g=\{g_0,g_1,g_2,...,g_i,...g_{N_A}\}$ is calculated by:
\begin{equation}
    g = \frac{1}{H_EW_E}\sum_{i=1}^{H_EW_E}A(j,i,:)
\end{equation}
where $A$ has been reshaped as $B\times H_EW_E\times N_A$.
\subsubsection{\textbf{Manifold Loss(EUBVsLoss)}} 
To guarantee that $G$ is more robust against large geometric and modality change, the $E\in R^{N_L \times C_V}$  is required to be local geometric and modality invariant by satisfying the following constraints:
\begin{equation}
\label{e3}
\begin{array}{l}
E_{SAR} \approx E_{Opt}^{\prime} = FN(\phi(T_{OS}{F_{Opt}}))\\
E_{Opt} \approx  E_{SAR}^{\prime} = FN(\phi(T_{SO}{F_{SAR}}))
\end{array}
\end{equation}  
where $N_L$ is the number of local embeddings in each mini-batch, $C_V$ is the dimension of each local embedding, $T_{OS}$ and $T_{SO}$ are the geometric transforms that can match $I_O$ to $I_S$ and match $I_S$ to $I_O$, respectively. $FN$ represents the flatten and L2 Normalization operators. To facilitate that $E$ satisfies the constraints Equation~(\ref{e3}), we design a novel 
loss function EUBVsLoss to constraint cross-modality consistency of local embeddings. This function regulates the position of each local embedding on the unit sphere by aggregating local embeddings for the reconstruction of fixed EUBVs. Compared to other loss functions for descriptors learning networks, EUBVsLoss offers several advantages. Firstly, by guiding the position of local embeddings on a sphere space constructed by EUBVs, we ensure that local embeddings are evenly distributed on the unit sphere, effectively leveraging the expressive power of the unit sphere space. Secondly, this loss function operates without the need for additional supervised information concerning local embeddings. Lastly, unlike other loss functions, our approach individually regulates each non-corresponding local embedding, reducing the risk of treating repeated regions as negative samples.

% In the realm of manifold learning, Locally Linear Embedding (LLE) reconstructs each sample by leveraging its local neighbouring samples to preserve the topology of the feature space. Based on this idea, we utilize local embeddings to reconstruct the EUBVs based on the combination coefficients of cross-modality corresponding local embeddings. 

On the one hand, to preserve the relation between the local embeddings $E$ and the EUBVs $V$ across modality, we  attempt to reconstruct EUBVs $V$ by the local embedding $E$ of one modality and the normalized coefficients $B$ of local embeddings of another modality. Given EUBVs $V$, the local embeddings $E_1$ in one modality are used to reconstruct the EUBVs $\hat{V}^{C}$ based on the coefficients $B_2$ of another modality $E_2$ by 

% Firstly, to make the  correlation coefficients $B\in R^{N_A\times N_L}$ between $V$ and $E$ of different modality be consistent, As shown in Fig. \ref{metric_network}(la), the reconstructed $\hat{V^C}(E_1,E_2))$ is obtained by:
\begin{equation}
\centering
\label{e_al}
\hat{V^C}(E_1,E_2) = L2Norm(E_1^TB_2)
\end{equation}
where $B_2$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
B_2 = \frac{exp(\frac{VE_2^T}{\tau_B})}{\sum_{j=1}^{j=N_L}exp(\frac{Ve_j^T}{\tau_B^C})}
\end{equation}  
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{led1.pdf}
\caption{A EUVB is reconstructed by local embeddings of one modality(a) and two modalities(b) with coefficients calculated from the corresponding local embeddings of the other modality.}\label{LED1}
\end{figure}

On the other hand, to make $E$ be able to reconstruct $V$, we need to promote that $E$ distribution in the space formed by EUBVs. Therefore, we utilize the $V$ to reconstruct $E$ given by:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{E} = L2Norm(AV)
\end{equation}
Meanwhile, to perverse the relation between $\hat{E}, E$ and $V$, we adopt $\hat{E}$ and the coefficients of local embedings(or reconstructed local embeddings) from another modality to reconstruct $V$ by the Equation~(\ref{e_al}).
Therefore, the cross-modality consistency EUBVsLoss $L_M^C$ is formulated as:
\begin{align}
    \begin{aligned}
        L_M^C = - \sum_{(E_1,E_2)\in S} &V\hat{V^C}^T(E_1,E_2) + V\hat{V^C}^T(E_2,E_1)\\
        &V\hat{V^C}^T(\hat{E_1},E_2) + V\hat{V^C}^T(E_2,\hat{E_1})\\
        &V\hat{V^C}^T(E_1,\hat{E_2})) + V\hat{V^C}^T(\hat{E_2},E_1)\\
        &V\hat{V^C}^T(\hat{E_1},\hat{E_2}) + V\hat{V^C}^T(\hat{E_2},\hat{E_1})\\
    \end{aligned}
\end{align}
where $S$ is the set of $\{(E_{SAR},E_{Opt}^{\prime}),(E_{SAR}^{\prime},E_{Opt})\}$

As depicted in Fig. \ref{LED1}(a), each fixed equiangular unit basis vector is reconstructed using neighboring local embeddings from one modality and the normalized correlation between the EUBVs and the corresponding local embeddings from the other modality. Consequently, $L_M^C$ not only disperses the local embeddings across the unit sphere but also penalizes inconsistency correlation between the corresponding local embeddings. For example, while the first embedding may be relatively close to a certain EUBV compared to other embeddings of same modality, its corresponding embedding from another modality may be farther away from the EUBV than other embeddings of that modality.

Moreover, to regulate the position of each local embedding relative to embeddings from both modalities in EUBVs space, we utilize corresponding embeddings $E_1(\hat{E_1})$ and $E_2(\hat{E_2})$ from two different modalities to jointly reconstruct EUBVs. The reconstruction coefficients $B_2^{1}$ are associated with the relative distance between the corresponding local embedding and EUBVs. As illustrated in Fig. \ref{metric_network}(lb), the reconstructed $\hat{V^J}(E_1,E_2)$ is obtained as follows:
 \begin{equation}
\hat{V^J}(E_1,E_2) = L2Norm((E_1^{2})^TB_2^{1})
\end{equation}   
where $E_1^2$ is the concatenation of $E_1$ and $E_2$, $E_2^1$ is likewise, and $B_2^1$ is the joint normalize coefficients given by:
\begin{equation}
B_2^1 = \frac{exp(\frac{V(E_2^1)^T}{\tau_B^J})}{\sum_{j=1}^{j=2N_L}exp(\frac{Ve_j^T}{\tau_B^J})}
\end{equation}  
Thus, the joint multimodal consistency EUBVsLoss $L_M^J$ is calculated as:
\begin{align}
    \begin{aligned}
        L_M^J = -\sum_{(E_1,E_2)\in S} & V(\hat{V^J}(E_1,E_2))^T + V(\hat{V^J}(\hat{E_1},E_2))^T\\
        & V(\hat{V^J}(E_1,\hat{E_2}))^T + V(\hat{V^J}(\hat{E_1},\hat{E_2}))^T
    \end{aligned}
\end{align}
As depicted in Fig. \ref{LED1}(b), each fixed EUBV is reconstructed by neighboring local embeddings of two modalities jointly. Different from $L_M^C$, $L_M^J$ utilizes normalized coefficients calculated between $V$ and local embeddings from both SAR and optical. Therefore, $L_M^J$ can prevent inconsistencies in the positions of corresponding embeddings in the multimodal domain that contains local embeddings from both modalities. For instance, while the blue second local embedding is close to the center EUBV, its corresponding embedding is far from this EUBV.
\subsubsection{Contrastive Loss}
To enhance the discriminability of the patch-level descriptors, we utilize contrastive loss to widen the gap between negative samples and narrow the gap between positive samples in the feature space. The contrastive loss for $G_{S}$ and $G_{O}$ is calculated as:
\begin{equation}
L_{C} = \sum_{i=1}^{i=B}-\ln(\frac{exp(G_{S}^{i}(G_{O}^{i})^T)/\tau_C)}{Z})
\end{equation}
where $Z$ is defined as:
\begin{align}
\begin{aligned}
Z &= exp(G_{S}^{i}(G_{O}^{i})^T)/\tau_C)\\
&+\sum_{j \ne i} exp(G_{S}^{j}(G_{O}^{i})^T+\alpha)/\tau_C)+ exp(G_{O}^{j}(G_{O}^{i})^T+\alpha)/\tau_C)\\
&+exp(G_{S}^{j}(G_{S}^{i})^T+\alpha)/\tau_C)+ exp(G_{O}^{i}(G_{S}^{j})^T+\alpha)/\tau_C)
\end{aligned}
\end{align}  

The loss $L$ for training our proposed HSCMLNet is the sum of the $L_M^1$, $L_M^2$ and $L_C$is given by:
\begin{equation}
L = L_M^C+L_M^J+L_C
\end{equation} 
\subsection{Grid-based Optimal Transformation Solving Strategy} \label{subsectionB}
Due to the significant radiation and geometric differences between UAV SAR and optical images, feature-based methods often struggle to identify repeatable keypoints and obtain accurate nearest matching, which lead to a high number of outlier matches. Consequently, accurate registration using feature-based image matching algorithms becomes challenging. Inspired by \cite{66} that utilize global measure to match semantic, we propose our Grid-Solver to estimate transformation by minimizing global matching loss. Compared to other methods, Grid-Solver offers three distinct advantages. Firstly, it eliminates the reliance on keypoint detection by employing a sliding window to gridize the image. Secondly, our Grid-Solver utilizes a global matching loss to assess the similarity of the transformed source image and target image, thereby mitigating the impact of falsely matched points. Thirdly, we propose a coarse-to-fine optimization strategy, enabling the rapid and robust determination of optimal transformations through the minimization of matching loss.
\subsubsection{Gridizing Image}
As shown in Fig. \ref{overview_fig}(A), we crop the image using a sliding window to create patches. Each patch center serves as a grid point with a corresponding descriptor. This yields $N_P^S$ SAR patches and $N_P^O$ optical patches, where $N_P^S$ is computed as:
\begin{equation}
N_P^S = \left( \frac{H^S - H^P/2}{\text{Step}} \right) \times \left( \frac{W^S - W^P/2}{\text{Step}} \right)
\end{equation}
where $H^P$ and $W^P$ are the height and width of each patch, respectively, and $\text{Step}$ denotes the sliding window's step size. With a SAR and optical image resolution of 0.51m, we use $H^P = W^P = 256$, ensuring each patch adequately covers a medium-sized building.
\subsubsection{Obtaining the Set of Matched Grid Point Pairs}
As shown in Fig. \ref{overview_fig}, after gridizing, the obtained patches are fed into the pretrained network to acquire patch-level representations. The cosine similarity is then employed to calculate the distance between SAR  \( F_S^{P} \) and optical patch descriptors \( F_O^{P} \), yielding the distance matrix \( D \):
\begin{equation}
D = -F_S^{P}{(F_O^{P})}^T
\end{equation}
where \( D \in [-1, 1]^{N_S^P \times N_O^P} \), and if \( F_S^{P} \) and \( F_O^{P} \) are not 2-norm normalized, we need to normalize their representations. Next, we sort each row of \( D \) in ascending order. Finally, according to the sorting results, for each grid point $(x_i^s,y_i^s)$ in SAR image, we find $K_c$ and $K_f$ neighbor grid points in optical images to obtain corresponding grid point set $P_c$ and $P_f$, respectively. As the coverage of the reference image increases, similar structures become more prevalent. We define $K_c$ and $K_f$ to be proportional to the SAR and optical coverage area ratios, which are given by:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
K_c = K\sqrt{\frac{H^O\times W^O}{H^S\times W^S}}\frac{Step}{16} ,
K_f = 4K\sqrt{\frac{H^O\times W^O}{H^S\times W^S}}\frac{Step}{16}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
In the next step, $P_c$ calculates the coarse optimal transformation, and $P_f$ refines these initial parameters. Setting $P_f$ larger than $P_c$ accelerates the search for the correct initial estimate and provides more precise local matching correspondences for refining the coarse estimate in the subsequent stage.
\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{got.pdf}
\caption{\centering{The flowchart of solving global optimal transformation by minimizing grid matching loss. The local and global refinement stages are the same, except for the set of some parameters.}}
\label{got}
\end{figure*}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Estimating Global Optimal Transformation by Minimizing Grid Matching Loss}}
As shown in Fig. \ref{got}, we iteratively minimize the grid matching loss $L_{min}$ to find the optimal transformation $T_{\text{optimal}}$. Each iteration involves randomly selecting three point pairs $p_3$ from $P_c$. Utilizing the equal resolution of SAR and optical images allows us to apply the equal area constraint\cite{Li2012RSCJRS} to discard abnormal correspondences. Specifically, the areas of triangles formed by three points from the SAR image $s_1$ and the optical image $s_2$ must satisfy that $s_{th}^1<=\frac{s_1}{s_2}<=s_{th}^2$.
If $p_3$ conforms to this constraint, we use $p_3$ to calculate the coarse transform matrix $\hat{T}$.
Then, we need to certain whether $\hat{T}$ are the optimal transformation parameters. Traditional feature-based methods usually assess the transformation by calculating the number of matched points that fit the estimated parameters, but this method is susceptible to mismatches. To evaluate the parameters more robustly, we propose a new grid-based matching loss, which can globally measure the performance of the estimated transformation. For each grid point $(x_i^s, y_i^s)$ in the source grid, the index $i^o$ of the corresponding grid point $(x_{i^o}^o, y_{i^o}^o)$ in the target grid is calculated as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
(\hat{x}_i^s,\hat{y}_i^s)=\hat{T}(x_i^s,y_i^s,1.0)\\
i_x^o = clip(round((\hat{x}_i^s-W^P//2)/Step),0,N_W^O-1)\\
i_y^o = clip(round((\hat{y}_i^s-H^P//2)/Step),0,N_H^O-1)\\
i^o = i_y^o \times N_W^O + i_x^o
\end{array}
\end{equation}
After that, the loss of aligning the source grid to the optical grid using the current estimation parameters is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\label{e8}
L_{curr} = \sum_{i=1}^{i=N_P^S}D(i,i^o)
\end{equation}
Next, we decide whether to perform local refinement by determining if $L_{{curr}}$ is not greater than the set threshold $L_{\text{th}}$.

In the local refinement stage, for each iteration, we first obtain a subset of matches, $P_f'$, from $P_f$, as given by:
\begin{equation}
P_f^{\prime} = \{(p_s,p_o)|(p_s,p_o) \in P_f , and , 1<||p_o-\hat{T}(p_s,1.0)||_2 \leq r_l\}
\end{equation}
 where $p_s$ is the grid point of SAR image, and $p_o$ is its corresponding grid point of optical image. Then,we add three matches $p_3^f$ from $P_f^{\prime}$ to $P_{curr}$, and obtain $P_{curr}^{\prime}$ that is used to estimate $\hat{T}^{\prime}$ and calculate matching loss $L_{curr}^{\prime}$. if $L_{curr}^{\prime}$ less than $L_{curr}$, we update that:
 \begin{equation}
     L_{curr} = L_{curr}^{\prime},P_{curr} = P_{curr}^{\prime}, \hat{T} = \hat{T}^{\prime}
 \end{equation}

 After the local refinement, if $L_{curr}$ less than $L_{min}$, we update that:
 \begin{align}
 \label{e_update}
     \begin{aligned}
     &L_{min} = L_{curr}, l_{th} = L_{curr} + \rho\\
     &P_{optimal} = P_{curr}, T_{optimal} = \hat{T} 
     \end{aligned}
 \end{align}

Then start the next iteration, by iterating the above steps and update Equation~(\ref{e_update}), estimated optimal transformation $T_{\text{optimal}}$ is optimized.
Finally, global refinement, similar to local refinement, is implemented to adjust $T_{\text{optimal}}$ to ensure its optimality.

\section{Experiments and Results}
\subsection{Experiment Dataset}
Due to the lack of publicly available airborne SAR and spaceborne optical image registration datasets, we constructed a benchmark dataset with drone-based MiniSAR and optical images from Google Earth. Our dataset includes 29 image subsets, each containing SAR images and multiple optical images captured at different times. In our dataset, the ground-truth affine transformations are computed by manually annotating control points for each image pair. 17 image subsets are chosen for training and the remaining for testing, ensuring no overlap between the two sets. Due to differences in imaging mechanisms and viewpoints, there are nonlinear distortions between SAR and optical images that cannot be calibrated by affine transformation. To ensure evaluation accuracy, we selected 12 image subset with better affine correction for the test set. 
%In practical applications, accurately registering large-scale images is time-consuming and labor-intensive, especially in regions with nonlinear deformation. Therefore, inaccurately registered images in the training set can assess method robustness against noise.  In addition, in practice, especially for large-scale images, we cannot guarantee that there is only affine transformation between images. The usual method is to solve the affine transformation first and then solve the nonlinear transformation. Therefore, some image pairs with nonlinear transformations can test the performance of different methods for solving affine transformation in this common application scenario with nonlinear distortion.
