On one hand, to demonstrate our effectiveness, we compare our method with leading descriptor learning methods:
\begin{itemize}
\item HardNet \cite{10} emphasizes distance between positive and hardest negative samples, using a dual sample approach \cite{47} to prevent over-fitting.
\item SosNet \cite{11} employs First Order Similarity Loss and Second Order Similarity Regularization (SOSR).
\item HyNet \cite{9} enhances local patch matching by focusing on relative distances among descriptors.
\item SDGMNet \cite{49} improves triplet loss with dynamic gradient modulation techniques.
\end{itemize}
To receive input patches of size $256\times256$, their network architectures(Fig .\ref{L2Net}(a)) are modified as shown in Fig.\ref{L2Net}(b). Since the input of L2Net is a grayscale image, we convert the RGB optical image into a grayscale image and adopt the siamese architecture to extract the features of SAR and optical patches.  

To verify the effectiveness of our proposed Grid-Solver, we compared Grid-Solver with the feature-based parameter estimation method (Feat-Solver), which utilizes ORB to detect keypoints and employs RANSAC to estimation affine transform parameters. For a fair comparison, we also apply the constraint of $s_{th}^1<=\frac{s_1}{s_2}<=s_{th}^2$ for the Feat-Slover. Specifically, the area constraint is applied after the 'random sampling' step and before the 'model estimation' step of the RANSAC algorithm. If the area constraint is not satisfied, the model estimation is not performed, effectively eliminating abnormal matches.
