\subsection{Implement Details}
\subsubsection{Training}
We adopt Adam with learning rate $2.5e^{-4}$ as optimizer to train networks. The batch size is set 512. We apply data augmentation of random rotation and flip for  SAR and optical patches. All networks are trained using the same training parameters and data augmentation.
\subsubsection{Testing}
The number of iterations is a crucial factor influencing the performance of the algorithm. As the coverage area of the reference image expands, the difficulty of registration increases. Therefore, we set the number of iterations $ITER$ as $ITER = \beta \times \frac{H^O\times W^O+H^S\times W^S}{2.0}$, where $\beta$ is used to adjust $ITER$ to balance registration accuracy and efficiency, and set $\beta=1.0$ and $\beta=2.0$ for Grid-Solver and Feat-Solver respectively. For Grid-Solver, we set $Step$ as 16. In refinement stage, we set the $r_l$ and $r_g$ set as $min\{4 \times Step,100\}$ and $min\{6 \times Step, 150\}$ respectively. And we set $ITER_f^l$ and $ITER_f^g$ as 200 and 20000 respectively.
For area constraint, we set $s_1$ and $s_2$ as $10/14$ and $14/10$, respectively.
\subsection{Metrics}
To evaluate the performance of image registration task on our dataset, we utilize the success rate\cite{67,24,69} to robustly evaluate different methods. Specifically, the success rate is defined by:
\begin{equation}
Reg_{sr}^{th} = \frac{N_{success}}{N_{images}}
\end{equation}
where $N_{images}$ is the number of image pairs in testset, and $N_{success}$ is the number of the registrated image pairs. In our dataset, the aligned image pairs need satisfy that  median error(MEE) are less than or equal to th pixels. Since different tasks require different registration accuracy standards, we set $th$ to 25, 50, 75, and 100 to evaluate the success rate of registration by different methods.
The MEE is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \label{rmse}
    MEE = \text{median} \{ || \hat{T}(x_i^s, y_i^s,1.0) - T(x_i^s, y_i^s,1.0) )||_2 \}_{i=1}^{N_p^k}
\end{equation}
where $(x_i^s,y_i^s) \in \{(x_i^s,y_i^s)|((x_i^s,y_i^s)\in I^s \& T(x_i^s,y_i^s)\in I^t)\}$
,$I^s$ is source image, $I^t$ is target image, $N_p^K$ is the number of pixels in SAR image of $k$th test pair, $T$ and $\hat{T}$ are the ground-truth and estimated transform parameters respectively.
