\subsection{Discussion}
The Step of sliding window $Step$ and the number of iterations for estimating transformation $ITER$ are parameters related to time and memory consumption.
In this subsection, we will discuss the impact of these two parameters on the registration performance.
\subsubsection{The Variation of Step}
We compare our method with other methods that use HSModule under $Step=16,32,64,128$. 
Fig. \ref{Step} shows that as the step size increases, the MEE of various methods also increases.
But compared with others, our method can retain better results for step sizes up to 96. Particularly, when the step size is 64 or less, our method significantly outperforms other methods, demonstrating its superior balance between 
accuracy and computational efficiency.
\subsubsection{The combined variation of Step and ITER}
Fig. \ref{ITERSTEP} shows the impact of reducing the iteration numbers on the performance of our method for step sizes of 16, 32, and 64. When $\beta \geq 0.1$, decreasing $\beta$ only slightly reduces our method's registration success rate. Our approach is more sensitive to iteration numbers at $L_2$ than at $L_{-1}$, due to the larger search range required for accurate transformation in larger RSO. For $\beta \in [2^0, 2^{-2}]$, decreasing $\beta$ moderately reduces the registration success rate of our method, indicating a balance between accuracy and time efficiency within this range. Different step sizes show varying impacts of $\beta$ reduction on success rate. At $Step = 16$, $Reg_{sr}^{20}$ remains above 20\% even at $\beta = 2^{-8}$. Conversely, at $Step = 64$, $Reg_{sr}^{20}$ drops below 10\% at $\beta = 2^{-2}$. This highlights the need to optimize time and memory consumption based on specific application requirements.
\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we propose a new grid-based multimodal image registration 
framework(Grid-Reg) for UAV SAR and Google optical image registration in large-scale complex scenes.
Firstly, in this framework, we propose a new local descriptor learning network, HSCMLNet, to extract features for SAR and optical patches.
In HSCMLNet, we adopt a hybrid siamese module(HSModule) to extract high-level features. Meanwhile, to robustly measure the similarity between image patches with large geometric and radiometric differences, 
we introduce a new metric learning module, CMLModule, and train it using the proposed EUBVsLoss combined with contrastive loss. Finally, we propose a novel Grid-based optimal parameter finding strategy(Grid-Solver), 
which estimates the affine transformation by minimizing the proposed matching loss. We compared our method(Grid-Reg) with others using a newly constructed multimodal large-scale complex scene dataset.
We validated the effectiveness of Grid-Solver, HSCMLNet, CMLModule  and loss functions through both quantitative and qualitative comparisons. The experimental results demonstrate that our proposed method consistently outperforms 
the alternatives, particularly in scenarios characterized by numerous changing regions and significant noise presence.
