We can repeat the  procedure in \cite{torus} (and reviewed in the introduction of the present paper). This is technically simpler in the present paper, since the pinching step is now irrelevant, and it suffices to add only three lozenges to the surface $S$ in order to obtain a compact space $V$ with universal cover the Brady complex (however, the connexion to surgery is lost, and one cannot recover, for instance, a result such as Theorem 1.3 in \cite{torus}).

