\title{Spectra of high-dimensional sparse random geometric graphs}

\author{Yifan Cao}
\address{Department of Operations Research and Financial Engineering, Princeton University }
\email{yc0570@princeton.edu}
\author{Yizhe Zhu}
\address{Department of Mathematics, University of Southern California}
\email{yizhezhu@usc.edu}



%\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze the spectral properties of the high-dimensional random geometric graph  $\mathcal G(n, d, p)$,  formed by sampling  $n$ i.i.d vectors $\{v_i\}_{i=1}^{n}$ uniformly on a $d$-dimensional unit sphere and connecting each pair $\{i,j\}$ whenever  $\langle v_i, v_j \rangle \geq \tau$ so that $p=\mathbb P(\langle v_i,v_j\rangle \geq \tau)$. This model defines a nonlinear random matrix ensemble with dependent entries.  We show that if  $d =\omega( np\log^{2}(1/p))$ and $np\to\infty$,  the limiting spectral distribution of the normalized adjacency matrix $\frac{A}{\sqrt{np(1-p)}}$  is the semicircle law.  To  our knowledge, this is the first such result for $\mathcal G(n, d, p)$ in the sparse regime. In the constant sparsity case $p=\alpha/n$, we further show that if $d=\omega(\log^2(n))$  the limiting spectral distribution of $A$ in $\mathcal G(n,d,\alpha/n)$ coincides with that of the Erd\H{o}s-R\'{e}nyi graph $\mathcal G(n,\alpha/n)$. 

Our approach combines the classical moment method in random matrix theory  with a novel recursive decomposition of closed-walk graphs, leveraging block‐cut trees and ear decompositions,  to control $\mathbb E \tr (A^k)$. A refined high trace analysis further yields a near-optimal bound on the second eigenvalue  when $np=\Omega(\log^4 (n))$, removing technical conditions previously imposed in \cite{liu2023local}.
\end{abstract}





\section{Introduction}



Random geometric graphs arise naturally in modeling complex systems with latent spatial or geometric structures \cite{penrose2003random,liu2023probabilistic}, such as wireless communication networks \cite{mao2012connectivity}, biological networks \cite{higham2008fitting}, and social graphs \cite{hoff2002latent}. In a random geometric graph, each node is assigned a random position in a geometric space, and edges are formed based on pairwise geometric proximity, inducing intricate dependencies between edges. These dependencies distinguish random geometric graphs from classical random graph models like Erdős–Rényi graphs, where edges are included independently. 


Let $\bv_1,\dots,\bv_n\in \R^d$ be $n$ independent random vectors uniformly distributed on the $(d-1)$-dimensional unit sphere $\mathbb S^{d-1}$. The  \textit{random geometric graph} $\mathcal G(n, d, p)$ is constructed by adding edges between two vertices $i$ and $j$ if their corresponding vectors satisfy $\langle \bv_i, \bv_j \rangle \geq \tau$. The threshold parameter $\tau$ is chosen such that for $i\not=j$, 
  $ \E \1(\langle \bv_i, \bv_j \rangle \geq \tau)=p$. 
This implies the edge density of $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ is the same as the Erd\H{o}s-R\'{e}nyi graph $\mathcal G(n,p)$.  







A compelling aspect of this model is that the graph structure emerges from a geometric representation of the vertices. This makes it particularly suitable for modeling applications in data science, where network nodes are naturally described by feature vectors in a $d$-dimensional space, and edges reflect similarity in features \cite{racz2017basic}. Additionally, the geometric random graph exhibits the \textit{triangle-closing property} \cite{dall2002random}, the phenomenon that “a friend of a friend is also likely to be a friend.” This arises from the inherent dependency among edges: if two nodes are each close to a common neighbor in the geometric space, then they are more likely to be close to each other as well, making the formation of triangles more probable. Such edge correlations are absent in classical Erdős–Rényi graphs, but are crucial for capturing clustering behaviors observed in real-world networks.







In this paper, our primary objective is to investigate the limiting spectral distribution and the second eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix associated with $\mathcal G(n, d, p)$, and to determine under what conditions it resembles the spectral properties of  $\mathcal G(n, p)$. Understanding the spectral properties of random geometric graphs, particularly in high dimensions and sparse regimes, is crucial for advancing both theoretical and applied research in network science, high-dimensional statistics, and random matrix theory.




\subsection*{Testing high-dimensional geometry}
Recent advances in statistics and learning theory highlight the growing importance of high-dimensional feature spaces. In this context, the random geometric graph model $\mathcal{G}(n, p, d)$ has become a canonical model.  \cite{devroye2011high} first showed  that  that for fixed $n$ and $d \to \infty$, $\mathcal{G}(n, p, d)$ and   $\mathcal{G}(n, p)$ become indistinguishable in total variation distance. When $n,d\to\infty$,  with constant $p$, \cite{bubeck2016testing}  identified a sharp phase transition at $d \asymp n^3$ in the total variation distance between the two models.  In the sparse regime $p=\frac{\alpha}{n}$ for a constant $\alpha>0$,  the same authors proved showed that the total variation distance between the two distributions goes to $1$ if $d=o(\log^3(n))$ and they also conjectured a a critical window at $d\asymp \log^3(n)$.  This conjecture was nearly solved by  \cite{liu2022testing}, who showed that indistinguishability holds when $d=\Omega(\log^{36}(n))$.

Subsequent works have extended these testing thresholds to varied settings.
\cite{baguley2025testing} used Edgeworth‐style expansions to analyze random toroidal graphs. \cite{bangachev2024fourier,bangachev2025sandwiching} investigated low‐degree polynomial complexity and the sandwiching coupling for comparing random geometric and Erdős–Rényi graphs. \cite{brennan2024threshold} determined thresholds for anisotropic latent geometries, and  \cite{li2023spectral,mao2024impossibility} studied the recovery of the latent inner product  $\langle \bv_i, \bv_j\rangle$, locating a  threshold at $d\asymp np \log(1/p)$.






While the total variation distance gives one angle of comparison, another perspective comes from spectral graph theory.  Since Wigner’s semicircle law, it has been known that for the Erdős–Rényi model  $\mathcal{G}(n, p)$, the empirical eigenvalue distribution of its adjacency matrix converges to the semicircle law \cite{tran2013sparse}. This raises the question:
\begin{center}
    \textit{Can one distinguish $\mathcal{G}(n,d,p)$ from $\mathcal{G}(n,p)$ by their limiting spectral distributions?}
\end{center}

We answer this in the negative under a mild dimension condition: as soon as  $d \gg np\log^2(1/p)$, the two models share the same global spectrum for all $p\in (0,1/2)$. We treat the case when $np\to\infty$ and $np=c$ differently since $\mathcal G(n,p)$ has different limiting spectral distributions \cite{jung2018delocalization,tran2013sparse}. The case when $np\to 0$ is trivial since $(1-o(1))n$ many vertices are isolated and  $1-o(1)$ portion of the eigenvalues are $0$.

Our results (Theorems~\ref{thm:semicircle} and ~\ref{thm:constantp}) thus reveal a universality phenomenon: despite their very different constructions, $\mathcal{G}(n,d,p)$ and $\mathcal{G}(n,p)$ become spectrally indistinguishable above a critical dimension.
Based on the entropic and spectral thresholds $d\asymp np\log(1/p)$ pointed out in \cite{bangachev2025sandwiching,li2023spectral,mao2024impossibility},  we conjecture that the true threshold for semicircle convergence is in fact $d\gg np\log(1/p)$, removing just a single logarithmic factor from our current condition.





\subsection*{Nonlinear random matrices}
A nonlinear random matrix is obtained by a nonlinear function applied entrywise to a classical random matrix ensemble.
In recent years, a rich theory has emerged around such models, including kernel random matrices \cite{bordenave2008eigenvalues,cheng2013spectrum,do2013spectrum,el2010spectrum,mei2022generalization,pandit2024universality},  conjugate kernels and neural tangent kernels arising from neural networks \cite{louart2018random,pennington2017nonlinear,fan2020spectra,guionnet2025global}. Semicircle laws have been obtained  \cite{lu2022equivalence,dubova2023universality,wang2024deformed} for a broad class of nonlinear random matrices. 

Closest to the random geometric graph setting is the random inner-product kernel matrices studied in \cite{lu2022equivalence}.  Let $\bA$ be an $n\times n$ random matrix such that $\bA_{ij}=f(\langle \bv_i,\bv_j\rangle)\1\{i\not=j \}$, where $\bv_1,\dots, \bv_n$ are i.i.d. random vectors uniformly distributed on $\mathbb S^{d-1}$, and $f$ is a function independent of $d$. It was shown in \cite{lu2022equivalence} that when $d=\omega(n)$, the limiting spectral distribution of $\bA$ is the semicircle law. Taking $f(x)=\1\{x\geq \tau\}$ recovers the adjacency matrix of a $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ for fixed $p\in (0,1)$. However, in the sparse regime $p\to 0$, $f$ is a function depending on $d$, and existing proofs break down.

 To the best of our knowledge,  Theorems~\ref{thm:semicircle} and ~\ref{thm:constantp} are the first to describe the limiting spectral distributions of $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ in the simultaneous high‐dimensional ($d\to\infty$) and sparse ($p\to0$) regimes. This model unites two of the most challenging aspects in modern random matrix analysis, nonlinearity and sparsity, which require novel technical tools. Beyond the growing‐dimension setting, the spectrum of fixed‐dimensional random geometric graphs has also been explored in
\cite{adhikari2022spectrum,hamidouche2023normalized}.




\subsection*{Spectral gap of random graphs}
 The adjacency matrix in the Erdős–Rényi graph $\mathcal{G}(n, p)$ has long been an important object in both random graph theory and random matrix theory. In particular, once the average degree satisfies $np = \Omega(\log n)$, it is known that, with high probability, the second-largest eigenvalue (in absolute value) is of order $ O(\sqrt{np})$ \cite{feige2005spectral, le2017concentration, benaych2020spectral}. These results show that sparse Erdős–Rényi graphs exhibit nearly optimal spectral expansion, comparable with random regular graphs \cite{friedman2008proof}.


In recent years, this line of work has been extended to high‐dimensional random geometric graphs  \cite{liu2023local,bangachev2024fourier,bangachev2025sandwiching}, motivated by connections to high‐dimensional expanders and data‐science models. In parallel, \cite{baguley2025testing} shows that by changing to the $\ell_{\infty}$-metric on the $d$-dimensional torus, the spectral properties of the random geometric graphs are different from the spherical case. \cite{li2023spectral} analyzed a Gaussian‐mixture block‐model variant of $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$, showing consistency of spectral clustering based on the adjacency matrix.

A natural question is whether, despite the edge dependencies induced by geometry, $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ still achieves the optimal $O(\sqrt{np})$  bound once $d$ exceeds an appropriate threshold. 
Our Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral_gap} shows that for $d=\Omega(np)$, the second eigenvalue of $\bA$ is of order $O(\log^4(n)\sqrt{np})$, which is optimal up to a $\mathrm{polylog}(n)$ factor. 
We conjecture that, above a critical dimension, the second largest eigenvalue of $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ indeed satisfies the optimal  $O(\sqrt{np})$ bound.  Achieving such a bound with the moment method would likely require a novel combinatorial counting argument that goes beyond the classical high‐trace method developed in \cite{vu2007spectral}.



\subsection*{Organization of the paper}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section \ref{sec:main}, we state our main theorems and give an outline of the proofs.  Section~\ref{sec:prelim} includes some basic facts about random matrix theory, ear decomposition, and the block-cut tree, which will be used in our proof. In Sections~\ref{sec:proof_semicircle} and ~\ref{sec:proof_constant}, we prove Theorems~\ref{thm:semicircle} and Theorem~\ref{thm:constantp} for the limiting spectral distribution, respectively. We combine the new proof technique with the high trace method to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral_gap} in Section~\ref{sec:proof_spectra_gap}. 
 Additional proofs and auxiliary lemmas are included in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix}. 









\section{Main results}\label{sec:main}




\subsection{Semicircle law for $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$}


Our first main result establishes a semicircle law for random geometric graphs.




\begin{theorem}[Semicircle law]\label{thm:semicircle}
    Let $\bA$ be the adjacency matrix of a random graph $G\sim \mathcal G(n,d,p)$. Assume $p\in (0,p_0]$ for any constant $p_0<1/2$,  $np\to\infty$ and $d=\omega( np \log^2(1/p))$. Then the empirical spectral distribution of $\frac{\bA}{\sqrt{np(1-p)}}$ converges weakly to the semicircle law.
\end{theorem}

When $p$ is constant, Theorem~\ref{thm:semicircle} recovers the semicircle law for random inner-product kernel matrices from \cite{lu2022equivalence} in the regime $d=\omega(n)$. The special case $p=1/2$ can also be proved using the results from \cite{bangachev2024fourier}. More broadly, it exhibits a universality phenomenon for $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$:  even as the graph becomes sparse, the semicircle law persists under the relatively mild condition  $d=\omega( np \log^2(1/p))$, quantifying the precise trade‐off between sparsity and the ambient dimension.






\subsection{Limiting spectral distribution for $\mathcal G(n,d,\frac{\alpha}{n})$}


We next turn to the very sparse regime $p = \frac{\alpha}{n}$, for fixed $\alpha > 0$. In this case, the limiting spectral distribution of $\mathcal{G}(n,\frac{\alpha}{n})$ no longer follows the semicircle law. Instead, as shown in \cite{bordenave2010resolvent,jung2018delocalization,enriquez2016spectra}, the empirical spectral distribution of the rescaled  adjacency matrix $\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha}} \bA$ converges weakly to a deterministic limit $\nu_{\alpha}$. Its odd moments vanish, and for even $k=2m$,


\begin{align} \label{eq:sparse_limit}
    \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E} \frac{1}{n} \mathrm{tr} \left(\frac{\bA}{\sqrt{\alpha}} \right)^{2m}
=\sum_{\ell=1}^{m} \frac{1}{\ell+1}\binom{2\ell}{\ell} \alpha^{\ell - m}.
\end{align}
Moreover, as $\alpha\to\infty$, $\nu_{\alpha}$ converges back to the semicircle law, although no closed‐form density is known for $\nu_{\alpha}$.
Our next theorem shows that the random geometric graph $\mathcal G(n,d,\frac{\alpha}{n})$ shares this same limiting spectral distribution $\nu_{\alpha}$ whenever the dimension grows mildly.


\begin{theorem}[Bounded expected degree]\label{thm:constantp}
    Let $\bA$ be the adjacency matrix of a random graph $G$ sampled from $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$. Assume $p=\frac{\alpha}{n}$ and $d=\omega( \log^2(n))$. Then the empirical spectral distribution of $\frac{\bA}{\sqrt{\alpha}}$ converges weakly to $\nu_{\alpha}$.
\end{theorem}


We present two numerical simulations for  Theorems~\ref{thm:semicircle} and ~\ref{thm:constantp}. Figure~\ref{fig:simulation_1} shows a semicircle law behavior of the empirical spectral distributions for both $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ and $\mathcal G(n,p)$ and Figure~\ref{fig:simulation_2}
