\subsection{Second largest eigenvalue}

Applying the trace estimates based on ear decomposition and the block-cut tree in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:semicircle}, we also obtain the following bound on the second largest eigenvalue (in absolute value) \[\lambda(\bA):=\max\{|\lambda_2(\bA)|, |\lambda_n(\bA)| \}.\]
\begin{theorem}[Second largest eigenvalue]\label{thm:spectral_gap}
 Let $\bA$ be the adjacency matrix of a random graph $G$ sampled from $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$. For any constant $\gamma \geq 1$, assume $\frac{C_0\gamma^4 \log^{4}(n)}{n} \leq p\leq e^{-4}$ for an absolute constant $C_0>0$. Then there is an absolute constant $C$  such that with probability $1-n^{-\gamma}$,
\begin{align}\label{eq:second}
     \lambda(\bA)\leq C\left(\gamma^4(\log n)^4 \sqrt{np} + \tau np\right).
    \end{align}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Removing technical conditions in \cite{liu2023local}]
    A second eigenvalue bound appears in \cite[Theorem 1.7]{liu2023local}  as a corollary of \cite[Theorem 1.10]{liu2023local}, but only under two restrictive assumptions: (i) $\tau \geq \frac{1}{\sqrt{np}}$,  equivalently  $d=O(np\log^2(1/p))$  and (ii) $np=\omega(d^3 \log^4(n))$, which confine their result to a low-dimensional regime (see also \cite[Eq. (6) and Theorem 3.1]{liu2022testing}). 
    These conditions limit its applicability when one studies expansion properties of  $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$  \cite{abdalla2024guarantees}. By contrast, we remove these technical constraints so that \eqref{eq:second} holds for all ranges of $d$.
\end{remark}




When $d=\Omega( np)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral_gap} implies $\lambda (\bA)=O(\log^4(n)\sqrt{np})$, showing that in high–dimensional regimes,  the expansion of $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ matches that of the Erd\H{o}s-R\'{e}nyi graph \cite{feige2005spectral,le2017concentration}, up to a $\mathrm{poly}\log (n)$ factor. The generalized Alon-Boppana bound in \cite[Theorem 8]{jiang2019spectral} implies that $\lambda(\bA)=\Omega(\sqrt{np})$ with high probability. Thus Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral_gap} is tight up to a $\mathrm{poly}\log (n)$ factor.



\subsection{Technical overview}
Our proof relies on the moment method from random matrix theory \cite{bai2010spectral,vu2007spectral}, applied both to derive the limiting spectral distribution and to bound the top eigenvalues.

To obtain the limiting spectral distribution for a Wigner-type matrix $\mathbf M$ with independent (up to symmetry) entries \cite{zhu2020graphon}, one analyzes the normalized trace $\frac{1}{n}\E \tr (\mathbf M^k)$ for each fixed $k$. After a proper scaling, the dominant contributions come from closed walks on rooted planar trees, each edge of which is traversed exactly twice. Counting these “double‐edge” walks yields the Catalan numbers and hence the $k$-th moment of the semicircle law. Crucially, independence forces any walk containing an edge visited only once to have zero expectation. To obtain a sharp spectral norm bound, one lets $k=O(\log n)$ and evaluates $\E \tr (\mathbf M^k)$ by a finer subgraph‐count estimate, and then applies Markov’s inequality \cite{vu2007spectral}.



In contrast, the adjacency matrix of  $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$ exhibits edge dependencies that invalidate the “zero‐contribution” argument for edges traversed only once. 
To apply the moment method, we work with the centered adjacency matrix  $\bQ=\bA-\E \bA$ and study its normalized trace: \begin{align}
    \frac{1}{n}\E\tr\left(\frac{\bQ}{\sqrt{np(1-p)}} \right)^k=\frac{1}{n^{k/2+1}(p(1-p))^{k/2}}\sum_{i_1,\dots,i_k\in [n]} \E[\bQ_{i_1i_2}\bQ_{i_2i_3}\cdots \bQ_{i_{k-1}i_k} \bQ_{i_ki_1}].
\end{align}
Each term corresponds to a closed walk on the complete graph. To overcome the edge dependencies, our analysis proceeds in two stages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Tree-like closed walks:} When the underlying walk graph is a tree (each edge traversed exactly twice), we exploit conditional independence: by peeling off leaves one at a time, we evaluate the expectation recursively. This recovers the Catalan‐number moments of the semicircle law.
    \item \textit{Non‐tree closed walks:} For walks whose underlying graph contains cycles or edges traversed more than twice, we develop a novel graph‐decomposition algorithm combining block‐cut trees and ear decompositions (see Section~\ref{sec:prelim} for a brief introduction) to evaluate the expectation of a closed-walk graph recursively. Conditioned on the random vectors associated with the endpoints of each ear, this peeling procedure allows us to show that each ear contributes negligibly in the limit. Together with refined subgraph probability estimates from \cite{li2023spectral}, we obtain the semicircle law down to $d=\omega( np\log^2(1/p))$. A similar strategy works for the proof of  Theorem~\ref{thm:constantp} in the constant expected degree regime.
\end{enumerate}




Earlier works also applied the trace method to $\mathcal G(n,d,p)$. \cite{liu2023local} used a 2-core contraction argument to bound $\E\tr(\bQ^k)$ by conditioning on  vertices with degree $\geq 3$.  \cite{li2023spectral} expanded the threshold function in Gegenbauer polynomials and used their orthogonality to evaluate the closed-walk subgraphs, also with a contraction argument. However, their estimates fall short of proving the moment convergence to the semicircle.
Different from their arguments, we evaluate conditional expectations recursively by peeling off ears one by one, which helps us to obtain a tighter bound for the contribution of non-tree closed walks.

For the second eigenvalue bound, we apply our new estimates to all closed-walk subgraphs to bound $\mathbb{E}[\tr(\bA-\E\bA)^k]$ for $k=O(\log n)$. This allows us to remove the technical conditions in \cite{liu2023local} and achieve a spectral gap bound valid across all regimes of $d$.
We expect that our decomposition and peeling techniques will extend to other sparse random geometric graph models and to more general nonlinear random matrices.




\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}
\subsection{Random matrix theory}
\begin{definition}[Empirical spectral distribution]
  Given an $n \times n$ Hermitian matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\lambda_1(A) \leq \lambda_2(A) \leq ... \leq \lambda_n(A)$, the empirical spectral distribution (ESD) for $A$ is 
\begin{equation}
    \mu_A(x) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{\lambda_i(A)} (x). \nonumber
\end{equation}  
\end{definition}
The semicircular distribution with density supported on $[-2,2]$ is 
$\sigma(x) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{4-x^2}$, whose $k$-th moment $m_k$ satisfies $m_k =\begin{cases}
    0  & k \text{ is odd}\\
    C_{\frac{k}{2}} & k \text{ is even}
\end{cases}$,  ~where $C_k = \frac{\binom{2k}{k}}{k+1}$ is  the Catalan number \cite{stanley2011enumerative}.

We will show that the empirical spectral distribution of a random matrix converges to a limiting distribution due to the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}[Lemma B.3 in \cite{bai2010spectral}]
Let $m_k := \int x^k d\mu$ be the $k$-th moment of a probability distribution $\mu$ on $\R$. Under the following Carleman’s condition \[\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{m_{2k}^{1/2k}}=\infty,\]  a distribution is uniquely defined by  $m_k$ for all $k\in \mathbb Z_+$.
\end{lemma} 
The moment method states that, if a distribution $\mu$ is uniquely defined by its moment, then for a given sequence of distribution $\mu_n$ for which $\E_{\mu_n}(x^k) \to \E_{\mu}(x^k)$ for all $k \geq 1$ integer, then $\mu_n$ converges weakly, in the sense of distributions, to $\mu$.






\subsection{Ear decomposition}
Our proof for the semicircle law will rely on the ear decomposition \cite{maon1986parallel,robbins1939theorem} of 2-edge connected graphs to evaluate the contribution of each subgraph in the trace expansion. We give a brief introduction below.



\begin{definition}[$k$-edge-connected]
    A graph is $k$-edge-connected if the removal of any $(k-1)$ edges leaves a connected subgraph.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[Ear decomposition]\label{def:ear_decompose}
Let $G = (V, E) $  be a finite, undirected, 2-edge-connected graph. An \textit{ear decomposition} of $G$ is a sequence of subgraphs
$(R_1, R_2, \dots, R_s)$ satisfying the following conditions:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $R_1$ is a simple cycle in $G$.
        \item For each $ i \geq 2 $, $ R_i $ is a path (called an \emph{ear}) whose internal vertices are disjoint from $ \bigcup_{j=1}^{i-1} V(R_j) $, and whose endpoints lie in $\bigcup_{j=1}^{i-1} V(R_j) $.
        \item The union of all edges in the sequence gives the edge set of $ G $, i.e., $\bigcup_{i=1}^s E(R_i) = E(G).$
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}
See Figure~\ref{fig:Ear Decomposition} for an example of an ear decomposition. By peeling off $R_4, R_3, R_2, R_1$ recursively, the graph becomes empty.

