In the proof of our main results, we will use the following fact about ear decomposition:
\begin{lemma} \label{eq:ear}
A graph $G$ is 2-edge-connected if and only if it has an ear decomposition. For every vertex $v$ in $G$, there exists an ear decomposition in which $v\in R_1$.
\end{lemma}
The proof of Lemma~\ref{eq:ear} is deferred to Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix}.



\subsection{Block-cut tree}
Another ingredient in our proof is the \textit{block-cut tree}. Any connected graph can be decomposed into a tree of 2-edge-connected components, which is called the \textit{block-cut tree} of the graph \cite{hopcroft1973algorithm}. We now introduce some definitions for \textit{block-cut trees}.
\begin{definition}[bridges]
 We define \textit{bridges} as edges whose removal increases the number of connected components. 
\end{definition}


\begin{definition}[Block-cut tree]\label{def:block_cut}
 We decompose the graph $G$ into its 2-edge-connected components, which are connected by bridges.  We express $G$ as
\begin{align}
    G = \bigcup_{i=1}^{r} G_{C_i} \cup \bigcup_{j=1}^{q} F_j,
\end{align}
where each $G_{C_i}$ denotes a \textit{2-edge-connected component}, and each $F_j$ represents a \textit{1-edge-connected component} spanned by bridges. We call each $F_j$ a \textit{bridge component}, and it is a subtree of $G$. This gives a \textit{block-cut tree} of $G$.    
\end{definition}

