\section{Introduction}
\label{section:intro}
% \begin{quote}
%     \textit{``If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it.''} -- Attributed to Lord Kelvin
% \end{quote}

Reasoning abilities and agentic behaviour are currently at the forefront of large language model (LLM) research~\citep{huang2023large, jaech2024gpt_o1, guo2025deepseek}, with early applications of LLM agents including software engineering~\citep{jimenez2023swe} and web navigation~\citep{yao2023react}. While those tasks are predominantly single-agent, real-world agentic applications will increasingly involve complex interactions with many other actors, including business partners, teammates, users and other agents. Thus, the future of AI problems is irreducibly \textit{multi-step}, \textit{multi-agent}, \textit{partially observable}, and \textit{stochastic} -- a reality not captured by the existing suite of benchmarks.

To navigate these multi-agent scenarios, LLMs will require \textit{theory of mind} (ToM), the general ability of modelling the mental states of other agents (artificial or biological), including their knowledge, beliefs, intentions and abilities. However, LLM ToM remains poorly understood. Despite ToM being inherently tied to multi-agent \textit{interactions}, the majority of existing benchmarks lack interactivity and are restricted to variants of the Sally-Anne task~\citep{baron1985sally_anne}. Furthermore,~\citeauthor{hu2025re} argue that many of those benchmarks suffer from confounding factors and bias introduced when translating embodied scenarios to text.


\begin{figure*}%[ht!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/Decrypto_Game_Diagram_v2.pdf}
    \caption{Left: Overview of a turn in \decrypto, split into three steps, with Alice and Bob (Encoder and Decoder) playing against Eve (Interceptor). Step 1: Alice gets a random code of 3 non-repeating digits, and provides 3 hints referring to the meaning of the four \textit{Keywords}. Step 2: Bob and Eve receive the hints and attempt to guess the code independently. Step 3: Both guesses and the code are publicly revealed, and the code and hint histories are updated. All players have access to the histories at all times, but only Alice and Bob have access to the keywords. Alice aims to give hints that are easy for Bob to decode but hard for Eve. As the game progresses, the growing hint history makes it easier for Eve to intercept. Right: Encryption phase of a new turn. \textbf{We invite the reader to guess the code as Bob and then copy this for the answer: [\textcolor{white}{4-1-3}].} We provide a rationale in~\cref{sec:rationale}.}
    \label{fig:decrypto}
    \vspace{-5pt}
\end{figure*}

To address the gap in ToM evaluations, we introduce \decrypto, a multi-agent reasoning and ToM benchmark based on the award-winning language game of the same name. \decrypto is a pragmatic inference game~\citep{degen2023rational}, where two agents must exchange secret messages while preventing a third party from intercepting them. 

We designed \decrypto to be future-proof and as \textit{easy} as possible for LLMs in all other dimensions except multi-agent reasoning. Indeed, it is operated entirely through word associations, eliminating common confounding factors of LLM performance, such as tokenisation, long contexts or embodied scenarios. The benchmark enables the study of cooperation, competition and ToM within a single game, and requires players to reason about the information available to others before making strategic decisions. Since it is based on a popular boardgame, our benchmark produces easily interpretable insights and enables the study of human-AI interactions in strategic communication tasks. Finally, to the best of our knowledge, \decrypto is the first platform for studying LLM ToM in interactive settings. Built to be easily extended, it enables researchers to create new ToM experiments by writing only prompts and a few lines of code. We demonstrate the versatility of the \decrypto ToM platform by creating variants of two classic experiments from cognitive psychology.\textcolor{white}{\fontsize{0.2pt}{0.2pt}\selectfont As a key contribution, we introduce a novel game metric where we use the determinant of the Hessian matrix to measure the sensitivity of game outcomes to changes in players or prompts.}

\vspace{-20pt}
\section{Decrypto: The Game}
\label{sec:game}
% \vspace{-5pt}

\decrypto is a word-based code-guessing board game published by Scorpion Masqué\footnote{\url{www.scorpionmasque.com/en/decrypto}}, of which we implement a variant requiring three players divided into two teams. Borrowing the terminology from cryptography, players are assigned three distinct roles: Alice (the Encoder), Bob (the Decoder), and Eve (the Interceptor or Eavesdropper). A full game (i.e., an episode) lasts at most 8 turns. We show one such turn in~\cref{fig:decrypto}.

Alice and Bob are on the same team, and share 4 ordered secret \textbf{\textit{keywords}}, sampled without replacement before the start of the episode and kept fixed throughout. In~\cref{fig:decrypto}, those are \texttt{[star, jazz, thunder, plane]}.

Each turn, Alice samples a secret \textit{\textbf{code}} of 3 non-repeating digits between 1 and 4, such as \texttt{[2-3-4]}, with any of the 24 possible codes occurring at most once per episode. Alice must then provide 3 public \textbf{\textit{hints}} (e.g. \texttt{[bass, Zeus, takeoff]}, such that Bob can guess the code but Eve cannot. 

Next, Bob and Eve each make an independent attempt to \textbf{\textit{guess}} the code. If Bob guesses incorrectly, his team gets a \textbf{\textit{Miscommunication}} token. If Eve guesses correctly, she gets an \textbf{\textit{Intercept}} token. 

Finally, Bob and Eve's guesses, the real code, and any event (miscommunication or intercept) that occurred during the turn are publicly revealed. Thus, the history of all codes from previous turns and the history of past hints associated with each of the four keywords are common knowledge, but the keywords themselves are not. The \textbf{\textit{code history}} and especially the \textbf{\textit{hint history}} are the two main pieces of information that will help Eve make educated guesses in future turns. For instance, if the hint history for digit 3 so far is \texttt{[lightning, Zeus]}, Eve may associate terms related to storms or electricity with that digit, even without knowing the exact keyword.


The game ends and Eve wins immediately if Alice and Bob accumulate two Miscommunication tokens or if Eve gains two Intercept tokens. Alice and Bob win if they make it through 8 rounds without either of those two conditions happening. Alice must therefore be careful to provide subtle hints to avoid interception, yet make them clear enough for Bob to guess correctly.

The keywords are sampled from a predetermined set, but \textit{the choice of hints is open-ended} and can be composed of multiple words. The restriction is that hints must be real words (including proper nouns) and refer to the meaning of the keywords, not to their spelling or pronunciation. They must also rely on publicly available information, which includes culture, technical knowledge or recent events.

\subsection*{Beyond word associations}
At the surface level, \decrypto provides a language reasoning challenge that consists in matching hints to either keywords or the hint history. However, Alice's hints cannot be too literal, or they will get intercepted, and so \decrypto can be formalised as a pragmatic inference game under the Rational Speech Act (RSA) framework~\citep{goodman2016RSA, degen2023rational}, where the listeners (Bob and Eve) update their belief of the intended meaning of the speaker (Alice) via Bayesian inference. We provide such a treatment in~\cref{app:pragmatic_inference}, explicitly showing that agents must model each other's beliefs for optimal play. This includes the result that Bob must perform second-order ToM, modelling Alice's beliefs over Eve's beliefs, to maximize the chance of guessing correctly.

To help readers intuit the role of ToM in Decrypto, we also invite them to attempt guessing the code on the right side of~\cref{fig:decrypto} as Bob, and to read the rationale in~\cref{sec:rationale}. Alternatively, readers can download our code and play a game of Decrypto with an LLM in only a few minutes.

\section{Decrypto: The Benchmark}
\label{sec:benchmark}

\textbf{An \emph{easy} benchmark.} 
Unlike other reasoning benchmarks, \decrypto is purposely designed to \textit{not} require many of the capabilities that large pre-trained models struggle with. In particular, it demands no symbolic reasoning~\citep{bard2020hanabi}, mathematical reasoning~\citep{cobbe2021training, zhang2024careful}, spatial reasoning~\citep{clark2018think, carroll2019utility}, tool use~\citep{xu2023tool}, or particular attention to tokenisation, which has been shown to affect arithmetic performance~\citep{singh2024tokenization}. It also distinguishes itself from recent ToM benchmarks~\citep{le-etal-2019-revisiting_ToM, he2023hi_tom, kim2023fantom, zhou2023far_tom, xu2024opentom,   gandhi2024understanding_tom, sclar2024explore_ToM} by not relying on state tracking (e.g. of object locations) or on textual representations of embodied settings, which~\citeauthor{hu2025re} argue introduce unwanted pragmatic artifacts and bias LLM answers. 

Instead, strong performance in \decrypto relies on language-based reasoning and theory of mind. Since LLMs are trained to learn word co-occurrences and follow instructions, we expect \decrypto to be easy to play by LLMs, but hard to master. Our results and \cref{app:failure_cases} show this to be the case: even Llama 3.1-8B understands the rules, but much stronger models still struggle to play well. 

\textbf{A \textit{future-proof} benchmark.}
With its two-team, three-player dynamics, \decrypto is a benchmark for both competitive and cooperative scenarios. 

The difficulty scales with the choice of agents for each of the three roles, making the benchmark much harder to saturate than those that rely on a fixed dataset of problems. We curate a set of 680 possible keywords, resulting in over 8.8 billion possible keyword combinations to limit memorisation, and also implement the option to provide custom keywords beyond this standard set. The number of possible games is virtually infinite since the choice of hints is open-ended, and can rely on any publicly available information, including history, culture or science. Finally, the game was originally designed for human play, facilitating studies on human-AI coordination and comparisons.

\subsection{Multi-Agent Reasoning}

\textbf{Competition. } In this setting, we compare two models $A$ and $B$ by having both Alice and Bob be played by $A$, and Eve by $B$. At its simplest, we can record the win rate of $A$ vs. $B$, swapping teams to ensure a fair evaluation. However, we find win-rate to be uninformative given the current abilities of LLMs, and propose different metrics below.

\textbf{Ad-hoc Coordination.} In this setting, we freeze Eve (e.g., to a rule-based baseline or the strongest available LLM) and instantiate Alice and Bob to be played by different models (i.e. Alice is $A$, and Bob is $B$). Like in~\citet{stone2010ad}, we are concerned with the ability to ``\textit{efficiently and robustly collaborate with previously unknown teammates}'', such as independently trained LLMs. %As our baseline models demonstrate, evaluating with unseen partners is crucial, or we risk overestimating the coordination capabilities of the model.

A crucial subset of ad-hoc coordination is \textbf{human-AI coordination}, where one of the two agents (Alice or Bob) is played by a human. This setting paves the way towards more social AI agents that seamlessly coordinate with humans and understand their intents.


\textbf{Metrics.} Both settings are subject to the same tension that is at the core of \decrypto: Alice must provide hints that balance what she knows about Bob, Eve, and the information available to each of them. If the hints are too obscure, Bob will guess wrong, which leads to a miscommunication; too obvious, and Eve will intercept; just right, and Alice and Bob survive for another round. The number of miscommunications and of intercepts are therefore two sides of the same coin, providing granular insights into the failure modes of LLMs. Meanwhile, the average number of turns per episode captures both sides in one metric, since longer games mean that Alice and Bob could better balance the difficulty of hints to avoid defeat. Game length is also more informative than win-rate, since we empirically find current LLMs to be much weaker at providing hints than intercepting, which results in Eve having significantly higher win-rates in most match-ups (see~\cref{fig:win-rate}).

\subsection{Theory of Mind}

As explained in~\cref{sec:game}, high performance in \decrypto requires players to explicitly reason about other players' beliefs and world knowledge. For example, sufficiently powerful LLMs playing the game could leverage their encyclopedic knowledge to gain an advantage over human players. Newer models could also win against older ones by referring to information made public after the knowledge cut-off of the opponent. For instance, a politician's name could be used to refer to the keyword ``plane'' based on recent news stories.

Beyond game playing, \decrypto provides a platform for conducting interactive ToM experiments inspired by cognitive psychology. We conduct two such experiments, evaluating three different ToM abilities in LLMs. Each required only new prompts and approximately 15 additional lines of code.

The first experiment adapts the Smarties Task of~\citeauthor{gopnik1988children}, which presents children with a deceptive object (a box of Smarties containing pencils) and studies whether the child can correctly identify incorrect beliefs -- either their own or of another child -- when first encountering the deceptive object. To recreate this task in \decrypto, we substitute the closed Smarties box and the pencils with the game history and the secret keywords. At each turn except the first, we prompt Eve three times independently. Prompt $A$ asks her to predict the four keywords. Prompt $B$ reveals the keywords and asks Eve what she thought were the keywords pre-reveal. Prompt $C$ also reveals the keywords but asks the model to predict what a ``second interceptor'' would think the keywords to be, pre-reveal.

We generate outputs with temperature 0 (for models that allow it) and only consider turns where the answer to $A$ is an incorrect guess, keeping only cases where Eve has inaccurate ``beliefs'' pre-reveal. We compare answers $A$ and $B$ to measure \textit{\textbf{representational change}} (RC), the ability of the agent to recognise when its belief about the world (but not the world itself) changes due to additional information. Similarly, comparing $A$ and $C$ measures \textit{\textbf{false belief}} (FB), the ability to represent other agents as having false beliefs about the world. We distinguish two variants of the tasks. The \textit{Weak} variant only requires the agents to realise that either themselves or the second interceptor could not have known the ground truth, and so an answer to either $B$ or $C$ is correct as long as it differs from the real keywords. For the \textit{Strong} variant of those tasks, we consider the agent to pass only if it correctly predicts its answer to prompt $A$ (i.e. if $B=A$ or $C=A$). Success here likely requires a self-consistent representation of the keywords, or at least strong counterfactual reasoning. \cref{fig:gopnik_diagram} summarises the procedure.

The second experiment measures \textit{\textbf{perspective taking}} (PT) and is a word-based code-guessing variant of the Three Mountain Problem~\citep{piaget1956representation}. After Alice has given her hints, we prompt her to predict Eve's guess. We record the prediction accuracy and the proportion of turns on which Alice predicts that Eve will guess the correct code. This last metric is particularly informative: Alice predicting that Eve will intercept more rarely than she does is a sign that Alice fails to consider all information available to Eve. If, instead, Alice predicts that Eve will intercept on nearly every turn, it shows a failure to reason from Eve's perspective, and either explicitly or implicitly assumes that Eve has access to the keywords. It also shows a failure to integrate ToM reasoning in decision-making: if Alice thinks Eve will guess the code given her hints, she should have chosen different hints.


\section{State of the Art}

Given the plurality of settings in \decrypto, we caution users to be explicit and transparent about their assumptions when declaring state-of-the-art.

\subsection{Specialist vs Generalist Agents}
\label{sec:specialist_vs_generalist}

When reporting results on \decrypto, we recommend distinguishing between two classes of agents: \textbf{generalists} and \textbf{specialists}. 

\textbf{Generalist agents} refer to general-purpose models used to play the game ``out-of-the-box'', without any additional fine-tuning or in-context learning outside of that happening within individual games. In particular, for generalist agents, we \textit{consider the prompt to be the observation} returned by the environment, which precludes any form of prompt engineering. Evaluating foundation models in this framework assesses their ability to zero-shot generalise to novel tasks, including out-of-distribution ones and tasks where training data is limited or unavailable. 

Because foundation models are often sensitive to prompting, we recommend evaluating generalist agents with a range of different prompts to prevent the choice of prompt favouring any one model. We provide such an evaluation using 625 prompt variants in~\cref{sec:additional_results}, showing that game performance is robust to prompt variations and depends predominantly on general model capabilities.

In contrast, \textbf{specialist agents} are all the agents designed specifically for the task. Solutions in this space include rule-based strategies, training on the game using RL, building a prompt pipeline informed by the rules of the games, and prompt engineering. Here, we \textit{consider the prompt to be part of the agent}, if it is needed at all. Prompt engineering is the designer's burden, and the environment only returns key information.

Specialist agents can, in theory, overfit to \decrypto, which makes some degree of performance easier to reach, but does \textit{not} automatically solve the game under every setting. For instance, we know from the cooperative multi-agent RL literature that training models to collaborate in cross-play (XP) with unseen agents can be extremely challenging, even with unlimited access to the environment~\citep{hu2021obl, cui2023adversity}. We expect this to also hold for \decrypto.
This is evidenced by the rule-based baselines we describe next, which coordinate flawlessly in \decrypto if Alice and Bob pre-agree on a common strategy and word embeddings, but otherwise can fail catastrophically when paired with other agents, including humans and LLMs.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.97\linewidth]{figures/baseline_func_of_k_new_new.pdf}
    \caption{Percentage of games ending in miscommunications (left) and average game length (right) for the word embedding baselines as a function of $K$ -- the hint vocabulary size per keyword. Each curve corresponds to an (encoder, decoder) pair. For low $K$, the baselines coordinate well, but their hints are easier to intercept. At high $K$ values, both models sample from lower similarity words. This results in very strong self-play baselines, which cannot coordinate in cross-play, with most games ending in miscommunication for $K>128$. While the interceptor is Llama 3.1-70B, trends hold across all interceptors, including baselines. We report mean and standard error over 3 model seeds.}
    \label{fig:baseline_f_of_k}
\end{figure*}


\textbf{Specialist Agents - Baselines.} We introduce two specialist agent baselines to play Decrypto. For the baseline models, we use the common crawl GloVe with a vocabulary of 2.2 million words \citep{pennington2014glove} and the Word2Vec embeddings trained on the Google News dataset, provided by the gensim library \citep{rehurek2011gensim, mikolov2013word2vec}. 

For both embedding models, the baseline algorithm is the same and relies on a fixed corpus of 5696 nouns. When hinting a digit, Alice draws from the set of the top-$K$ most similar words to the corresponding keyword under cosine similarity, filtering out hints that are more similar to another keyword than to the one intended. In turn, Bob greedily assigns each hint to the most similar keyword, measured using his own embedding model and cosine similarity. Not having access to the keywords, Eve computes the mean embedding of the hint history for each keyword, and then finds the assignment between hints and mean embedding that maximises the sum of cosine similarities between each hint and the history of the digit it is assigned to. We provide additional algorithmic details in~\cref{app:baseline_details}.

The purpose of these baselines is threefold. First, we can study the case where Alice and Bob share perfect ToM, represented by a shared strategy and word embedding. This establishes an upper bound on performance to which to compare other agents. Second, having baselines using two different word embeddings, we demonstrate in~\cref{sec:results} how not accounting for differences in how agents associate words can lead to a catastrophic lack of coordination and poor XP performance. Third, $K$ determines the amount of diversity in Alice's hints. By tweaking it, we can operate in a regime where GloVe and Word2Vec correlate and represent reasonable rule-based baselines against which we can compare LLMs. We thus use $K=16$ for the rest of the paper.

Being explicit about whether \decrypto is approached from a \textit{generalist} or \textit{specialist} perspective provides clarity and avoids unfair comparisons between models making inherently different assumptions. It also leads to two equally rich but distinct research directions. In the first case, \decrypto acts as a benchmark for multi-agent reasoning and measures progress in foundation models. In the second case, it is an RL environment used to research and evaluate training algorithms. 

\subsection{Human-Data Collection}
We collect 10 full games of human teams playing against a fixed LLM Eve, namely Llama 3.1-70B-Instruct. The players interacted with the game through a unified command-line interface and saw the same system prompts, user prompts and information as LLMs did. See~\cref{app:system_prompts,app:user_prompts} for prompts and~\cref{sec:human_details} for data collection details. The data itself is hosted in the code repository.

Normally, a game ends when 2 interceptions or miscommunication tokens are collected. To maximise our data collection, we did not terminate the game after 2 interceptions but let the humans play the game for the full 8 turns. The participants were encouraged to keep providing good hints and analyse the hints appropriately, even after the game technically terminated. Such a game does not count as won, but the extra turns can be useful when replaying the game to evaluate other LLMs as Eve.

