To kickstart this benchmark, we assess the cooperative, competitive, and theory of mind capabilities of specialised and generalist agents in Decrypto. Amongst generalist open-source models, we evaluate Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct~\citep{dubey2024llama}, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B~\citep{guo2025deepseek}. From the closed-source models, we only evaluate GPT-4o due to resource constraints. We also include the two baselines mentioned in~\cref{sec:specialist_vs_generalist}. Finally, we present human-AI coordination and competition results. Experimental details can be found in~\cref{app:experimental_details}. Unless otherwise mentioned, we report mean $\pm$ standard error over three seeds for all experiments.

% \paragraph{Baselines.}
\textbf{Baselines.} We first look at the specialised agents baselines and show in~\cref{fig:baseline_f_of_k} that we can control $K$ for the top-$K$ selection to make them arbitrarily strong. Smaller $K$ prioritises words semantically similar to the keyword, making the hints easier to guess. Bigger $K$ selects semantically less similar hints, which are harder to guess if Bob cannot access the same embeddings. For example, suppose GloVe-Alice wants to hint the keyword ``fire'' to Word2Vec-Bob, and $K=50$. The 50th most similar word to ``fire'' from the hint corpus would be ``oil'' for GloVe. However, ``oil'' is not even in the top 1000 most similar words for Word2Vec-Bob, highlighting a situation where cross-play difficulties arise. This finding also holds when an LLM plays Bob. As $K$ increases, miscommunications increase, and the average turn length decreases.


% \paragraph{Coordination.} 
\textbf{Coordination.} Baseline-LLM teams get significantly more miscommunications than baseline-baseline or LLM-LLM teams. The top row of~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns} shows the total number of games ending in miscommunication out of 32 games for the two baselines and two LLMs, Llama-3.1-8B and Llama-3.1-70B. Among LLMs, the main determining factor for miscommunications is the model used for Bob, with the smallest model, LLama-3.1-8B, seeing the most miscommunications. Interestingly, we do not observe any self-play/cross-play gap, even though it would technically be possible for an LLM to perfectly model its counterparts when playing with or against the same model.


% \paragraph{Competition.} 
\textbf{Competition.} For our competitive results, larger models generally perform better as both Alice/Bob and Eve. However, we find that the win rate is heavily skewed in favour of Eve, as shown in~\cref{fig:win-rate}, with most models rarely surviving.
We measure the number of interceptions and average game length of different SP teams against different Eve Agents. We report our results in the bottom row of~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns}. However, as our human experiments show, such a heavy bias is not a property of the game but of the LLMs themselves. Indeed, we find that novice humans achieve $33\%$ win rate against even the strongest Eve agents.

% \paragraph{Human Evaluation.}
\textbf{Human Evaluation.}~\cref{table:human_eval} shows the result of replaying collected human games by re-using the human-provided hints and replacing either Eve or Bob with different LLMs. When Alice and Bob are humans, we find the strongest interceptors to be DeepSeek-R1 and Claude 3.7, with the most intercepts and the lowest win rate. We next fix Eve's guesses to match the data and replace Bob with different LLMs. Surprisingly, DeepSeek-R1-32B, which showed strong results in~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns}, trails significantly behind both LLama-3.1-70B and GPT-4o. More importantly, all decoders tested (LLMs or baselines) fall short of the human decoders, with only Claude 3.7 with extended thinking coming close.
This demonstrates a significant gap for most models in their ability to coordinate with humans.

