% \paragraph{Theory of Mind.} 
\textbf{Theory of Mind.} We show results for both experiments in~\cref{fig:tom_barplots}. Most models tested achieve high accuracy in both weak RC and weak FB, with Llama 3.1-70B slightly outperforming newer models, including Claude 3.7 with extended thinking and o1 high. In contrast, all models get $\leq 10\%$ accuracy on the strong tasks, showing poor self-consistency even when generating outputs with 0 temperature. In PT, we see Llama trail behind other models in prediction accuracy. However, the last metric in~\cref{fig:tom_barplots} reveals that all models except Llama predict that Eve will intercept on nearly every turn, when the real interception rate is $\sim 52\%$, indicated by the dotted line. Inspecting model outputs in~\cref{app:piaget_first_turn}, we find those models to predict that Eve will intercept \textit{even on the first turn}, with only Llama correctly pointing out that Eve can do no better than a random guess. Surprisingly,~\cref{app:pt_o1_handhold} shows these results to hold even if we modify the PT prompt to emphasize that Eve ``\emph{does *NOT* know the secret keywords}''. As stated above, this is a double failure of ToM: first, as a failure to reason from Eve's perspective, and second, because the model failed to consider how the hints will be received when choosing them. %We discuss these findings further in~\cref{app:piaget_first_turn}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

BattleAgentBench~\citep{wang2024battleagentbench}, multi-robot studies \citep{li2023theory} and other works such as~\citet{liu2023llm} and~\citet{guo2024embodied} have recently studied LLMs in multi-agent contexts. While detailed, they focus on spatial reasoning rather than on the language-based reasoning and ToM abilities central to Decrypto. Among multi-agent environments, social deduction games such as Werewolf~\citep{xu2023exploring, xu2023language} and Avalon~\citep{light2023avalonbench} are particularly amenable to studying LLMs, as are negotiation games \citep{abdelnabi2023llm}. However, they provide less structured and atomic insight than Decrypto, given the free-form dialogue and frequent deception in those settings. AgentPro \citep{zhang2024agent} focuses on competitive scenarios in games like Blackjack and Poker, but lacks the cooperative aspect of Decrypto.

ToM has been a subject of focus in AI as early as 2018, when~\citeauthor{rabinowitz2018machine} trained ToM-net, a simple network capable of predicting the behaviour of other artificial agents and acting based on false beliefs. In contrast, recent works on ToM have focused on LLMs modelling human beliefs~\citep{chen2024tombench,sclar2024explore_ToM,kim2023fantom}. \decrypto connects both by enabling the study of LLM ToM applied to both humans and other artificial agents. Unlike other recent ToM benchmarks~\citep{le-etal-2019-revisiting_ToM, he2023hi_tom, kim2023fantom, zhou2023far_tom, xu2024opentom,   gandhi2024understanding_tom, sclar2024explore_ToM} and~\citet{rabinowitz2018machine}, however, \decrypto does so in an interactive setting, where agents can not only model the beliefs of other agents, but also influence them. 

\citet{kosinski2023spontaneous} also studied the Smarties Task, from which they concluded that ``ToM-like ability [...] may have spontaneously emerged'' in LLMs. Those claims were directly challenged by~\citeauthor{ullman2023large} in subsequent work, with which our results agree. \citet{shaikh2023codenames} use another language game, Codenames, to study cross-cultural pragmatics. Their work stops short of evaluating LLMs, but raises important questions about the role of cultural backgrounds in pragmatic inference. \decrypto is a natural choice for pursuing research in this direction, since we can specify different personas in the LLM prompts.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
Our paper introduces \decrypto, an interactive multi-agent benchmark designed to evaluate coordination, competition and theory of mind capabilities in language models. \decrypto addresses critical limitations of existing ToM benchmarks, such as biases arising from textual translation of embodied scenarios or lack of interactivity. Designed to be future-proof and to eliminate confounding factors known to limit LLM performance, \decrypto fills an important gap in existing benchmarks. Furthermore, our codebase provides a versatile platform for quickly designing interactive ToM experiments inspired by cognitive psychology.


We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate open-source and closed-source LLMs. We find that even state-of-the-art models struggle with the nuanced communication and strategic reasoning that \decrypto requires, often underperforming simple baselines in cooperative and competitive settings. Similarly, our human-AI experiments shed light on the limited ability of recent LLMs to coordinate with humans or understand their communications. 

Finally, we create variants of two seminal experiments from cognitive psychology to study LLM ToM in interactive settings, analogously to how it is studied in children. Our experiments provide strong evidence that state-of-the-art still lack many ToM skills. Moreover, we find newer and more capable reasoning models such as Claude 3.7 Sonnet and o1 high to be significantly worse at some ToM tasks than older models, demonstrating the need for more ToM methods and benchmarks.

\decrypto opens up several promising research directions. On the ToM side, these include enhancing the ToM capabilities of language models and investigating differences between how LLMs model humans versus how they model other agents. From the multi-agent perspective, an exciting avenue for future work is adapting algorithms from multi-agent RL~\citep{silver2017mastering, hu2020other} to fine-tune LLMs. For a longer discussion on the limitations of our work and on the multiple research directions enabled by \decrypto, we refer the reader to~\cref{app:limitations_future_work}.

