\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{app:limitations_future_work}

We firmly believe \decrypto to be a valuable and future-proof benchmark for evaluating multi-agent reasoning and theory of mind. However, our work still has a number of limitations, which we hope to address in future work.

First, while we largely eliminated the confounding factors that affect LLM performance, the game dynamics and the ToM tasks may still be too complicated for weaker models. For instance, when trying Llama 3.1-8B-Instruct on the perspective taking task, we found that it failed to understand the prompt altogether. 

Regarding evaluations, the main limitation of our work is the computational cost of computing~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns}. An individual run is relatively cheap (approximately \$0.10 per role per episode for Claude 3.7 Sonnet). However, given the 3-player nature of the game, the total number of combinations for $n$ models is $n^3$, or $2n^2$ if only computing the matrix slices in~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns}. For a large number of models, this can induce significantly larger costs than standard single-agent benchmarks. To mitigate this, we provide the tools to run evaluations locally and efficiently, leveraging prefix caching where possible to reduce unnecessary computations.
We also acknowledge the limited scope of our human study in~\cref{table:human_eval}. While the 10 games collected were sufficient to demonstrate the limitations of LLMs in human-AI interactions, we believe that there is much to be learned from a more extensive study, including with humans as interceptors. We therefore made sure to provide the tools to enable such research, such as a lightweight command-line interface for conducting human studies.

Finally, theory of mind is an umbrella term for multiple abilities. Our work evaluates some of them, such as representational change, false belief and perspective taking, and we hope extensions to \decrypto to expand on this set in the future. Nevertheless, there are ToM abilities which \decrypto is ill-fitted to evaluate. For instance, models performing well in the game and the ToM tasks we designed may still fail to reason about the intentions or emotions of other agents.

\decrypto also enables a number of other research directions connected to ToM, multi-agent reasoning and reinforcement learning, and pragmatic inference. From a ToM perspective, \decrypto is easy to extend with additional ToM experiments, which can complement the results presented in our work. Given the limitations of current frontier models, we also believe there is a dire need for methods to improve the ToM abilities of LLMs. \decrypto features short episodes and dense rewards, making it a suitable environment to study the application of multi-agent RL algorithms towards LLM post-training. In particular, \decrypto features stochasticity and partial observability, two key ingredients for ``meaningful'' coordination challenges~\citep{ellis2023smacv2}. It would therefore be interesting to study whether fine-tuning LLMs on \decrypto using self-play~\citep{silver2017mastering} or other MARL algorithms~\citep{hu2020other} produces the same kind of idiosyncrasies observed when training \textit{tabula rasa} models in other cooperative environments~\citep{cui2023adversity, gessler2025overcookedv2}. Finally, we believe the study of pragmatic inference in LLMs to be a promising avenue for future research, which is made much easier by the release of our benchmark. We formalise \decrypto as a pragmatic inference game and provide suggestions for this line of work in~\cref{app:pragmatic_inference}.

\section{Impact Statement}
\label{app:impact_statement}

As LLMs become more prevalent in our daily lives and start interacting with each other and with human users, we believe it becomes crucial to understand the multi-agent and ToM abilities of foundation models. Our work provides a novel assessment of those abilities, and the tools to conduct future studies on inter-AI and human-AI interactions. As such, we hope to bring attention to what we consider to be an understudied subset of agentic capabilities, and that our work will lead to models with a strong social and human-centric behaviour.

However, ToM encompasses a broad set of abilities, of which \decrypto evaluates only a subset. A possible misuse of \decrypto is to equate strong performance on our benchmark to more general ToM abilities, including intent inference and emotion understanding, which are not evaluated by \decrypto. Such claims have incorrectly been made about other ToM benchmarks~\citep{hu2025re}, and so we ask future researchers to be careful when concluding that an AI system has ToM.

\section{Rationale for the code in Figure 1}
\label{sec:rationale}
The code that Alice encrypted in~\cref{fig:decrypto} is \texttt{[4-1-3]}. The rationale is at follows. The four keywords are \texttt{[1. star, 2. jazz, 3. thunder, 4. plane]}, and the hints are \texttt{[two, clone, AC/DC]}. ``Two'' refers to ``two dimensions'', which is the defining characteristic of a geometric plane. ``Clone'' is a hint for ``star'', since both ``clone'' and ``star'' are common operations performed on a GitHub repository. ``AC/DC'' is a rock band, and one of their most famous songs, ``Thunderstruck'' starts with the word ``Thunder'' being chanted repeatedly.

The choice of hints relies on multiple assumptions made by Alice, here played by the authors, about Bob, played by the readers. The first hint relies on the assumption that many readers will have a mathematical background and quickly think of ``plane'' in its geometric sense, rather than as an aircraft. Similarly, we assume for the second hint that readers will be sufficiently familiar with GitHub to make the same connection as us. Failing that, we also anticipated that some readers might link the two terms by thinking of the ``clone troopers'' from Star Wars, which also leads to a correct guess. Finally, we assumed that the song ``Thunderstruck'' is famous enough to be picked up by most readers.

This is an example of how Alice can use ToM and the specific information she has about another player, in this case the readers, to inform her choice of hints. We hope we were successful. 

