\section{Baseline Details}
\label{app:baseline_details}

Here we provide additional details on the word-embedding baselines introduced in~\cref{sec:specialist_vs_generalist}.

The baselines work as follows. Alice can choose hints from a fixed corpus of the 5696 most common nouns extracted from the Brown, Gutenberg, and Webtext corpora \citep{bird2009nltk}, filter to ensure all extracted nouns are present in both the GloVe and Word2Vec embeddings. (We use this hint corpus for the baselines only; LLM agents generate hints in an open-ended fashion.)

Before Alice chooses hints, Alice and Bob agree on a strategy. For each code digit $c_i\in[1,4], i\in [1,3]$, Alice picks a hint $h_i$ and guarantees that the hint picked for the digit is more similar to the $c_i$-th keyword $k_{c_i}$ than to any other keyword, i.e., $s(h_i, k_{c_i}) > s(h_i, k_j)\quad \forall j \in -\mathbf{c}$, where $-\mathbf{c} = [1,4] \setminus c_i$ and $s$ is the cosine similarity.

Alice increases the diversity of her hints by randomly sampling each hint from the top-$K$ most similar words for each keyword under cosine similarity, filtering those that do not satisfy the constraint above. We also enforce that Alice cannot reuse any previously used hints in the same episode.

Bob then analyses the hints and assigns each hint to its most similar keyword under cosine similarity, i.e., $\argmax_{c \in [1,4]} s(h_i, k_c)\hspace{4pt} \forall i \in [1,3]$. Bob guesses the code perfectly if Alice and Bob use the same word embeddings because the hints are guaranteed to be the most similar to the correct keyword under the same embedding model.

Eve is only given the hints $\mathbf{h}$ and the hint history for each keyword, which at turn 0 is empty. Eve calculates the cosine similarity between the average embedding of each keyword's hint history with the hints. This results in a similarity matrix of size $N \times M$, where $N (=3)$ is the number of hints and $M (=4)$ is the number of keywords. Eve combinatorially calculates the globally optimal guess based on the similarity values. Since $N$ and $M$ are typically small values, we are unconcerned about the computational complexity. However, Eve's selection task is equivalent to a linear assignment problem and efficient algorithms, such as the Jonker-Volgenant algorithm, are available in open-source libraries such as SciPy \citep{scipy2020}.

\section{Experimental Details}
\label{app:experimental_details}

Here we provide details for the experiments reported in~\cref{sec:results}. 

\subsection{Prompting}
All our experiments are text-only and conducted exclusively through prompts, which we provide in~\cref{app:system_prompts,app:user_prompts}. The system prompt is composed of an explanation of the game rules, common to all roles, and a set of role-specific instructions. The user prompts are role-specific but follow a similar structure, providing, in order, a summary of the previous turn and of the game so far, role specific information (e.g. the code or the hints), a summary of the role task, and formatting instructions. For the ToM tasks, the prompts are structure similarly, and rely on the same output formatting. For agents that do not admit a system prompt, such as Deepseek-R1, we include the system prompt as a prefix to the first user message. 

We maintain prompts and model outputs in context, making \decrypto a multi-turn environment. We use a regular expression to extract the hints or the guess from the model outputs. If the code fails to detect a correctly-formatted answer in the output, we re-prompt the model again up to 10 times, reminding it of the correct output format. If the model has still not provided an answer after 10 attempts, we default to a dummy answer. In practice, this almost never happens unless the output token limit is set very low for very verbose models, such as Deepseek-R1. By default, retries are not added to the model's context to avoid cluttering it and unnecessarily lowering its performance. ToM prompts and outputs are also excluded from the context for future turns.

\subsection{Generation parameters}
We set the model temperature to 0.6 for experiments in~\cref{fig:comp_win_rate_and_turns,table:human_eval}, and to 0 for ToM experiments in~\cref{fig:tom_barplots} to reduce the impact of model stochasticity. We note that some close source models are not entirely deterministic, even with the temperature set to 0. 

Our goal was not to evaluate model verbosity, but rather the limit of their capabilities. Therefore, we set generous token limits (between 750 for non-reasoning models and up to 10000 for reasoning ones) to prevent cutting model generations prematurely. However, we found that models rarely got close to their token limit. For instance, Claude 3.7 Sonnet with Extended Thinking output approximately 500 total tokens per turn on average, and sometimes as low as 350 despite a limit of 1500 tokens.

The full set of generation parameters can be found in the configs we provide as part of our code release, and which enable reproduction of the results in our paper with only a few commands.

\subsection{Computational Costs}
We ran our experiments on a mix of local and API-hosted models. 

All open-source models were hosted locally on a high performance computing cluster using vLLM~\citep{kwon2023vllm}. Each model instance ran on 8x Nvidia A100 GPUs. Although not necessary, we also launched multiple instances of the same model running, using concurrency to accelerate inference.

System prompts are on the order of 1000 tokens, and the user prompts at each turn are less than 300 tokens each. Models will typically output around 500 tokens per prompt, but the number can go much higher for particularly verbose models. We estimate that evaluating Claude 3.7 Sonnet with Extended Thinking cost approximately \$0.10 per role per episode. A full run of 32 episodes, 3 random seeds, and with all three roles played by Claude 3.7 Sonnet costs approximately \$30.

Finally, we detail human data collection in~\cref{sec:human_details}.

