\section{\decrypto as a pragmatic inference game}
\label{app:pragmatic_inference}

With mild assumptions, we can formalize \decrypto as an iterated pragmatic inference game in the Rational Speech Act (RSA) framework introduced by \citeauthor{frank2012RSA_og} and extended in~\citet{goodman2016RSA}. Doing so provides a Bayesian view of how successful communication in the game requires agents to model each other's decision process.

We consider each turn in isolation, so that each episode of \decrypto is composed of up to 8 independent pragmatic inference games. Agents must associate utterances $u$ (i.e. a set of 3 hints) to an intended meaning $m$ (the code for that turn or, equivalently, the corresponding combination of 3 keywords).

The RSA framework starts by defining a hypothetical \textit{literal listener}, which interprets utterances according to their semantics:
\begin{equation}
    P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) \propto \delta_{m\in[[u]]}P(m).
\end{equation}
Here $P(m)$ is the probability of a given meaning and $\delta_{m\in[[u]]}$ is the publicly known \textit{lexical function}, which returns $1$ if the meaning $m$ is \textit{semantically compatible} with utterance $u$ and 0 otherwise. The literal listener therefore assigns 0 probability to meanings that are semantically incompatible with the utterance, but otherwise defers to the normalized prior probability of a meaning. In \decrypto, all meanings are equally likely, so $P(m) = \frac{1}{|M|}$, where $M$ is the set codes that have not been seen on previous turns. 

The game rules stipulate that hints must refer to the meaning of the keywords. Therefore, $[[u]]$ can be defined as the subset of all combinations of three keywords $m \in M$ that have a semantic link to the hints. For instance, suppose the keywords are \texttt{[star, jazz, thunder, plane]}, as in~\cref{fig:decrypto}, and the hints are $u' = \texttt{[fusion, Zeus, pilot]}$. Then,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:lexicon_example}
    m_1 &\defeq \texttt{[star, thunder, plane]} \equiv [1,3,4] \in [[u']], \\
    m_2 &\defeq \texttt{[jazz, thunder, plane]} \equiv [2,3,4] \in [[u']],
\end{align}
among other meanings, and $P_\textrm{Lit}(m_1|u') = P_\textrm{Lit}(m_2|u') = \frac{1}{|[[u']]|}$. This is because stars are fuelled by nuclear fusion and ``jazz fusion'' is a popular music genre.

Alice is then assumed to be an \textit{approximately rational speaker}, who chooses utterances according to a probability
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:p_alice}
    P_\textrm{Alice}(u|m) \propto e^{\lambda U(u,m)},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ captures the degree of optimality and $U(u,m)$ is the utility of speaking utterance $u$ to communicate meaning $m$. To avoid infinite recursion, the speaker's \textit{utility} in RSA balances the utterance cost with the probability that the literal listener will interpret the utterance correctly:
\begin{equation*}
    U(u,m) =\log P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) - \textrm{cost}(u,m).
\end{equation*}

The RSA framework typically assumes a single speaker and collaborative listener, but we can capture the competitive dynamics between Alice and Eve by letting the utterance cost be a function of the probability that Eve intercepts the code.
There are multiple possible candidate functions, but since miscommunication and intercepts play an equally important role in \decrypto, we let
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cost}
    \textrm{cost}(u,m) = \varepsilon \log (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)).
\end{equation}
Here, $P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)$ denotes Eve's probability of intercepting the code and $\varepsilon \in [0,1]$ captures how bad an intercept would be on that turn.
For instance, if this is the eighth turn and no intercept has occurred so far in the game, getting intercepted will not impact Alice and Bob's chances of winning, so we set $\varepsilon=0$.
Similarly, we can add a variable $\beta \in [0,1]$, which captures the importance of not miscommunicating on this turn. This makes Alice's utility
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:utility}
    U(u,m) = \beta \log P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) + \varepsilon \log (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)).
\end{equation}

Finally, Bob, the \textit{pragmatic listener}, decodes utterances based on the common prior over meanings and to a model of the speaker, with probability
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bob_perfect}
    P_\textrm{Bob}(m|u,\textrm{Alice}) \propto P(m)P_\textrm{Alice}(u|m) %= \frac{1}{|M|} P_\textrm{Alice}(u|m).
\end{equation}

Expanding~\cref{eq:bob_perfect}, we get
\begin{align}
    P_\textrm{Bob}(m|u,\textrm{Alice}) &\propto \frac{1}{|M|} e^{\lambda U(u,m)} \\
    &= \frac{1}{|M|} e^{\lambda(\beta \log P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) + \varepsilon \log (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)))} \\
    &= \frac{1}{|M|} P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u)^{\lambda\beta} (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u))^{\lambda\varepsilon}.
\end{align}
Hence, in all cases where $\varepsilon > 0$, \textbf{Bob must model how Alice models Eve in order to interpret the hints correctly}. This is a clear example of why multi-step theory of mind is necessary for optimal play in \decrypto.

The listener rarely has a perfect model of the speaker. \citeauthor{goodman2016RSA} discuss \textit{uncertain RSA} (uRSA), a common extension to the RSA framework where the pragmatic listener is uncertain about the model underlying the speaker's behaviour~\citep{kao2014speaker_uncertainty}.
Under uRSA, the pragmatic listener has a distribution over possible speakers $s$, and so Bob has a joint probability over speaker and interpretation:
\begin{equation}
    P_\textrm{Bob}(m,s|u) \propto P(m)P(s)P_\textrm{s}(u|m,s).
\end{equation}
Marginalizing over speakers gives us the probability that Bob interprets the hints correctly:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bob_marginal}
    P_\textrm{Bob}(m|u) \propto P(m)\sum_s P(s)P_\textrm{s}(u|m,s) = P(m) P_\textrm{marginal}(u|m),
\end{equation}
with $P_\textrm{marginal}(u|m) \defeq \sum_s P(s)P_\textrm{s}(u|m,s)$. Comparing this to~\eqref{eq:bob_perfect} highlights the fact that Bob's probability of interpreting the hints correctly depend on how close the marginal probability $P_\textrm{marginal}(u|m)$ is to the real utterance probability $P_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)$. This provides an entry point for studying cross-cultural pragmatics in \decrypto, expanding upon work done by~\citet{shaikh2023codenames} in Codenames: if Alice provides hints informed by her cultural background, but such background is unlikely under Bob's distribution, we expect the number of miscommunications between them to increase significantly. 

\subsection{Modelling Eve}

Under the RSA framework, Alice's degree of optimality is determined by $\lambda$, which can be thought to capture the bounded rationality of the speaker. However, our definition of cost in~\eqref{eq:cost} introduces a second failure mode in the ability to model Eve accurately. 

To show this, suppose Alice only has access to a proxy model $\tilde{P}(m|u)$ of Eve, rather than to her true probabilities. Alice therefore chooses utterances according to probability $\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u,m)$, which follows the same form as~\eqref{eq:p_alice}, except using the proxy utility
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{U}(u,m) = \beta \log P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) + \varepsilon \log (1-\tilde{P}(m|u)).
\end{equation}

We can use this to derive Alice's expected (real) utility for each meaning $m$:
\begin{align}
    \Esp\{U(m)\} &= \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)U(u,m) \\
    &= \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \frac{P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u)^{\lambda\beta}(1-\tilde{P}(m|u))^{\lambda\varepsilon}}{Z(m)} [\beta \log P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) + \varepsilon \log (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u))],
\end{align}
where $Z(m) \defeq \sum_{u'} P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u')^{\lambda\beta}(1-\tilde{P}(m|u'))^{\lambda\varepsilon}$.

For simplicity, we let $\varepsilon=1$ and we can eliminate the terms depending on $P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u)$. This corresponds to scenarios where $\beta=0$ because miscommunication does not matter, or where $P_\textrm{Lit}(m|u) = 1$ for all $u$ under consideration. The latter is not as strong an assumption as it seems. The space of all possible utterances, even if restraining hints to be single words, is equal to the entire English vocabulary cubed. Constraining Alice to utterances with a unique interpretation given the keywords (s.t. $|[[u]]| = 1$) is therefore reasonable, and still allows for a very large number of possible utterances. In fact, this is very similar to the way the word embedding baselines in~\cref{fig:baseline_f_of_k} only choose hints that are guaranteed to be interpreted correctly by Bob. Given these conditions, the task becomes exclusively to minimize the probability of interception:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:esp_utility}
    \Esp\{U(m)\} &= \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)log(1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)) \\ 
    &= \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \frac{(1-\tilde{P}(m|u))^\lambda}{Z(m)}\log (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)).
\end{align}

Under the same simplifications, the utterance probability under the RSA framework if Alice had access to the real $P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u)$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:p_true}
    P_\textrm{true}(u|m) \defeq \frac{(1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u))^\lambda}{Z_\textrm{true}(m)}, 
\end{equation}
where $Z_\textrm{true}(m) \defeq \sum_{u'} (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u'))^{\lambda}$.

Combining~\eqref{eq:esp_utility} and~\eqref{eq:p_true}, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \Esp\{U(m)\} &= \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)\frac{1}{\lambda}[\log(P_\textrm{true}(u|m)) + \log(Z_\textrm{true}(m))] \\ 
    &= \frac{1}{\lambda} \sum_{u \in \mathcal{U}}\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)[\log(\frac{P_\textrm{true}(u|m)}{\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)}) + \log(\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m)) + \log(Z_\textrm{true}(m))] \\
    &= -\frac{1}{\lambda}D_\textrm{KL}(\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}||P_\textrm{true}) - \frac{1}{\lambda}H(\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}) + \frac{1}{\lambda}Z_\textrm{true}(m),
\end{align}
with $D_\textrm{KL}(\cdot||\cdot)$ denoting the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence and $H(\cdot)$ the entropy.

Therefore, for a given $\lambda$, the log likelihood of getting intercepted by Eve depends linearly on the KL divergence between $\tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}$ and $P_\textrm{true}$, where
\begin{align*}
    \tilde{P}_\textrm{Alice}(u|m) &\propto (1-\tilde{P}(m|u))^\lambda \\
    P_\textrm{true}(u|m) &\propto (1-P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u))^\lambda.
\end{align*}
From this it follows that expected utility is only maximised if $\tilde{P}(m|u) = P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u) \: \forall u$.
In the limit of $\lambda \rightarrow \infty$, Alice concentrates her utterance probability on $u^* \defeq \argmax_u\tilde{U}(u,m)$. In such case, the expected utility is given by
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{\lambda\rightarrow\infty}\Esp\{U(m)\} = \log(1 - P_\textrm{Eve}(m|u^*)).
\end{equation}
This demonstrates that even if miscommunication is no issue and Alice is perfectly rational, she still incurs a risk of interception and a lower utility if her model of Eve is inaccurate. 

However, we note that an imperfect model of Eve does not fully account for the behaviour we observe in LLMs. Indeed, our ToM results in section~\cref{sec:results} show that LLMs not only fail to predict Eve's guess, they often fail to consider the cost of intercepts altogether when choosing hints.

\subsection{Further refinements}
Our formalism assumes $[[u]]$ to be shared by Alice and Bob, in line with classical RSA. This may not always be the case. Revisiting the example from~\eqref{eq:lexicon_example}, $m_1$ may not be in $[[u']]$ for a player who has never heard of nuclear fusion, or who is unaware of its role in stellar physics. Differences in lexicons arise naturally if players have different world knowledge and in the authors' experience, are often the cause of miscommunications when playing \decrypto. Such differences can also be exploited, and we provide in~\cref{sec:benchmark} the example of a model referring to events that occurred after their opponent's knowledge cut-off. 

Evaluating LLM abilities to play \decrypto and communicate under lexical differences is a promising avenue for future work. We suggest that readers interested in this direction start with the study of RSA under lexical uncertainty in~\citet{bergen2016lexical_uncertainty}.

