We present here four representative examples showing how different models answer the Perspective Taking task described in~\cref{sec:results}. All four examples are taken from the first turn of the same environment seed, and therefore feature the same keywords. \cref{app:pt_llama3.1} shows that Llama 3.1-70B outputs some unnecessary reasoning, before correctly concluding that Eve can do no better than random on the first turn. \cref{app:pt_claude3.7} and \cref{app:pt_o1} show that both o1 with high reasoning effort and Claude 3.7 Sonnet with extended thinking fail to adopt the interceptor's perspective, instead wrongly assuming it has access to the keywords. They therefore predict that the interceptor will correctly guess the code on the first turn. 

Finally, \cref{app:pt_o1_handhold} shows how o1 with high reasoning effort answers a modified Perspective Taking prompt that specifies that the interceptor does not have access to the keywords or even the set of possible keywords. Surprisingly, despite this modification, o1 still reasons that the interceptor will somehow link the hints to the keywords and guess the correct code. 

We hypothesise that the observed failure modes may be due to RL on verifiable tasks where the model is incentivised to always give a definite answer. Investigating this hypothesis and better understanding the link between training methods and the resulting ToM abilities is an important direction for future work. Regardless of the cause, the listings here and the results in~\cref{fig:tom_barplots} are strong evidence that reasoning models lack key ToM abilities, such as perspective taking. Even more worryingly, there is a significant regression in abilities when comparing newer models to Llama 3.1-70B. 

