Table \ref{tab:incr_model_complexity} shows the increasing model complexity. We start with the established LARX models and naively estimate the unconditional residual distribution (denoted as $\operatorname{LARX} + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma)$ and $\operatorname{LARX} + \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma)$). We increase complexity by moving to full distributional model for the marginals and adding an adaptive estimation of the dependence structure using the Gaussian copula (denoted as oDistReg+Copula). Lastly, we estimate the full multivariate distribution in a conditional way using the proposed multivariate online distributional regression approach (denoted as oMvDistReg($\mathcal{F}$, parameterization, method)). We describe the full model in the following and note that we additionally describe the hyper parameters in Appendix \ref{app:hyperparameters}. For all three complexity levels, we include a reference model that assumes independence to showcase the value-add of including the dependence structure. We model the mean/location for all regression models by \begin{equation}\label{eq:mean_model}
    \begin{aligned}
        g_\mu(\mu_{t, h}) &= \beta_{\mu, 0, h} 
            + \sum_{l=1}^{L=7} + \beta_{\mu, l, h} y_{t-l, h}
            + \sum_{h \in \{0, ..., 23\}/h} \beta_{\mu, 8+h, h} y_{t-1, h} \\
            &+ \beta_{\mu, 31, h} \operatorname{min}(\vec{y}_{t-1}) 
            + \beta_{\mu, 32, h} \operatorname{max}(\vec{y}_{t-1}) 
            + \beta_{\mu, 33, h} \operatorname{Q}_{10}(\vec{y}_{t-1})
            + \beta_{\mu, 34, h} \operatorname{Q}_{90}(\vec{y}_{t-1})\\
           &+ \beta_{\mu, 35, h} \operatorname{Load}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 36, h} \operatorname{RES}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 37, h} \overline{\operatorname{Load}}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 38, h} \overline{\operatorname{RES}}_{t} \\
            &+ \beta_{\mu, 39, h} \operatorname{EUA}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 40, h} \operatorname{Gas}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 41, h} \operatorname{Coal}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\mu, 42, h} \operatorname{Oil}_{t} 
            + \sum_w^{W=6} \beta_{\mu, 42+w, h} \operatorname{WD}_{t, h}
      \end{aligned}
\end{equation} We model the scale parameters for univariate distributional models, as well as the elements of the Cholesky-factor $\mat{\Omega} = \transpose{(\mat{A}^{-1})}(\mat{A}^{-1})$, and the elements of the diagonal matrix $\mat{A}$ in the LRA-based scale matrices by \begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        g_\theta(\theta_{t, h, h}) 
            &= \beta_{\theta, 0, h} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 1, h} \operatorname{mean}(\vec{y}_{t-1})  
            + \beta_{\theta, 2, h} \operatorname{SignedSquare}\left(\mat{\Sigma}_{h,h}^{[t-1:t-7]}\right)
            + \beta_{\theta, 3, h} \overline{\operatorname{Load}}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 4, h} \overline{\operatorname{RES}}_{t}  \\
            &+ \beta_{\theta, 5, h} \operatorname{Load}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 6, h} \operatorname{RES}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 7, h} \operatorname{EUA}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 8, h} \operatorname{Gas}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 9, h} \operatorname{Coal}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\theta, 10, h} \operatorname{Oil}_{t}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{SignedSquare}(a) = \operatorname{sign}(a)\sqrt{|a|}$ is the signed square root and $\mat{\Sigma}^{[t-1:t-7]}$ is the rolling empirical covariance matrix of $\mat{y}_t$ for the last 7 days. For the LRA-based parameterization, we choose $r=2$ and model the elements of $\mat{V}$ as 
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        g_v(v_{t, h, 0}) 
            &= \beta_{v, 0, h} 
            + \beta_{v, 1, h} \operatorname{mean}(\vec{y}_{t-1})  
            + \beta_{v, 2, h} \operatorname{SignedSquare}\left(\mat{\Sigma}_{h,h}^{[t-1:t-7]}\right)
            + \beta_{v, 3, h} \operatorname{min}(\vec{y}_{t-1}) \\
            &+ \beta_{v, 4, h} \operatorname{max}(\vec{y}_{t-1}) 
            + \beta_{v, 5, h} \operatorname{Q}_{10}(\vec{y}_{t-1})
            + \beta_{v, 6, h} \operatorname{Q}_{90}(\vec{y}_{t-1})
            + \beta_{v, 7, h} \overline{\operatorname{Load}}_{t} 
            + \beta_{v, 8, h} \overline{\operatorname{RES}}_{t}  \\
            &+ \beta_{v, 9, h} \operatorname{Load}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{v, 10, h} \operatorname{RES}_{t, h} 
            + \beta_{v, 11, h} \operatorname{EUA}_{t} 
            + \beta_{v, 12, h} \operatorname{Gas}_{t} 
            + \beta_{v, 13, h} \operatorname{Coal}_{t} 
            + \beta_{v, 14, h} \operatorname{Oil}_{t}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        g_v(v_{t, h, 1}) = \sum_w^{W=6} \beta_{v, 14+w, h} \operatorname{WD}_{t, h}
    \end{aligned} 
\end{equation}
that is, the first rank takes most of the fundamental variables, while the second rank contains the weekday binary variables. The degrees of freedom are modeled as\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        g_\nu(\nu_{t}) 
            &= \beta_{\nu, 0} 
            + \beta_{\nu, 1} \operatorname{mean}(\vec{y}_{t-1})  
            + \sum_w^{W=6} \beta_{\nu, 1+w, h} \operatorname{WD}_{t, h}
            + \beta_{\nu, 8} \overline{\operatorname{Load}}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\nu, 9} \overline{\operatorname{RES}}_{t}  \\
            &+ \beta_{\nu, 10} \operatorname{EUA}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\nu, 11} \operatorname{Gas}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\nu, 12} \operatorname{Coal}_{t} 
            + \beta_{\nu, 13} \operatorname{Oil}_{t}.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation} The univariate models are therefore a slight simplification compared to the models used in \cite{hirsch2024online}, however thereby the multivariate distributional regression models and the Copula-based approaches are better comparable. 
%An overview with all model hyper parameters can be found in Table \ref{TODO}. 
Lastly, let us remark on the online tracking of the Gaussian copula. The probability density function (PDF) for the Gaussian copula is given by: \begin{equation}
    \ell(\vec{u} \mid \mat{\Sigma}) = \frac{1}{| \tilde{\mat{\Sigma}}|^{1/2}} \exp\left(- \frac{1}{2} \transpose{\vec{n}} (\tilde{\mat{\Sigma}}^{-1} - \mat{I})\vec{n}\right)\prod_{d=0}^Dp(y_d \mid \vec{\theta_d})
\end{equation}
where $\vec{u}$ are the pseudo-observations on the $\mathcal{U}(0, 1)$ space, $\mat{n} = \Phi^{-1}(\vec{u})$, $\Phi$ is the CDF of the standard normal distribution and $\tilde{\mat{\Sigma}}$ is the covariance matrix $\mat{\Sigma}$ scaled to the correlation matrix, $\mat{I}$ is the identity matrix and $p(x_d \mid \vec{\theta}_d)$ is the likelihood of the observation $y_d$ under the (conditional) marginal distribution \citep[see][]{kock2023truly,arbenz2013bayesian}. We fit the Copula model by the transforming the in-sample data to the uniform space $\mat{u}$ by the probability integral transformation (PIT) and subsequently transforming to the $\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ space $\vec{n}$, on which we can fit the dependence structure. We update the scale matrix of the Gaussian copula by taking \begin{equation}
    \widehat{\mat{\Sigma}}^{[t+1]} = \frac{t-1}{t} \widehat{\mat{\Sigma}}^{[t]} + \frac{1}{t} \left(\transpose{\vec{n}^{[t+1]}(\vec{n}^{[t+1]})} \right)
\end{equation}
where $\vec{n}$ are the PIT-transformed in-sample values and the superscript $[t]$ denotes the observations available in the online learning \citep[see e.g.][]{dasgupta2007line}. Samples are drawn from the Gaussian copula in the usual manner. We use the same principle to track the residual covariance structure for the LARX models under the normality assumption. We employ a second model, where we sparsify the estimated dependence matrix of the Gaussian copula by the graphical LASSO \citep{friedman2008sparse}.

\paragraph{Scoring Rules} We employ four well-established multivariate probabilistic scoring scores: The Energy Score (ES), the Dawid-Sebastiani Score (DSS), the Variogram Score (VS) and the Log-Score (LS). Additionally, we employ the root mean square error RMSE (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE) and the continuous ranked probability score (CRPS). We test for statistically significant score differences using the well-established Diebold-Mariano test. The following paragraphs introduce the scores and are largely based on \cite{gneiting2007probabilistic, gneiting2007strictly, nowotarski2018recent, marcotte2023regions, ziel2019multivariate} as well as the references mentioned for the individual scores. Denote the true price vector as $\mat{Y} = (\vec{y}_0, ..., \vec{y}_H)$ of shape $T \times H$ and an ensemble forecast $\mat{F}$ of shape $T \times H \times M$ of $M = 2500$ samples. The RMSE is defined as 
\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{TH} \sum_{t=0}^T\sum_{h=0}^H\left( \mat{y}_{t} - \hat{\vec{\mu_t}}\right)^2}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\vec{\mu}}_t = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=0}^M \mat{F}_{t,h,m}$ is the mean prediction vector. The MAE is defined as \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{MAE} = \frac{1}{TH} \sum_{t=0}^T\sum_{h=0}^H\left| \mat{y}_{t} - \operatorname{median}\left(\mat{F_t}\right)\right|
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{median}\left(\mat{F_t}\right)$ denotes the median trajectory for each day $t$. The CRPS is estimated from the forecast ensemble by using the probability-weighted moment estimator of \cite{zamo2018estimation}:\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{CRPS}_t = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{m=0}^M |\mat{F}_{t,h,m} - y_{t.h}| + \frac{1}{M} \sum_{m=0}^M\mat{F}_{t,h,m} + \frac{1}{M(M-1)}\sum_{m=0}^M m\mat{F}_{t,h,m}
\end{equation}
The CRPS is strictly proper scoring rule for the marginal distribution. Note that many works on energy price forecasting report the average pinball loss (APS) as CRPS, which needs to be rescaled $\operatorname{CRPS} = 2 \cdot\operatorname{APS}$ to be comparable.
The energy score \citep[ES,][]{gneiting2007strictly} is defined as \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{ES}_t = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=0}^M\lVert \mat{y}_{t} - \vec{F}_{t,m}\rVert_2^2 - \frac{1}{M^2}\sum_{i=0}^M\sum_{j=i+1}^M \lVert \vec{F}_{t,i} - \vec{F}_{t,j}\rVert_2^2.
\end{equation}
The energy score is a strictly proper scoring rule, however, \cite{alexander2024evaluating, pinson2013discrimination, marcotte2023regions} argue that the ES is rather insensitive to misspecified dependence structures. We aggregate the ES by taking the average: $\operatorname{ES} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^T \operatorname{ES}_t$.
The Log-Score (LS) is defined as \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{LS}_t = - \log\left(\mathcal{L}(\vec{y}_t \mid \hat{\vec{\theta}}^\mathcal{D}_t)\right)
\end{equation} 
where $\mathcal{L}$ is the underlying likelihood or probability density function of the distribution $\mathcal{D}$ and $\hat{\vec{\theta}}^\mathcal{D}_t$ is the estimated parameter vector. Again, we aggregate the LS by simple averaging over all points in the test set $\operatorname{LS} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^T \operatorname{LS}_t$. It is a strictly proper scoring rule.
The Dawid-Sebastiani-Score \citeyearpar[DSS,][]{dawid1999coherent} is defined as \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{DSS}_t = \log\left(\det(\widehat{\mat{\Sigma}}_F)\right) + (\vec{y_t} - \vec{\hat{\mu}_t})\widehat{\mat{\Sigma}}_F^{-1}(\vec{y_t} - \vec{\hat{\mu}_t})
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\Sigma}_F$ denotes the empirical covariance of the forecast ensemble $\mat{F}$ and $\hat{\vec{\mu}}$ denotes the mean ensemble as above. We aggregate the $\operatorname{DSS} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^T \operatorname{DSS}_t$ by simple averaging. The DSS is a proper scoring rule for the first and second moment and strictly proper for the Gaussian predictive distribution, since it is a linear transformation of Gaussian log-likelihood.
The Variogram Score \citep[VS,][]{scheuerer2015variogram} is defined as \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{VS}^p_t = \sum_{i=0}^H \sum_{j=0}^H \left(\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=0}^M |\vec{F}_{t,i,m} - \vec{F}_{t,j,m}|^p - |y_{t, i} - y_{t.j}|^p\right)^2
\end{equation}
and is a proper scoring rule. We aggregate the VS by taking the average and normalize the score by dividing by $H^2$ and taking the square root, i.e. $\operatorname{VS} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^T\sqrt{\frac{1}{H^2}\operatorname{VS}_t}$ to make the scales of the score comparable. The scoring rules used are implemented in the \texttt{Python} package \texttt{scoringrules} \citep{zanetta2024scoringrules}.

\paragraph{Diebold-Mariano-Test} Conclusions on the performance of forecasting models cannot be drawn from looking at aggregate scores alone, but need to be drawn by evaluating whether the differential between the loss series of two models is statistically significantly from zero \citep{diebold2002comparing, diebold2015comparing}. For the DM-test, we evaluate the differential of two score series $\Delta\vec{s}^{A,B} = \vec{s}^A - \vec{s}^B$, where $\vec{s}^\mathcal{A} = (s_0^A, ..., s_T^A)$ are the scores for each scoring rule at $t$ for model $A$ respectively $B$. We provide two one-sided and hence complimentary tests.                         

